<h2>The uncertainty set \(\mathcal U(\Gamma,\varepsilon)\)</h2>
<div class="figwrap">
<svg viewBox="0 0 680 230" width="680" role="img" aria-label="Uncertainty set" font-family="inherit">
  <rect x="40" y="30" width="600" height="170" rx="14" fill="#fbfbff" stroke="#dcdcec"/>
  <text x="340" y="22" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="700" font-size="13">re-weightings W the adversary may pick</text>
  <ellipse cx="270" cy="118" rx="200" ry="78" fill="#d6272815" stroke="#d62728" stroke-width="2"/>
  <text x="170" y="70" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" fill="#a31515" font-weight="650">(O) odds / MSM box</text>
  <text x="170" y="150" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11.5" fill="#a31515">aᵢ ≤ Wᵢ ≤ bᵢ  (unobs. confounding, Γ)</text>
  <ellipse cx="430" cy="118" rx="170" ry="70" fill="#17becf15" stroke="#0f8fa0" stroke-width="2"/>
  <text x="520" y="78" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" fill="#0c6f7d" font-weight="650">(W) Wasserstein balls</text>
  <text x="520" y="158" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11.5" fill="#0c6f7d">covariate balance, radius εₖ</text>
  <text x="350" y="122" text-anchor="middle" font-size="12" font-weight="700" fill="#4a3abf">∩</text>
</svg>
</div>
<p class="figcap"><strong>IPW-O-X / Hajek-O-X</strong> use the box (O) alone; <strong>IPW-O-W / Hajek-O-W</strong> use the
intersection (O)∩(W). At \(\Gamma=1\) the box collapses to the point \(\hat w\) and the robust methods reduce to IPW.</p>

<h2>Shared building blocks</h2>
<div class="grid2">
<div class="card">
<h3 style="margin-top:6px;border:none">common/ — pre-algorithm</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>propensity</strong>: multinomial logistic \(\hat e(X)=P(T|X)\) — from \((X,T)\) only, <em>never</em> \(e_{\text{true}}\)/\(S\).</li>
<li><strong>hajek</strong>: inverse weights \(\hat w\), per-arm \(\sum_{i:T_i=k}\hat w_i=n\).</li>
<li><strong>geometry</strong>: \(D_{ij}=\lVert x_i-x_j\rVert\) (z-scoreable) — the Wasserstein ground cost.</li>
<li><strong>support</strong>: snap continuous \(X\) to a grid + tie groups (the <code>discretize</code> switch).</li>
<li><strong>sensitivity</strong>: matched \(\Gamma=e^{\gamma/2}\); MSM box \(a_i=1+\Gamma^{-1}(\hat w_i-1)\), \(b_i=1+\Gamma(\hat w_i-1)\).</li>
<li><strong>wasserstein_radius</strong> (tight \(\varepsilon_k\)) · <strong>outcome_model</strong> (\(\hat\mu\)) · <strong>diagnostics</strong> (overlap/ESS) · <strong>config</strong>/<strong>persistence</strong>.</li>
</ul>
</div>
<div class="card">
<h3 style="margin-top:6px;border:none">extensions/ &amp; conventions</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Shapley</strong>: closed-form \(\pi^{S}(x)=\min_k\max_j A_{jk}(x)\). <a href="extensions/Shapley/Shapley.html">details →</a></li>
<li><strong>KNN</strong>: inverse-distance \(k\)-NN of the support policy. <a href="extensions/KNN/KNN.html">details →</a></li>
<li><strong>No true propensities</strong> — every weight is estimated from data.</li>
<li><strong><code>maximize</code></strong> — \(Y\) as reward (default) vs the paper's loss convention.</li>
<li><strong>Capped / Uncapped</strong> — per-arm budget \(\tfrac1n\sum_i\pi_k(x_i)\le\text{cap}_k\), or none.</li>
<li><strong><code>obj_kind</code></strong> — each saved objective is tagged (not cross-comparable across kinds).</li>
</ul>
</div>
</div>
</div><!-- /overview -->

<!-- ============================== IPW-O-W ============================== -->
<div id="sub-r-ow" class="subpanel">
  <div class="mhead"><span class="pill" style="background:var(--c-row)">IPW-O-W</span><h2>Robust, odds-box ∩ Wasserstein — value maximiser</h2></div>
  <div class="accent-bar" style="background:var(--c-row)"></div>
<div class="mbox mbox-obj"><div class="mbox-l">Objective</div>
  <p class="lead">The project's “RW” method: maximise the <strong>worst-case</strong> inverse-propensity value over
  the sensitivity box <em>intersected</em> with per-arm Wasserstein covariate-balance balls.</p>
  <h3>In-sample problem</h3>
  <div class="eq">\[ \pi^\star=\arg\max_{\pi\in\Pi}\ \min_{W\in\mathcal U(\Gamma,\varepsilon)}\ \tfrac1n\sum_i W_iY_i\,\pi_{T_i}(x_i). \]</div>
  </div>
<div class="mbox mbox-set"><div class="mbox-l">Uncertainty set</div><p>the re-weightings \(W\in\mathbb{R}^n_{+}\) the adversary may pick:</p>
  <div class="eq">\[ \mathcal U(\Gamma,\varepsilon)=\Big\{\,W:\ \underbrace{a_i\le W_i\le b_i\ \ \forall i}_{\text{(O) odds / MSM box}}\ \ \text{and}\ \ \underbrace{\mathcal W_{D}\!\big(\hat{\mathbb{P}}_k,\,\mathbb{P}^{W}_k\big)\le\varepsilon_k\ \ \forall k}_{\text{(W) Wasserstein covariate balance}}\,\Big\} \]</div>
  <div class="eq">\[ a_i=1+\Gamma^{-1}(\hat w_i-1),\qquad b_i=1+\Gamma(\hat w_i-1),\qquad D_{ij}=\lVert x_i-x_j\rVert. \]</div>
  <p class="muted">(O) is the Rosenbaum/Tan marginal-sensitivity box (unobserved confounding at level \(\Gamma\); \(\Gamma=1\Rightarrow a_i=b_i=\hat w_i\)). (W) bounds, per arm \(k\), the Wasserstein distance between the nominal arm-\(k\) covariate law \(\hat{\mathbb{P}}_k\) and its \(W\)-reweighting \(\mathbb{P}^{W}_k\) under ground cost \(D\). Feasible policies \(\Pi\): \(\pi_k(x_i)\ge0,\ \sum_k\pi_k(x_i)=1\) (+ per-arm capacity for Capped).</p>
  </div>
<div class="mbox mbox-solve"><div class="mbox-l">How it is solved</div><h3>How it is solved — the dual LP (Gurobi)</h3>
  <p>Dualising the inner \(\min_W\) (box + per-arm Wasserstein transport, by LP strong duality) collapses the
  max–min into a single <strong>MAX linear program</strong>. Variables: the policy \(\pi\); \(\beta_k\ge0\)
  (W-radius); \(\gamma_{k,i},\theta_{k,j}\) (transport, \(j\in I_k=\{i:T_i=k\}\)); \(\mu_i,\nu_i\ge0\) (box).</p>
  <div class="eq">\[ \max_{\pi,\beta\ge0,\gamma,\theta,\mu\ge0,\nu\ge0}\ \sum_k\Big(-\beta_k\varepsilon_k+\tfrac1n\sum_i\gamma_{k,i}\Big)+\sum_i\big(\mu_i a_i-\nu_i b_i\big) \]</div>
  <p>subject to a valid policy (\(\sum_k\pi_k(x_i)=1,\ \pi_k\in[0,1]\), tied per cell; + capacity when Capped) and</p>
  <div class="eq">\[ \begin{aligned}
  &\tfrac1n\pi_k(x_i)Y_i+\tfrac1n\theta_{k,i}-\mu_i+\nu_i\ \ge\ 0 &&\forall k,\ \forall i\in I_k\quad\text{(factual rows)}\\[2pt]
  &\beta_k D_{ij}-\gamma_{k,i}-\theta_{k,j}\ \ge\ 0 &&\forall k,\ \forall i,\ \forall j\in I_k\quad\text{(transport / Wasserstein)}
  \end{aligned} \]</div>
  <p class="muted">Gurobi (<code>DualReductions=0</code>). The dual certificate \((\beta,\mu,\nu,\gamma,\theta)\) is saved with \(\pi^\star\). Reproduces <code>solve_rw_multiarm</code> to machine precision.</p>
  </div>
<div class="mbox mbox-args"><div class="mbox-l">Arguments &amp; returns</div>
  <table><tr><th>arg</th><th>default</th><th>effect</th></tr>
  <tr><td><code>Gamma</code></td><td>—</td><td>MSM box size; \(\Gamma=1\)⇒reduces to IPW</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>cap</code></td><td>(Capped only)</td><td>per-arm capacity</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>discretize</code>/<code>mesh</code>/<code>mesh_range</code></td><td>True / 6 / (−1,1)</td><td>snap \(X\) to a grid so the free-π LP is well-posed</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>zscore</code>/<code>metric</code></td><td>False / euclidean</td><td>Wasserstein ground-cost geometry (z-score the covariates first)</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>c_eps</code>/<code>epsilon</code></td><td>1.0 / tight</td><td>scale / override the Wasserstein radius</td></tr>
  </table>
  <p class="muted"><strong>Returns</strong> <code>ROWResult</code>: \(\pi^\star\) (K×n), <code>usage</code>, and the
  dual certificate \(\beta\!\ge\!0\) (W-radius), \(\mu,\nu\) (box), \(\gamma,\theta\) (transport).</p>
  </div>
<p><span class="tag">Capped + Uncapped</span><span class="tag">obj_kind: worst_case_value</span> · <a href="methods/IPW-O-W/IPW-O-W.html">full derivation →</a></p>
</div>

<!-- ============================== IPW-O-X ============================== -->
<div id="sub-r-o" class="subpanel">
  <div class="mhead"><span class="pill" style="background:var(--c-ro)">IPW-O-X</span><h2>Robust, odds-box only — value maximiser</h2></div>
  <div class="accent-bar" style="background:var(--c-ro)"></div>
<div class="mbox mbox-obj"><div class="mbox-l">Objective</div>
  <p class="lead">IPW-O-W with the Wasserstein term removed: the worst case ranges over the Marginal-Sensitivity box
  alone, plus the per-arm Hájek calibration \(\sum_{i:T_i=k}W_i=n\).</p>
  <h3>In-sample problem</h3>
  <div class="eq">\[ \pi^\star=\arg\max_{\pi\in\Pi}\ \min_{W\in\mathcal U(\Gamma)}\ \tfrac1n\sum_i W_iY_i\,\pi_{T_i}(x_i). \]</div>
  </div>
<div class="mbox mbox-set"><div class="mbox-l">Uncertainty set</div><p>the box only (no Wasserstein term):</p>
  <div class="eq">\[ \mathcal U(\Gamma)=\Big\{\,W\in\mathbb{R}^n_{+}:\ \underbrace{a_i\le W_i\le b_i\ \ \forall i}_{\text{(O) odds / MSM box}},\ \ \underbrace{\textstyle\sum_{i:T_i=k}W_i=n\ \ \forall k}_{\text{per-arm Hájek calibration}}\,\Big\} \]</div>
  <div class="eq">\[ a_i=1+\Gamma^{-1}(\hat w_i-1),\qquad b_i=1+\Gamma(\hat w_i-1). \]</div>
  <p class="muted">Same Rosenbaum/Tan box as IPW-O-W; the per-arm calibration keeps the worst-case weighting a valid re-normalisation. \(\Pi\): \(\pi_k(x_i)\ge0,\ \sum_k\pi_k(x_i)=1\) (+ capacity for Capped).</p>
  </div>
<div class="mbox mbox-solve"><div class="mbox-l">How it is solved</div><h3>How it is solved — the dual LP</h3>
  <p>The same MAX LP as IPW-O-W <em>minus</em> the transport block (no distance matrix). Variables: \(\pi\);
  \(\mu_i,\nu_i\ge0\) (box); a <strong>free-sign</strong> per-arm calibration multiplier \(\beta_k\).</p>
  <div class="eq">\[ \max_{\pi,\mu\ge0,\nu\ge0,\ \beta\ \text{free}}\ \sum_i\big(a_i\mu_i-b_i\nu_i\big)+\sum_k n\,\beta_k \]</div>
  <p>subject to a valid policy (+ capacity when Capped) and the per-unit stationarity</p>
  <div class="eq">\[ \mu_i-\nu_i\ =\ \tfrac1n\,Y_i\,\pi_{T_i}(x_i)-\beta_{T_i}\qquad\forall i. \]</div>
  <p class="muted">\(\beta_k\) is the Hájek-calibration dual (free sign — <em>not</em> a Wasserstein radius). Reproduces <code>solve_rw_no_wasserstein_multiarm</code> exactly.</p>
  </div>
<div class="mbox mbox-args"><div class="mbox-l">Arguments &amp; returns</div>
  <table><tr><th>arg</th><th>default</th><th>effect</th></tr>
  <tr><td><code>Gamma</code></td><td>—</td><td>MSM box size; \(\Gamma=1\)⇒IPW</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>cap</code></td><td>(Capped only)</td><td>per-arm capacity</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>discretize</code>/<code>mesh</code>/<code>mesh_range</code></td><td>True / 6 / (−1,1)</td><td>discretisation (no <code>zscore</code>/<code>c_eps</code> — box only)</td></tr>
  </table>
  <p class="muted"><strong>Returns</strong> <code>ROResult</code>: \(\pi^\star\), <code>usage</code>, \(\beta\) (free-sign calibration), \(\mu,\nu\) (box). No Wasserstein duals.</p>
  </div>
<p><span class="tag">Capped + Uncapped</span><span class="tag">obj_kind: worst_case_value</span> · <a href="methods/IPW-O-X/IPW-O-X.html">full derivation →</a></p>
</div>

<!-- ============================== Hajek-O-W ============================== -->
<div id="sub-regret-ow" class="subpanel">
  <div class="mhead"><span class="pill" style="background:var(--c-regow)">Hajek-O-W</span><h2>Robust worst-case regret over box ∩ Wasserstein (free-π)</h2></div>
  <div class="accent-bar" style="background:var(--c-regow)"></div>
<div class="mbox mbox-obj"><div class="mbox-l">Objective</div>
  <p class="lead">An <strong>intentional hybrid</strong>: Kallus &amp; Zhou's worst-case-<em>regret</em> objective on
  IPW-O-W's uncertainty set, with a <strong>free</strong> (non-parametric) policy so a capacity constraint can be enforced.</p>
  <h3>In-sample problem</h3>
  <div class="eq">\[ \pi^\star=\arg\min_{\pi\in\Pi}\ \max_{W\in\mathcal U(\Gamma,\varepsilon)}\ \big[\,V(\pi_0,W)-V(\pi,W)\,\big],\qquad \pi_0=\text{all-control},\ \ V(\pi,W)=\tfrac1n\textstyle\sum_i W_iY_i\pi_{T_i}(x_i). \]</div>
  </div>
<div class="mbox mbox-set"><div class="mbox-l">Uncertainty set</div><p>identical to IPW-O-W's (box ∩ Wasserstein):</p>
  <div class="eq">\[ \mathcal U(\Gamma,\varepsilon)=\Big\{\,W:\ \underbrace{a_i\le W_i\le b_i\ \ \forall i}_{\text{(O) odds / MSM box}}\ \ \text{and}\ \ \underbrace{\mathcal W_{D}\!\big(\hat{\mathbb{P}}_k,\,\mathbb{P}^{W}_k\big)\le\varepsilon_k\ \ \forall k}_{\text{(W) Wasserstein covariate balance}}\,\Big\} \]</div>
  <div class="eq">\[ a_i=1+\Gamma^{-1}(\hat w_i-1),\qquad b_i=1+\Gamma(\hat w_i-1),\qquad D_{ij}=\lVert x_i-x_j\rVert. \]</div>
  <p class="muted">The same adversary set as IPW-O-W — only the objective differs (worst-case regret vs \(\pi_0\), not worst-case value). \(\Pi\): \(\pi_k(x_i)\ge0,\ \sum_k\pi_k(x_i)=1\) (+ per-arm capacity for Capped).</p>
  <p class="muted"><strong>Already self-normalised (Hájek).</strong> The Wasserstein transport's mass-balance (demand \(\tfrac1n\), supply \(W_j/n\)) <em>forces</em> \(\sum_{i:T_i=k}W_i=n\) per arm, so this \(\tfrac1n\)-normalised value equals the per-treatment Hájek <em>ratio</em> \(\sum_{T_i=k}r_iW_i/\sum_{T_i=k}W_i\) (Kallus &amp; Zhou Eq. 4, 10) — the transport supplies the calibration, so (unlike box-only Hajek-O-X) no separate self-normalisation handling is needed.</p>
  </div>
<div class="mbox mbox-solve"><div class="mbox-l">How it is solved</div><h3>How it is solved — the MIN dual LP</h3>
  <p>Dualising the inner \(\max_W\) of the regret turns the min–max into a single <strong>MIN linear program</strong>
  (objective \(\le 0\); \(\pi_0\) is feasible ⇒ do-no-harm). Variables: \(\pi\); \(\beta_k\ge0\);
  \(\gamma_{k,i},\theta_{k,j}\); \(p_i,q_i\ge0\) (the regret-box duals, saved as <code>mu</code>/<code>nu</code>).</p>
  <div class="eq">\[ \min_{\pi,\beta\ge0,\gamma,\theta,p\ge0,q\ge0}\ \sum_k\Big(\beta_k\varepsilon_k+\tfrac1n\sum_i\gamma_{k,i}\Big)+\sum_i\big(b_i p_i-a_i q_i\big) \]</div>
  <p>subject to a valid policy (+ capacity when Capped) and</p>
  <div class="eq">\[ \begin{aligned}
  &p_i-q_i-\tfrac1n\theta_{k,i}+\tfrac1n\pi_k(x_i)Y_i\ =\ \tfrac1n\mathbf 1[k=0]Y_i &&\forall k,\ \forall i\in I_k\\[2pt]
  &\beta_k D_{ij}+\gamma_{k,i}+\theta_{k,j}\ \ge\ 0 &&\forall k,\ \forall i,\ \forall j\in I_k
  \end{aligned} \]</div>
  <p class="muted">The RHS \(\tfrac1n\mathbf 1[k=0]Y_i\) is \(\pi_0\)'s factual reward — what makes this <em>regret</em>, not value. <code>maximize</code> negates \(Y\) for the loss convention. Reproduces <code>solve_kallus_w_multiarm</code>.</p>
  </div>
<div class="mbox mbox-args"><div class="mbox-l">Arguments &amp; returns</div>
  <table><tr><th>arg</th><th>default</th><th>effect</th></tr>
  <tr><td><code>Gamma</code></td><td>—</td><td>MSM box size</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>maximize</code></td><td>True</td><td>reward (default) vs the paper's loss convention</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>cap</code></td><td>(Capped only)</td><td>per-arm capacity (the reason π is free, not parametric)</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>discretize</code>/<code>mesh</code> · <code>zscore</code>/<code>metric</code> · <code>c_eps</code>/<code>epsilon</code></td><td>as IPW-O-W</td><td>discretisation + Wasserstein geometry/radius</td></tr>
  </table>
  <p class="muted"><strong>Returns</strong> <code>HajekOWResult</code>: \(\pi^\star\), objective \(\le 0\),
  \(\mu,\nu\) (=Kallus box duals \(p,q\)), \(\beta\) (W-radius), \(\gamma,\theta\) (transport). <strong>Not pure
  Kallus</strong> — Kallus's own extension is a total-variation budget, and its policy is parametric.</p>
  </div>
<p><span class="tag">Capped + Uncapped</span><span class="tag">obj_kind: worst_case_regret</span> · <a href="methods/Hajek-O-W/Hajek-O-W.html">full derivation →</a></p>
</div>

<!-- ============================== Hajek-O-X ============================== -->
<div id="sub-regret-o" class="subpanel">
  <div class="mhead"><span class="pill" style="background:var(--c-rego)">Hajek-O-X</span><h2>Robust worst-case regret over the box only (free-π)</h2></div>
  <div class="accent-bar" style="background:var(--c-rego)"></div>
<div class="mbox mbox-obj"><div class="mbox-l">Objective</div>
  <p class="lead">Hajek-O-W without the Wasserstein term — the box-only worst-case-regret minimiser.</p>
  <h3>In-sample problem (self-normalised Hájek regret)</h3>
  <p>The per-treatment <strong>self-normalised</strong> Hájek ratio (Kallus &amp; Zhou Eq. 4, 10) — the denominator floats with \(W\):</p>
  <div class="eq">\[ \pi^\star=\arg\min_{\pi\in\Pi}\ \sum_t\ \sup_{W\in\mathcal U(\Gamma)}\ \frac{\sum_{i:T_i=t}\big(\mathbf 1[t=0]-\pi_t(x_i)\big)Y_iW_i}{\sum_{i:T_i=t}W_i},\qquad \pi_0=\text{all-control}. \]</div>
  </div>
<div class="mbox mbox-set"><div class="mbox-l">Uncertainty set</div><p>the box only, on the <strong>raw</strong> inverse weights \(\tilde W_i=1/\hat P(T_i\mid x_i)\ge1\) (so \(a_i\ge1>0\); the self-normalisation IS the calibration — no \(\sum W=n\) constraint):</p>
  <div class="eq">\[ \mathcal U(\Gamma)=\big\{\,W:\ a_i\le W_i\le b_i\ \ \forall i\,\big\},\quad a_i=1+\Gamma^{-1}(\tilde W_i-1),\ b_i=1+\Gamma(\tilde W_i-1). \]</div>
  <p class="muted">Unlike the value method IPW-O-X (which fixes the denominator with a hard \(\sum_{T_i=k}W_i=n\)), the Hájek regret optimises the <em>ratio</em>. \(\Pi\): \(\pi_k(x_i)\ge0,\ \sum_k\pi_k(x_i)=1\) (+ capacity for Capped).</p>
  </div>
<div class="mbox mbox-solve"><div class="mbox-l">How it is solved</div><h3>How it is solved — the Charnes–Cooper MIN dual LP</h3>
  <p>Each per-treatment \(\sup_W\) is linear-fractional; Charnes–Cooper duality (Eq. 12) gives a level \(\lambda_t=\hat Q_t\) and box duals \(u_i,v_i\ge0\). The outer \(\min_\pi\) fuses into one LP:</p>
  <div class="eq">\[ \min_{\pi,\,u\ge0,v\ge0,\ \lambda}\ \sum_t \lambda_t\quad\text{s.t.}\quad v_i-u_i+\lambda_{T_i}\ge\big(\mathbf 1[T_i=0]-\pi_{T_i}(x_i)\big)Y_i\ \ \forall i,\quad \textstyle\sum_{i\in I_t}(u_i a_i-v_i b_i)\ge0\ \ \forall t. \]</div>
  <p class="muted">Objective \(\le 0\) (do-no-harm). \(\lambda_t\) saved in <code>beta</code>, box duals \(u,v\) in <code>mu</code>/<code>nu</code>. At \(\Gamma=1\) the box is a point and Hajek-O-X reduces to the IPW maximiser. (\(Y\) is a reward — high = good — hence \(\mathbf 1[t{=}0]-\pi_t\).)</p>
  </div>
<div class="mbox mbox-args"><div class="mbox-l">Arguments &amp; returns</div>
  <table><tr><th>arg</th><th>default</th><th>effect</th></tr>
  <tr><td><code>Gamma</code></td><td>—</td><td>MSM box size</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>maximize</code></td><td>True</td><td>reward vs loss convention</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>cap</code> · <code>discretize</code>/<code>mesh</code>/<code>mesh_range</code></td><td>—/True/6/(−1,1)</td><td>capacity (Capped) + discretisation</td></tr>
  </table>
  <p class="muted"><strong>Returns</strong> <code>HajekOResult</code>: \(\pi^\star\), \(\beta\) (free-sign calib), \(\mu,\nu\) (box). No Wasserstein duals.</p>
  </div>
<p><span class="tag">Capped + Uncapped</span><span class="tag">obj_kind: worst_case_regret</span> · <a href="methods/Hajek-O-X/Hajek-O-X.html">full derivation →</a></p>
</div>

<!-- ============================== Kallus ============================== -->
<div id="sub-kallus" class="subpanel">
  <div class="mhead"><span class="pill" style="background:var(--c-kallus)">Kallus</span><h2>Parametric softmax regret-minimiser</h2></div>
  <div class="accent-bar" style="background:var(--c-kallus)"></div>
<div class="mbox mbox-obj"><div class="mbox-l">Objective</div>
  <p class="lead">The faithful Kallus &amp; Zhou method: a <strong>softmax</strong> policy \(\pi_\theta\) trained to
  minimise worst-case regret. Flat — no discretisation, no capacity.</p>
  <h3>In-sample problem</h3>
  <div class="eq">\[ \min_\theta\ \sum_t\ \sup_{W\in\mathcal U}\ \frac{\sum_{i:T_i=t}\big(\mathbf 1[t=0]-\pi_\theta(t\mid x_i)\big)Y_iW_i}{\sum_{i:T_i=t}W_i},\qquad \pi_\theta(\cdot|x)=\mathrm{softmax}\big(\theta^\top\varphi(x)\big). \]</div>
  <p>This is the per-treatment <strong>self-normalised Hájek</strong> regret vs the all-control baseline \(\pi_0\) (Kallus &amp; Zhou Eq. 4, 10) — the per-arm denominator \(\sum_{T_i=t}W_i\) floats with \(W\), it is <em>not</em> fixed to \(n\).</p>
  </div>
<div class="mbox mbox-set"><div class="mbox-l">Uncertainty set</div><p>the box by default (<code>wasserstein=False</code>), on the <strong>raw</strong> inverse weights \(\tilde W_i=1/\hat P(T_i\mid x_i)\ge1\) (so \(a_i\ge1>0\); no \(\sum W=n\) calibration — the self-normalisation supplies it):</p>
  <div class="eq">\[ \mathcal U(\Gamma)=\big\{\,W:\ a_i\le W_i\le b_i\ \ \forall i\,\big\},\quad a_i=1+\Gamma^{-1}(\tilde W_i-1),\ b_i=1+\Gamma(\tilde W_i-1). \]</div>
  <p class="muted">With <code>wasserstein=True</code> the set becomes the box ∩ per-arm Wasserstein balls (IPW-O-W's set; there the transport mass-balance pins \(\sum_{T_i=t}W_i=n\), so that variant is self-normalised with denominator \(n\)). The only structural difference from Hajek-O-X/Hajek-O-W is that the policy here is the <em>parametric</em> softmax \(\pi_\theta\), not a free \(\pi\).</p>
  </div>
<div class="mbox mbox-solve"><div class="mbox-l">How it is solved</div><h3>How it is solved (Algorithm 1)</h3>
  <p>For fixed \(\theta\) the inner per-arm \(\sup_W\) of the ratio is solved fast by a <strong>Dinkelbach</strong> root-find (box) — returning the per-arm <em>normalised</em> worst-case \(w^\star\) (\(\sum_{T_i=t}w^\star=1\)). The outer is <strong>subgradient</strong>
  descent with the <em>exact</em> policy gradient
  \(\nabla_\theta\pi_\theta(T_i|x_i)=\pi_{T_i}(\text{onehot}-\pi)\varphi(x_i)\) (weighted by \(w^\star\), no \(1/n\)), returning the
  <strong>Polyak iterate-average</strong> \(\bar\theta=\tfrac1{n_{\text{iter}}}\sum_t\theta_t\); the lowest-regret
  \(\bar\theta\) across restarts is kept. Deploys with <code>predict_kallus</code> (no Shapley/KNN).</p>
  </div>
<div class="mbox mbox-args"><div class="mbox-l">Arguments &amp; returns</div>
  <table><tr><th>arg</th><th>default</th><th>effect</th></tr>
  <tr><td><code>Gamma</code></td><td>—</td><td>MSM box size</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>maximize</code></td><td>True</td><td>reward vs the paper's loss convention</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>wasserstein</code>/<code>epsilon</code></td><td>False / —</td><td>odds set (default) vs box∩Wasserstein</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>basis</code>·<code>n_iters</code>·<code>n_restarts</code></td><td>affine·12·2</td><td>feature map \(\varphi\) + optimiser budget</td></tr>
  </table>
  <p class="muted"><strong>Returns</strong> <code>KallusResult</code>: \(\theta\), worst-case regret. Caveat: regret can be
  slightly \(\gt 0\) because a softmax cannot represent \(\pi_0\) exactly (the free-π Hajek-O-X hits \(\le 0\)).</p>
  </div>
<p><span class="tag">Flat (parametric)</span><span class="tag">obj_kind: worst_case_regret</span> · <a href="methods/Kallus/Kallus.html">full derivation →</a></p>
</div>

<!-- ============================== IPW ============================== -->
<div id="sub-ipw" class="subpanel">
  <div class="mhead"><span class="pill" style="background:var(--c-ipw)">IPW-X-X</span><h2>Hájek / inverse-propensity value maximiser</h2></div>
  <div class="accent-bar" style="background:var(--c-ipw)"></div>
<div class="mbox mbox-obj"><div class="mbox-l">Objective</div>
  <p class="lead">The non-robust nominal optimum at the estimated weights — no sensitivity model.</p>
  <h3>In-sample problem</h3>
  <div class="eq">\[ \pi^\star=\arg\max_{\pi\in\Pi}\ \tfrac1n\sum_i \hat w_i\,Y_i\,\pi_{T_i}(x_i). \]</div>
  </div>
<div class="mbox mbox-set"><div class="mbox-l">Uncertainty set</div><p><em>none</em>: the value is taken at the single nominal weighting \(W=\hat w\) (equivalently \(\mathcal U=\{\hat w\}\), the \(\Gamma=1\) limit of the box where \(a_i=b_i=\hat w_i\)).</p>
  </div>
<div class="mbox mbox-solve"><div class="mbox-l">How it is solved</div><h3>How it is solved</h3>
  <p>A direct LP (no \(\Gamma\), no box, no duals). It is exactly IPW-O-X / Hajek-O-X at \(\Gamma=1\).</p>
  </div>
<div class="mbox mbox-args"><div class="mbox-l">Arguments &amp; returns</div>
  <table><tr><th>arg</th><th>default</th><th>effect</th></tr>
  <tr><td><code>cap</code></td><td>(Capped only)</td><td>per-arm capacity</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>discretize</code>/<code>mesh</code>/<code>mesh_range</code></td><td>True / 6 / (−1,1)</td><td>discretisation</td></tr>
  </table>
  <p class="muted"><strong>Returns</strong> \(\pi^\star\), <code>usage</code>, the IPW objective. Consumes the estimated \(\hat w\) (never true propensities).</p>
  </div>
<p><span class="tag">Capped + Uncapped</span><span class="tag">obj_kind: ipw_value</span> · <a href="methods/IPW-X-X/IPW-X-X.html">full page →</a></p>
</div>

<!-- ============================== Direct-X-X ============================== -->
<div id="sub-policy-optimization" class="subpanel">
  <div class="mhead"><span class="pill" style="background:var(--c-po)">Direct-X-X</span><h2>Naive value maximiser (unit weights)</h2></div>
  <div class="accent-bar" style="background:var(--c-po)"></div>
<div class="mbox mbox-obj"><div class="mbox-l">Objective</div>
  <p class="lead">The most naive baseline — maximise observed outcomes with <strong>no</strong> propensity correction.
  <code>ips_weights</code> is not even an argument.</p>
  <h3>In-sample problem</h3>
  <div class="eq">\[ \pi^\star=\arg\max_{\pi\in\Pi}\ \tfrac1n\sum_i Y_i\,\pi_{T_i}(x_i). \]</div>
  </div>
<div class="mbox mbox-set"><div class="mbox-l">Uncertainty set</div><p><em>none</em>: weights are pinned to \(W\equiv 1\) (no propensity correction whatsoever).</p>
  </div>
<div class="mbox mbox-solve"><div class="mbox-l">How it is solved</div><h3>How it is solved</h3>
  <p>A direct LP with weights pinned to \(1\) — it is Direct IPW with \(\hat w\equiv 1\). Fooled by both observed
  and unobserved confounding; the floor the other methods must beat.</p>
  </div>
<div class="mbox mbox-args"><div class="mbox-l">Arguments &amp; returns</div>
  <table><tr><th>arg</th><th>default</th><th>effect</th></tr>
  <tr><td><code>cap</code></td><td>(Capped only)</td><td>per-arm capacity</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>discretize</code>/<code>mesh</code>/<code>mesh_range</code></td><td>True / 6 / (−1,1)</td><td>discretisation</td></tr>
  </table>
  <p class="muted"><strong>Returns</strong> \(\pi^\star\), <code>usage</code>, the naive objective. No weights, no Γ, no duals.</p>
  </div>
<p><span class="tag">Capped + Uncapped</span><span class="tag">obj_kind: naive_value</span> · <a href="methods/Direct-X-X/Direct-X-X.html">full page →</a></p>
</div>

<!-- ============================== DoublyRobust ============================== -->
<div id="sub-doublyrobust" class="subpanel">
  <div class="mhead"><span class="pill" style="background:var(--c-dr)">DoublyRobust-X-X</span><h2>Doubly-robust (DoublyRobust-X-X) value maximiser — no uncertainty set</h2></div>
  <div class="accent-bar" style="background:var(--c-dr)"></div>
<div class="mbox mbox-obj"><div class="mbox-l">Objective</div>
  <p class="lead">The plain <strong>DoublyRobust-X-X</strong> value maximiser: an outcome-model <em>direct</em> term plus the IPW
  correction of the residual \(r_i=Y_i-\hat\mu_{T_i}(x_i)\), evaluated at the <strong>nominal</strong> weights \(\hat w\).
  <strong>No robustness</strong> — no odds-box (the 2nd slot is X), no Wasserstein (the 3rd slot is X); the weights are
  <em>not</em> ranged over any uncertainty set.</p>
  <h3>In-sample problem</h3>
  <div class="eq">\[ \pi^\star=\arg\max_{\pi\in\Pi}\ \tfrac1n\sum_i\sum_k \pi_k(x_i)\,\hat\mu_k(x_i)\ +\ \tfrac1n\sum_i \hat w_i\,r_i\,\pi_{T_i}(x_i),\qquad r_i=Y_i-\hat\mu_{T_i}(x_i). \]</div>
  </div>
<div class="mbox mbox-set"><div class="mbox-l">Uncertainty set</div><p><em>none</em>: the IPW correction uses the single nominal weighting \(W\equiv\hat w\) (equivalently \(\mathcal U=\{\hat w\}\), the \(\Gamma=1\) limit of the box where \(a_i=b_i=\hat w_i\)). This is the standard doubly-robust estimator</p>
  <div class="eq">\[ \hat V_{\text{DoublyRobust-X-X}}(\pi)=\tfrac1n\sum_i\sum_k\pi_k(x_i)\Big[\hat\mu_k(x_i)+\tfrac{\mathbf 1[T_i=k]}{\hat e_k(x_i)}\big(Y_i-\hat\mu_k(x_i)\big)\Big]. \]</div>
  <p class="muted">Adding a worst-case bound on the IPW correction gives the box-only <strong>DoublyRobust-O-X</strong> and the box ∩ Wasserstein <strong>DoublyRobust-O-W</strong>; both reduce to this method at \(\Gamma=1\). This is the <strong>DoublyRobust-X-X</strong> baseline used throughout the experiments. \(\Pi\): \(\pi_k(x_i)\ge0,\ \sum_k\pi_k(x_i)=1\) (+ capacity for Capped).</p>
  </div>
<div class="mbox mbox-solve"><div class="mbox-l">How it is solved</div><h3>How it is solved</h3>
  <p>A <strong>direct LP</strong> — the objective is linear in \(\pi\) (no inner \(\min_W\), no box/Wasserstein duals):
  maximise \(\tfrac1n\sum_i\sum_k\pi_k(x_i)\hat\mu_k(x_i)+\tfrac1n\sum_i\hat w_i r_i\pi_{T_i}(x_i)\) subject to a valid
  policy (+ capacity when Capped). With no capacity it treats, per covariate cell, the arm with the larger DoublyRobust-X-X score.</p>
  </div>
<div class="mbox mbox-args"><div class="mbox-l">Arguments &amp; returns</div>
  <table><tr><th>arg</th><th>default</th><th>effect</th></tr>
  <tr><td><code>outcome_means</code></td><td>—</td><td>\(\hat\mu\) (n×K), estimated upstream (optionally cross-fit)</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>cap</code> · <code>discretize</code>/<code>mesh</code></td><td>—</td><td>capacity + discretisation (no \(\Gamma\), no box, no Wasserstein)</td></tr>
  </table>
  <p class="muted"><strong>Returns</strong> \(\pi^\star\), <code>usage</code>. Takes both <code>ips_weights</code> (\(\hat w\), nominal) and <code>outcome_means</code> (\(\hat\mu\)). Realised in code as the DoublyRobust solver at \(\Gamma=1\) (the box collapses to the nominal point \(\hat w\)).</p>
  </div>
<p><span class="tag">Capped + Uncapped</span><span class="tag">obj_kind: aipw_value</span> · <a href="methods/DoublyRobust-X-X/DoublyRobust-X-X.html">full derivation →</a></p>
</div>

<!-- ============================== DoublyRobust-O-W ============================== -->
<div id="sub-r-ow-doublyrobust" class="subpanel">
  <div class="mhead"><span class="pill" style="background:var(--c-row-dr)">DoublyRobust-O-W</span><h2>Robust DoublyRobust-X-X value maximiser (box ∩ Wasserstein)</h2></div>
  <div class="accent-bar" style="background:var(--c-row-dr)"></div>
<div class="mbox mbox-obj"><div class="mbox-l">Objective</div>
  <p class="lead">An outcome-model <em>direct</em> term plus a worst-case <strong>box ∩ Wasserstein</strong> (the OW set)
  bound on the IPW correction of the residual \(r_i=Y_i-\hat\mu_{T_i}(x_i)\) — IPW-O-W's covariate-balance robustness with
  doubly-robust efficiency.</p>
  <h3>In-sample problem</h3>
  <div class="eq">\[ \pi^\star=\arg\max_{\pi\in\Pi}\ \min_{W\in\mathcal U(\Gamma,\varepsilon)}\ \tfrac1n\sum_i\Big[\,\sum_k \pi_k(x_i)\,\hat\mu_k(x_i)\ +\ W_i\,r_i\,\pi_{T_i}(x_i)\,\Big],\qquad r_i=Y_i-\hat\mu_{T_i}(x_i). \]</div>
  <p class="muted" style="margin:4px 0 0">The direct outcome term is constant in \(W\), so it sits inside the \(\min_W\) without changing it: the inner objective is exactly the worst-case doubly-robust (AIPW) value over the OW set.</p>
  </div>
<div class="mbox mbox-set"><div class="mbox-l">Uncertainty set</div><p>the same box ∩ Wasserstein as IPW-O-W, applied to the residual \(r\):</p>
  <div class="eq">\[ \mathcal U(\Gamma,\varepsilon)=\Big\{\,W:\ \underbrace{a_i\le W_i\le b_i\ \ \forall i}_{\text{(O) odds / MSM box}}\ \ \text{and}\ \ \underbrace{\mathcal W_{D}\!\big(\hat{\mathbb P}_k,\,\mathbb P^{W}_k\big)\le\varepsilon_k\ \ \forall k}_{\text{(W) Wasserstein covariate balance}}\,\Big\} \]</div>
  <div class="eq">\[ a_i=1+\Gamma^{-1}(\hat w_i-1),\qquad b_i=1+\Gamma(\hat w_i-1),\qquad D_{ij}=\lVert x_i-x_j\rVert. \]</div>
  <p class="muted">Only the IPW correction of the residual is made robust; the direct outcome term \(\hat\mu\) is not. \(\Pi\): \(\pi_k(x_i)\ge0,\ \sum_k\pi_k(x_i)=1\) (+ capacity for Capped).</p>
  </div>
<div class="mbox mbox-solve"><div class="mbox-l">How it is solved</div><h3>How it is solved — the dual LP</h3>
  <p>IPW-O-W's box + Wasserstein dual with \(Y\to r_i\), plus the (dual-free) direct term added to the objective. Variables:
  \(\pi\); \(\beta_k\ge0\) (W-radius); \(\gamma_{k,i},\theta_{k,j}\) (transport); \(\mu_i,\nu_i\ge0\) (box).</p>
  <div class="eq">\[ \max_{\pi,\beta\ge0,\gamma,\theta,\mu\ge0,\nu\ge0}\ \tfrac1n\sum_i\sum_k\pi_k(x_i)\hat\mu_k(x_i)+\sum_k\Big(-\beta_k\varepsilon_k+\tfrac1n\sum_i\gamma_{k,i}\Big)+\sum_i\big(a_i\mu_i-b_i\nu_i\big) \]</div>
  <p>subject to a valid policy (+ capacity when Capped), the residual feasibility \(\tfrac1n\pi_k(x_i)r_i+\tfrac1n\theta_{k,i}-\mu_i+\nu_i\ge0\) on factual rows, and the transport-metric constraints \(\beta_k D_{ij}-\gamma_{k,i}-\theta_{k,j}\ge0\).</p>
  <p class="muted">Reduces to IPW-O-W's dual when \(\hat\mu\equiv0\) (verified bit-identical) and to ordinary DoublyRobust-X-X at \(\Gamma=1\).</p>
  <h3>Derivation of the dual</h3>
  <p>Fix \(\pi\). The direct term \(\tfrac1n\sum_i\sum_k\pi_k(x_i)\hat\mu_k(x_i)\) is constant in \(W\) and passes through
  untouched; the work is dualising the inner \(\min_W\) of the residual correction over the OW set. Write
  \(I_k=\{i:T_i=k\}\) for arm \(k\)'s factual units and \(r_i=Y_i-\hat\mu_{T_i}(x_i)\).</p>
  <ol class="deriv">
  <li><strong>Wasserstein balls as a transport LP.</strong> Each ball \(\mathcal W_D(\hat{\mathbb P}_k,\mathbb P^{W}_k)\le\varepsilon_k\)
  is realised by a coupling \(\zeta^{k}_{ij}\ge0\) (\(i\) over all units, \(j\in I_k\)) matching a uniform demand marginal,
  the \(W\)-induced supply marginal, and the transport budget \(\varepsilon_k\). The inner problem becomes a joint LP in
  \((W,\zeta)\), with the Lagrange multiplier shown beside each constraint:
  <div class="eq">\[ \min_{W,\ \zeta\ge0}\ \frac1n\sum_i r_i\,\pi_{T_i}(x_i)\,W_i\quad\text{s.t.}\quad
  \begin{cases}
   a_i\le W_i\le b_i & (\mu_i,\nu_i\ge0)\\[3pt]
   \textstyle\sum_{j\in I_k}\zeta^{k}_{ij}=\tfrac1n\ \ \forall i,k & (\gamma_{k,i}\ \text{free})\\[3pt]
   \textstyle\sum_i \zeta^{k}_{ij}=\tfrac{W_j}{n}\ \ \forall j\in I_k & (\theta_{k,j}\ \text{free})\\[3pt]
   \textstyle\sum_{i,j} D_{ij}\,\zeta^{k}_{ij}\le\varepsilon_k\ \ \forall k & (\beta_k\ge0)
  \end{cases} \]</div></li>
  <li><strong>Lagrangian.</strong> Attaching those multipliers — box \(\mu_i(a_i-W_i)+\nu_i(W_i-b_i)\), demand
  \(\gamma_{k,i}\big(\tfrac1n-\sum_{j}\zeta^{k}_{ij}\big)\), supply \(\theta_{k,j}\big(\tfrac{W_j}{n}-\sum_i\zeta^{k}_{ij}\big)\),
  budget \(\beta_k\big(\sum_{ij}D_{ij}\zeta^{k}_{ij}-\varepsilon_k\big)\) — and grouping the \(\zeta\) and \(W\) terms:
  <div class="eq">\[ \mathcal L=\sum_{k}\sum_{i,\,j\in I_k}\big(\beta_k D_{ij}-\gamma_{k,i}-\theta_{k,j}\big)\,\zeta^{k}_{ij}
  \;+\;\sum_i\Big(\tfrac1n r_i\,\pi_{T_i}(x_i)+\tfrac1n\theta_{T_i,i}-\mu_i+\nu_i\Big)W_i
  \;+\;\underbrace{\tfrac1n\sum_{k,i}\gamma_{k,i}-\sum_k\beta_k\varepsilon_k+\sum_i\big(a_i\mu_i-b_i\nu_i\big)}_{\text{constant in }W,\zeta}. \]</div></li>
  <li><strong>Minimise out \(\zeta\) and \(W\).</strong> For \(\inf_{W,\zeta\ge0}\mathcal L\) to be finite, the coefficient of
  each \(\zeta^{k}_{ij}\ge0\) must be \(\ge0\), and the coefficient of each (box-freed) \(W_i\) must vanish:
  <div class="eq">\[ \beta_k D_{ij}-\gamma_{k,i}-\theta_{k,j}\ge0\ \ (\text{transport metric}),\qquad
  \tfrac1n r_i\,\pi_{T_i}(x_i)+\tfrac1n\theta_{T_i,i}-\mu_i+\nu_i=0\ \ (\text{factual rows}). \]</div>
  The box is bounded and feasible, so strong LP duality holds: \(\min_W=\) the dual maximum (the boxed constant). The
  solver encodes the factual-row equality as the equivalent tight inequality
  \(\ \tfrac1n r_i\pi_{T_i}(x_i)+\tfrac1n\theta_{T_i,i}-\mu_i+\nu_i\ge0\ \) — it binds at the optimum, since the \(+a_i\mu_i\)
  term drives \(\mu_i\) up until the row is active.</li>
  <li><strong>Re-attach the direct term and the outer \(\max_\pi\).</strong> The boxed constant is the dual value; adding
  back the direct outcome term and the policy set \(\Pi\) (simplex, ties, capacity — none touching
  \(\beta,\gamma,\theta,\mu,\nu\)) merges the two maximisations into the single LP stated above. Because \(r_i\) is data,
  every term is linear in the joint variables \((\pi,\beta,\gamma,\theta,\mu,\nu)\).</li>
  </ol>
  </div>
<div class="mbox mbox-args"><div class="mbox-l">Arguments &amp; returns</div>
  <table><tr><th>arg</th><th>default</th><th>effect</th></tr>
  <tr><td><code>outcome_means</code></td><td>—</td><td>\(\hat\mu\) (n×K), estimated upstream (optionally cross-fit)</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>Gamma</code> · <code>cap</code> · <code>discretize</code>/<code>mesh</code></td><td>—</td><td>box size + capacity + discretisation</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>zscore</code>/<code>metric</code></td><td>False / euclidean</td><td>Wasserstein ground-cost geometry</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>c_eps</code>/<code>epsilon</code></td><td>1.0 / tight</td><td>scale / override the Wasserstein radius</td></tr>
  </table>
  <p class="muted"><strong>Returns</strong> \(\pi^\star\), <code>usage</code>, W-radius dual \(\beta\), box duals \(\mu,\nu\), transport duals \(\gamma,\theta\). Takes <code>ips_weights</code> + <code>outcome_means</code>.</p>
  </div>
<p><span class="tag">Capped + Uncapped</span><span class="tag">obj_kind: robust_aipw_value</span> · <a href="methods/DoublyRobust-O-W/DoublyRobust-O-W.html">full derivation →</a></p>
</div>

<!-- ============================== DoublyRobust-O-X ============================== -->
<div id="sub-r-o-doublyrobust" class="subpanel">
  <div class="mhead"><span class="pill" style="background:var(--c-ro-dr)">DoublyRobust-O-X</span><h2>Robust DoublyRobust-X-X value maximiser (box only)</h2></div>
  <div class="accent-bar" style="background:var(--c-ro-dr)"></div>
<div class="mbox mbox-obj"><div class="mbox-l">Objective</div>
  <p class="lead">An outcome-model <em>direct</em> term plus a worst-case <strong>box</strong> (the O set) bound on the IPW
  correction of the residual \(r_i=Y_i-\hat\mu_{T_i}(x_i)\). The IPW-O-X member of the DoublyRobust pair — identical to the
  existing <strong>DoublyRobust</strong> method.</p>
  <h3>In-sample problem</h3>
  <div class="eq">\[ \pi^\star=\arg\max_{\pi\in\Pi}\ \min_{W\in\mathcal U(\Gamma)}\ \tfrac1n\sum_i\Big[\,\sum_k \pi_k(x_i)\,\hat\mu_k(x_i)\ +\ W_i\,r_i\,\pi_{T_i}(x_i)\,\Big],\qquad r_i=Y_i-\hat\mu_{T_i}(x_i). \]</div>
  <p class="muted" style="margin:4px 0 0">The direct outcome term is constant in \(W\), so it sits inside the \(\min_W\) without changing it: the inner objective is exactly the worst-case doubly-robust (AIPW) value over the box.</p>
  </div>
<div class="mbox mbox-set"><div class="mbox-l">Uncertainty set</div><p>the same box ∩ calibration as IPW-O-X, applied to the residual \(r\):</p>
  <div class="eq">\[ \mathcal U(\Gamma)=\Big\{\,W\in\mathbb{R}^n_{+}:\ \underbrace{a_i\le W_i\le b_i\ \ \forall i}_{\text{(O) odds / MSM box}},\ \ \underbrace{\textstyle\sum_{i:T_i=k}W_i=n\ \ \forall k}_{\text{per-arm Hájek calibration}}\,\Big\},\quad a_i=1+\Gamma^{-1}(\hat w_i-1),\ b_i=1+\Gamma(\hat w_i-1). \]</div>
  <p class="muted">Only the IPW correction of the residual is made robust; the direct outcome term \(\hat\mu\) is not. Box-only — no Wasserstein term. \(\Pi\): \(\pi_k(x_i)\ge0,\ \sum_k\pi_k(x_i)=1\) (+ capacity for Capped).</p>
  </div>
<div class="mbox mbox-solve"><div class="mbox-l">How it is solved</div><h3>How it is solved — the dual LP</h3>
  <p>IPW-O-X's box dual with \(Y\to r_i\), plus the (dual-free) direct term added to the objective. Variables: \(\pi\); \(\mu_i,\nu_i\ge0\); free-sign \(\beta_k\).</p>
  <div class="eq">\[ \max_{\pi,\mu\ge0,\nu\ge0,\ \beta\ \text{free}}\ \tfrac1n\sum_i\sum_k\pi_k(x_i)\hat\mu_k(x_i)+\sum_i\big(a_i\mu_i-b_i\nu_i\big)+\sum_k n\,\beta_k \]</div>
  <p>subject to a valid policy (+ capacity when Capped) and \( \mu_i-\nu_i=\tfrac1n\,r_i\,\pi_{T_i}(x_i)-\beta_{T_i}\ \ \forall i. \)</p>
  <p class="muted">Reduces to IPW-O-X's dual when \(\hat\mu\equiv0\) (verified bit-identical); equals the existing DoublyRobust method; ordinary DoublyRobust-X-X at \(\Gamma=1\).</p>
  <h3>Derivation of the dual</h3>
  <p>Fix \(\pi\). The direct term is constant in \(W\) and passes through; only the inner
  \(\min_W \tfrac1n\sum_i r_i\,\pi_{T_i}(x_i)\,W_i\) over the O set (box \(\cap\) per-arm Hájek calibration) is dualised.
  Write \(I_k=\{i:T_i=k\}\) and \(r_i=Y_i-\hat\mu_{T_i}(x_i)\).</p>
  <ol class="deriv">
  <li><strong>Inner LP in \(W\)</strong> (Lagrange multiplier beside each constraint):
  <div class="eq">\[ \min_{W}\ \frac1n\sum_i r_i\,\pi_{T_i}(x_i)\,W_i\quad\text{s.t.}\quad
   \underbrace{a_i\le W_i\le b_i}_{(\mu_i,\nu_i\ge0)}\ \ \forall i,\qquad
   \underbrace{\textstyle\sum_{i\in I_k}W_i=n}_{(\beta_k\ \text{free})}\ \ \forall k. \]</div></li>
  <li><strong>Lagrangian.</strong> With box \(\mu_i(a_i-W_i)+\nu_i(W_i-b_i)\) and calibration \(\beta_k\big(n-\sum_{i\in I_k}W_i\big)\),
  collect the \(W_i\) terms:
  <div class="eq">\[ \mathcal L=\sum_i\Big(\tfrac1n r_i\,\pi_{T_i}(x_i)-\mu_i+\nu_i-\beta_{T_i}\Big)W_i
   \;+\;\underbrace{\sum_i\big(a_i\mu_i-b_i\nu_i\big)+n\sum_k\beta_k}_{\text{constant in }W}. \]</div></li>
  <li><strong>Stationarity in \(W\).</strong> Each (box-freed) \(W_i\) is free, so its coefficient must vanish — giving the
  dual-feasibility equality and leaving the constant as the dual value:
  <div class="eq">\[ \mu_i-\nu_i=\tfrac1n r_i\,\pi_{T_i}(x_i)-\beta_{T_i}\quad\forall i,\qquad
   \min_W=\sum_i\big(a_i\mu_i-b_i\nu_i\big)+n\sum_k\beta_k. \]</div>
   The box is bounded and feasible \(\Rightarrow\) strong duality, so this maximum equals \(\min_W\).</li>
  <li><strong>Single LP.</strong> Re-attaching the direct outcome term and the outer \(\max_\pi\) over \(\Pi\) (simplex,
  ties, capacity) merges into the one LP stated above — linear in \((\pi,\mu,\nu,\beta)\), since \(r_i\) is data.</li>
  </ol>
  </div>
<div class="mbox mbox-args"><div class="mbox-l">Arguments &amp; returns</div>
  <table><tr><th>arg</th><th>default</th><th>effect</th></tr>
  <tr><td><code>outcome_means</code></td><td>—</td><td>\(\hat\mu\) (n×K), estimated upstream (optionally cross-fit)</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>Gamma</code> · <code>cap</code> · <code>discretize</code>/<code>mesh</code></td><td>—</td><td>box size + capacity + discretisation</td></tr>
  </table>
  <p class="muted"><strong>Returns</strong> \(\pi^\star\), <code>usage</code>, box duals on the residual. Takes both <code>ips_weights</code> and <code>outcome_means</code>.</p>
  </div>
<p><span class="tag">Capped + Uncapped</span><span class="tag">obj_kind: robust_aipw_value</span> · <a href="methods/DoublyRobust-O-X/DoublyRobust-O-X.html">full derivation →</a></p>
</div>

<!-- ============================== Oracle ============================== -->
<div id="sub-oracle" class="subpanel">
  <div class="mhead"><span class="pill" style="background:var(--c-oracle)">Oracle</span><h2>Clairvoyant ceiling (benchmark, not deployable)</h2></div>
  <div class="accent-bar" style="background:var(--c-oracle)"></div>
<div class="mbox mbox-obj"><div class="mbox-l">Objective</div>
  <p class="lead">A reference upper bound that legitimately uses ground truth — maximise a known value matrix \(V\).</p>
  <h3>In-sample problem</h3>
  <div class="eq">\[ \pi^\star=\arg\max_{\pi\in\Pi}\ \tfrac1n\sum_i\sum_k \pi_k(x_i)\,V_{ik}. \]</div>
  </div>
<div class="mbox mbox-set"><div class="mbox-l">Uncertainty set</div><p><em>none</em>: it optimises the supplied ground-truth value matrix \(V\) directly (a clairvoyant benchmark, not a robust learner).</p>
  </div>
<div class="mbox mbox-solve"><div class="mbox-l">How it is solved</div><h3>How it is solved</h3>
  <p>A direct LP on the supplied value matrix. \(V=Y^{\text{pot}}\) gives <strong>Full info</strong> (per-unit
  clairvoyant); \(V=\mu\) gives <strong>Best (true means)</strong>. A benchmark only — it does <em>not</em> violate
  the no-true-propensities rule (it is never a learned/deployed policy).</p>
  </div>
<div class="mbox mbox-args"><div class="mbox-l">Arguments &amp; returns</div>
  <table><tr><th>arg</th><th>default</th><th>effect</th></tr>
  <tr><td><code>values</code></td><td>—</td><td>the (n×K) ground-truth value matrix (\(Y^{\text{pot}}\) or \(\mu\))</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>cap</code> · <code>discretize</code>/<code>mesh</code></td><td>—/True/6</td><td>capacity + discretisation</td></tr>
  </table>
  <p class="muted"><strong>Returns</strong> \(\pi^\star\), <code>usage</code>, the clairvoyant objective. No weights, no Γ.</p>
  </div>
<p><span class="tag">Capped + Uncapped</span><span class="tag">obj_kind: clairvoyant_value</span> · <a href="methods/Oracle/Oracle.html">full page →</a></p>
</div>

</div><!-- /tab-methods -->

<!-- ============================== CONFIGURATION ============================== -->
<div id="tab-configuration" class="tab-panel">
  <h2>Configuration</h2>
  <p class="intro">Every run is defined by one typed <code>ExperimentConfig</code> (<code>common/config.py</code>)
  that <code>run_experiment</code> reads. It bundles the <strong>geometry</strong> (Wasserstein ground cost), the
  <strong>support</strong> (discretisation), the <strong>methods</strong> to compare, and the experiment-level
  knobs — round-trips to JSON, and the exact config is saved with every run as <code>config.json</code>.</p>

  <div class="figwrap">
  <svg viewBox="0 0 1000 235" width="1000" role="img" aria-label="Configuration structure" font-family="inherit">
    <defs><marker id="carr" markerWidth="9" markerHeight="9" refX="7" refY="3" orient="auto"><path d="M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 Z" fill="#334155"/></marker></defs>
