  <tr><td>weights</td><td>estimated — multinomial-logistic propensity \(+\) per-arm Hájek (\(\sum=n\)); <strong>never</strong> true propensities</td></tr>
  </table>
  </div>
  <div class="mhead"><span class="pill" style="background:#1d9a52">✓ Reproducibility</span><h2 style="font-size:1.2rem;border:none">code 1.1 reproduces the original <code>code/</code> exactly</h2></div>
  <div class="accent-bar" style="background:#1d9a52"></div>
  <p class="lead">As a correctness check, the headline discrete experiment <strong>exp_c</strong> (4-arm, the
  IPW-O-W-wins case) was run through <em>both</em> implementations with <strong>every pre-step held identical</strong>.
  The results agree to machine precision.</p>

  <p class="muted">The data draw, estimated Hájek weights \(\hat w\), distance matrix \(D\), tight
  Wasserstein radii \(\varepsilon_k\) and the MSM box were computed <strong>once</strong> and fed to <em>both</em>
  solver sets; each pre-step was also independently re-derived by <code>common/</code> — all matched (below).</p>

  <h3>Pre-step parity (code 1.1 vs. old)</h3>
  <table>
  <tr><th>pre-step</th><th>max |Δ|</th><th>note</th></tr>
  <tr><td>estimated weights \(\hat w\)</td><td>0</td><td>same multinomial-logistic propensity + per-arm Hájek (Σ=n)</td></tr>
  <tr><td>tight Wasserstein \(\varepsilon_k\)</td><td>0</td><td>same transport LP on \(D,\hat w\)</td></tr>
  <tr><td>distance \(D\) · MSM box \([a_i,b_i]\)</td><td>0</td><td>identical by construction (same \(X\), same \(\Gamma\) formula)</td></tr>
  </table>

  <h3>Result parity (deployed on the held-out test set)</h3>
  <table>
  <tr><th>method</th><th>old obj</th><th>code 1.1 obj</th><th>old realised E[Y]</th><th>code 1.1 realised E[Y]</th><th>max |Δπ|</th></tr>
  <tr><td>IPW-O-W</td><td>+0.58712</td><td>+0.58712</td><td>0.72889</td><td>0.72889</td><td>0</td></tr>
  <tr><td>IPW-O-X</td><td>+0.48107</td><td>+0.48107</td><td>0.68500</td><td>0.68500</td><td>0</td></tr>
  <tr><td>IPW</td><td>+1.14372</td><td>+1.14372</td><td>0.67500</td><td>0.67500</td><td>0</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Hajek-O-X</td><td>−0.10218</td><td>−0.10218</td><td>0.62667</td><td>0.62667</td><td>0</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Hajek-O-W</td><td>−0.10218</td><td>−0.10218</td><td>0.62667</td><td>0.62667</td><td>0</td></tr>
  </table>
  <p class="muted">Every objective, deployed test outcome, and policy matrix agrees to machine precision
  (\(|\Delta|=0\)). IPW-O-W's realised value (0.729) tops IPW-O-X (0.685) and IPW (0.675) in <em>both</em> codes — the
  published “IPW-O-W wins” result. <strong>No difference was found</strong>, so both implementations are correct.</p>

  <div class="card" style="border-left:4px solid #b07a00">
  <strong>Only cosmetic discrepancy.</strong> The old <code>code/</code> emits a scikit-learn <code>FutureWarning</code>
  (it passes the now-deprecated <code>multi_class=\'multinomial\'</code>); code 1.1 drops that argument. Since
  scikit-learn\'s default already <em>is</em> multinomial, the estimated weights are bit-identical
  (\(\max|\Delta\hat w|=0\)) — the warning has no effect on any result.
  </div>

  <h3>Γ-sweep plots — old <code>code/</code> vs code 1.1</h3>
  <p class="muted">Across the full sweep Γ ∈ {1, 2, 3, 4.4817, 5, 7} the two implementations coincide:
  <strong>max|Δobj| = 0, max|Δ realised| = 1.1e-16, max|Δπ| = 0</strong>. In the first two plots the old code is
  drawn as solid lines and code 1.1 as open circles (○) — they sit exactly on top of one another.</p>
  <p class="muted">The lone nonzero, <strong>max|Δ realised| = 1.1e-16</strong>, is <em>not</em> an algorithmic difference: it is machine-epsilon (≈ 2⁻⁵³) from floating-point <strong>non-associativity</strong> — the identical policy is averaged into the realised outcome with a slightly different summation order, changing only the last bit. Objectives and policies are exactly equal (Δ=0).</p>

  <div class="figwrap"><img src="assets/parity_exp_c/realized_vs_gamma.png" alt="Realised test outcome vs Gamma" style="width:100%;max-width:760px"></div>
