<!-- ============================== EXPERIMENT ============================== -->
<div id="tab-experiment" class="tab-panel active">
  <h2>Experiments</h2>
  <div class="subtabs">
    <button class="subtab-btn active" onclick="showSub('exp_first',this)"><span class="dot" style="background:#334155"></span>First experiment · Case 4 (discrete, 2-arm)</button>
    <button class="subtab-btn" onclick="showSub('exp_second',this)"><span class="dot" style="background:#be185d"></span>Second experiment · 2-D</button>
    <button class="subtab-btn" onclick="showSub('exp_1d',this)"><span class="dot" style="background:var(--c-regow)"></span>Third experiment · 1-D · 2-T</button>
    <button class="subtab-btn" onclick="showSub('exp_hr',this)"><span class="dot" style="background:#0e7490"></span>HR training · two-Γ DGP (explainer)</button>
    <button class="subtab-btn" onclick="showSub('exp_wass',this)"><span class="dot" style="background:#1f77b4"></span>Fourth experiment · continuous-X (Wasserstein wins)</button>
    <button class="subtab-btn" onclick="showSub('exp_wass_disc',this)"><span class="dot" style="background:#2ca02c"></span>Fifth experiment · discrete-X (Wasserstein still wins)</button>
    <button class="subtab-btn" onclick="showSub('exp_first_prime',this)"><span class="dot" style="background:#0d9488"></span>First prime · piecewise-linear first experiment</button>
    <button class="subtab-btn" onclick="showSub('exp_owwin',this)"><span class="dot" style="background:#7c3aed"></span>Sixth experiment · discrete-X (clear OW &gt; OX &gt; XX)</button>
    <button class="subtab-btn" onclick="showSub('exp_owwin2',this)"><span class="dot" style="background:#b91c1c"></span>Seventh experiment · no-cap (OW&gt;OX&gt;XX + large-Γ collapse)</button>
    <button class="subtab-btn" onclick="showSub('exp_owwin3',this)"><span class="dot" style="background:#0891b2"></span>Eighth experiment · OW beats every method at small Γ</button>
  </div>
  <div id="sub-exp_1d" class="subpanel">
    <div class="mhead"><span class="pill" style="background:var(--c-regow)">2-arm · 1-D</span><h2 style="font-size:1.22rem">One discrete feature — IPW-O-W still wins</h2></div>
    <div class="accent-bar" style="background:var(--c-regow)"></div>
    <div class="card" style="background:#fbfbff;border-left:4px solid var(--c-regow)">
    <strong>Data-generating process.</strong>
    <div class="eq">\[
    \begin{aligned}
    X &\sim \mathrm{Unif}\ \text{on the 21-point grid in }[-1,1],\qquad
      S \sim \mathrm{Bernoulli}(\tfrac12)\ \ \text{(unobserved)},\\[3pt]
    Y^{0}\mid X,S &\sim \mathrm{Bernoulli}\!\big(\sigma(0.5)\big)
      \qquad\text{(control: flat, clean)},\\[3pt]
    Y^{1}\mid X,S &\sim \mathrm{Bernoulli}\!\big(\sigma(-1.2 + 4\,X + 5\,S)\big)
      \quad\text{(treatment: }X\text{-benefit }+\ S\text{-inflated)},\\[3pt]
    \operatorname{logit}\Pr(T{=}1\mid X,S) &= -\,X + \gamma\,(S-\tfrac12),
      \qquad Y = Y^{T},\\[3pt]
    \gamma_{\mathrm{true}} &= 5 \;\Longrightarrow\; \text{matched } \Gamma = e^{\gamma/2}=e^{2.5}\approx 12.18 .
    \end{aligned}
    \]</div>
    <p class="muted" style="margin:2px 0 0">The 1-D reduction of the 2-D experiment (drop \(X_2\), so \(\operatorname{proj}(X)=X\)).
    \(S\) is the <strong>unobserved</strong> confounder — it inflates both the treated outcome (\(+5S\)) and the treatment
    propensity (\(+\gamma(S-\tfrac12)\)), and the assignment is <em>mis-targeted</em> (treats where \(X\) is low, yet
    treatment truly helps where \(X\) is high).</p>
    </div>

    <h3 style="margin-top:20px">Ground-truth outcome surfaces</h3>
    <p>Before any method sees data, here is the <em>true</em> generative outcome model — the success probabilities the
    DGP actually draws from. This is the signal the robust methods must recover through the fog of the unobserved \(S\).</p>
    <div class="figwrap"><img onerror="this.style.display='none'" src="assets/exp_1d/outcome_means_vs_x.png" alt="True expected outcome per arm vs X" style="width:100%;max-width:820px"></div>
    <p class="figcap">Expected outcome of each arm as a function of \(X\), averaged over the unobserved \(S\):
    <strong>control</strong> (grey) is flat at \(\sigma(0.5)\approx0.62\); <strong>treatment</strong> (red) rises from
    \(0.23\) to \(0.97\) and overtakes control near \(X\approx0\) — so the optimal arm is control for \(X\lesssim0\) and
    treatment for \(X\gtrsim0\). The pink band is the treated outcome's full range over \(S\in\{0,1\}\): at any \(X\) the
    unobserved confounder can slide the treated success probability across the whole band, which is exactly the ambiguity
    the sensitivity parameter \(\Gamma\) is meant to absorb.</p>
    <div class="figwrap"><img onerror="this.style.display='none'" src="assets/exp_1d/outcome_heatmaps_xs.png" alt="Per-(X,S) outcome heatmaps, one per arm" style="width:100%;max-width:1000px"></div>
    <p class="figcap">Ground-truth success probability \(P(Y^k{=}1\mid X,S)\) for every \((X,S)\) pair — one heatmap per arm.
    <strong>Arm 0 (control)</strong> is uniformly \(0.62\): no \(X\) or \(S\) channel, so it is never confounded.
    <strong>Arm 1 (treatment)</strong> climbs with \(X\) (the \(+4X\) term) and is sharply lifted by the unobserved \(S\)
    (the \(+5S\) term) — the \(S{=}1\) row (\(0.65\!\to\!1.00\)) sits far above the \(S{=}0\) row (\(0.01\!\to\!0.88\)).
    Because \(S\) is hidden, two units at the same \(X\) can have very different treated outcomes; that gap is the
    confounding IPW-O-W hedges against.</p>

    <h3 style="margin-top:20px">Treatment-assignment propensity \(P(T{=}1\mid X,S)\)</h3>
    <p>The (unobserved-\(S\)-driven) assignment that generates the observed data:
    \(\operatorname{logit}P(T{=}1\mid X,S)=-X+\gamma(S-\tfrac12)\) with \(\gamma=5\). \(S\) swings the logit by \(\pm2.5\),
    and the \(-X\) term makes assignment <em>decrease</em> in \(X\) — so the behaviour policy is <strong>mis-targeted</strong>:
    it treats <em>low</em>-\(X\) units more, exactly where treatment is <em>least</em> helpful.</p>
    <div class="figwrap"><img onerror="this.style.display='none'" src="assets/exp_1d/propensity_by_S.png" alt="P(T=1|X,S) for S=0 and S=1, and the true outcome curves" style="width:100%;max-width:1100px"></div>
    <p class="figcap"><strong>Left:</strong> \(P(T{=}1\mid X,S{=}1)\) (red, \(\approx0.82\text{–}0.97\)) vs \(P(T{=}1\mid X,S{=}0)\)
    (blue, \(\approx0.03\text{–}0.18\)); the marginal (dashed) <em>decreases</em> from \(0.58\) to \(0.42\) — the
    mis-targeting. <strong>Right:</strong> the true outcomes — control \(\mu_0=\sigma(0.5)=0.62\) (flat green), and the
    marginal \(E[Y^1\mid X]\) (dark red) which only exceeds control for \(X\gtrsim0.1\); in the shaded band treatment
    <strong>hurts</strong> (\(\mu_1<\mu_0\)). So <strong>treat-all is not population-optimal</strong> (the oracle treats only the
    \(\approx48\%\) of units with \(X\gtrsim0.1\)): treating everyone would harm the low-\(X\) half. The treated units are an
    \(\approx88\%\) \(S{=}1\) elite at every \(X\) (dotted \(E[Y^1\mid X,S{=}1]\) is high throughout), which is why their
    <em>observed</em> outcomes look uniformly good — the confounding illusion that makes treat-all <em>seem</em> right while
    the \(S{=}0\) majority at low \(X\) would do badly under treatment.</p>

    <h3 style="margin-top:20px">Why IPW over-treats — \(\pi(\text{treat}\mid X)\) at \(N_{\text{train}}=1500\) (uncapped, IPW only)</h3>
    <div class="card" style="background:#fbfffb;border-left:4px solid var(--c-ipw)">
    <strong>IPW assigns treatment <em>almost everywhere</em> — and that is the confounding, not a bug.</strong>
    Because the estimated propensity \(\hat e(X)\) is built from \(X\) only (\(S\) is unobserved), the inverse weight
    \(\hat w=1/\hat e(X)\) is <em>constant within each \(X\)-cell</em> and <strong>cancels</strong> in the per-cell
    treat-vs-control comparison. So IPW effectively compares the <em>observed treated mean</em> against the
    <em>observed control mean</em> at each \(X\). But the treated units are an \(\approx\!90\%\) \(S{=}1\) elite, so the
    treated mean \(\approx\sigma(3.8+4X)\) (the \(S{=}1\)-inflated outcome) beats flat control (\(0.62\)) for all
    \(X\gtrsim-0.7\) — IPW therefore treats \(\approx\!80\%\) of units (large-\(N\) limit \(0.86\)) versus the optimal
    \(0.48\) (treat \(X\gtrsim0.1\)). It cannot recover the <em>marginal</em> \(\mu_1(X)\); it only ever sees the
    confounded treated arm.</div>
    <div class="figwrap"><img onerror="this.style.display='none'" src="assets/exp_1d/ipw_uncapped_n1500.png" alt="IPW pi(T=1|X) at N=1500, uncapped, vs optimal" style="width:100%;max-width:980px"></div>
    <p class="figcap"><strong>Uncapped IPW policy \(\pi(\text{treat}\mid X)\) at \(N_{\text{train}}=1500\)</strong> (green,
    mean of 15 seeds). It rises to \(1\) for \(X\gtrsim0\) and crosses \(0.5\) near \(X=-0.7\) — i.e. IPW treats almost
    everywhere except the low-\(X\) tail (where even the \(S{=}1\)-inflated treated outcome falls below control). The
    green-shaded band \(X\in[-0.7,\,0.1]\) is the <strong>over-treatment region</strong>: treatment truly <em>hurts</em>
    there (true \(\mu_1<\mu_0\), dark-red optimal step at \(X\gtrsim0.1\)), but the confounded data makes treatment look
    good, so IPW treats. Treat-fractions: <strong>IPW 0.79</strong> (large-\(N\) limit 0.86) vs <strong>optimal 0.48</strong>.
    This is the same unobserved-\(S\) confounding the robust methods hedge against. Data: <code>assets/exp_1d/ipw_uncapped_n1500.png</code>.</p>

        <p class="lead">A <strong>single discrete feature</strong> \(X\), one unobserved \(S\), two treatments — the
    simplest possible setting, shown in <strong>both capacity regimes</strong> (toggle below). <strong>Uncapped</strong>,
    nothing forces a ranking, so the robust covariate-balance term has no lever and <strong>IPW-O-W \(\approx\) IPW</strong> —
    robustness does not win. <strong>Under the \(\le 50\%\) cap</strong> the Wasserstein term binds, so <strong>IPW-O-W beats
    the box-only IPW-O-X</strong> and IPW, and the doubly-robust <strong>DoublyRobust-O-W is the single best method</strong>, reaching the
    best-deployable ceiling. The Wasserstein edge does <em>not</em> need multi-dimensional covariates — only that the
    capacity constraint binds.</p>

    <div class="card" style="border-left:4px solid var(--accent)">
    <strong>Configuration</strong>
    <table style="margin-top:8px">
    <tr><th>field</th><th>value</th></tr>
    <tr><td>DGP</td><td>discrete <strong>1-D</strong> 2-arm; \(X\) on a 21-point grid in \([-1,1]\); unobserved \(S\sim\)Bernoulli(0.5)</td></tr>
    <tr><td>outcome model</td><td>control \(\sigma(0.5)\); treatment \(\sigma(-1.2+4X+5S)\)</td></tr>
    <tr><td>treatment model</td><td>\(\operatorname{logit}P(T{=}1\mid X,S)=-\,X+\gamma(S-\tfrac12)\) (mis-targeted + S-channel)</td></tr>
    <tr><td>methods</td><td>IPW-O-W · IPW-O-X · IPW · <strong>DoublyRobust-X-X</strong> · Hajek-O-X · Hajek-O-W · <strong>DoublyRobust-O-W · DoublyRobust-O-X</strong> · Kallus</td></tr>
    <tr><td>\(\gamma_{\text{true}}\) · Γ sweep</td><td>5 (matched \(\Gamma=e^{2.5}=12.18\)) · \(\{1,3,6,9,12.18,16\}\)</td></tr>
    <tr><td>regimes · z-score · \(n_{\text{train}}/n_{\text{test}}\) · seeds</td><td>uncapped (1,&nbsp;1) &amp; capped (1,&nbsp;0.5) · OFF (1-D) · 500 / 2000 · 3</td></tr>
    </table>
    </div>

    <div class="innertabs">
      <span class="innertabs-label">Performance:</span>
      <button class="inner-btn active" onclick="showInner('exp1d-cap',this)">Capped&nbsp; (treat ≤ 50%)</button>
      <button class="inner-btn" onclick="showInner('exp1d-uncap',this)">Uncapped&nbsp; (treat ≤ 100%)</button>
    </div>

    <div id="exp1d-cap" class="inner-panel active">
    <h3>Performance — capped (treat \(\le 50\%\))</h3>
    <p class="note">At most 50% of units may be treated, so each method must <strong>rank</strong> who benefits most. The
    optimal fraction (\(X\ge0\)) is \(\approx0.52\), so the cap <em>just</em> binds — enough to activate the robust
    covariate-balance term. Curves are mean \(\pm\) SD over 3 seeds.</p>
    <h4 style="margin:18px 0 4px;font-size:1.04rem">Treatment policy \(\pi(\text{treat}\mid x)\)</h4>
    <div class="ichart" id="ichart-exp1d-cap-policy" style="max-width:940px"></div>
    <p class="figcap"><strong>Interactive</strong> — deployed \(\pi(\text{treat}\mid X)\) over the 21-X grid; pick a
    <strong>seed</strong> or <strong>Average</strong> (under Average a value in \((0,1)\) is the <em>fraction of seeds</em>
    that treat at that \(X\)), the <strong>Γ&nbsp;▾</strong>, and toggle methods. At the matched Γ, DoublyRobust-O-W / IPW-O-W
    concentrate the capped budget on the genuinely-helped high-\(X\) units; IPW-O-X / IPW spend budget on low-\(X\) cells the
    unobserved \(S\) inflated; Kallus hugs the never-treat baseline.</p>
    <h4 style="margin:18px 0 4px;font-size:1.04rem">Realised outcome vs Γ — train (in-sample) vs test (deployed)</h4>
    <div class="ichart" id="ichart-exp1d-cap-rz-train" style="max-width:880px"></div>
    <div class="ichart" id="ichart-exp1d-cap-rz-test" style="max-width:880px"></div>
    <p class="figcap"><strong>Top = train, bottom = test (deployed)</strong>; mean \(\pm\) SD over 3 seeds (band = ±1 SD;
    toggle methods to de-clutter). At the matched Γ the test ranking is <strong>DoublyRobust-O-W 0.718 &gt; DoublyRobust-X-X 0.715 &gt; IPW-O-W 0.705
    &gt; DoublyRobust-O-X 0.703 &gt; Hajek-O-W 0.698 &gt; IPW 0.670 &gt; IPW-O-X 0.646 &gt; Kallus 0.618 &gt; Hajek-O-X 0.617</strong>.
    DoublyRobust-O-W is both best and the most seed-stable (SD ±0.002). Green dashed = best-means ceiling 0.726; Full-info 0.729 is
    in the note (off scale).</p>
    <h4 style="margin:18px 0 4px;font-size:1.04rem">Worst-case objective vs Γ</h4>
    <div class="ichart" id="ichart-exp1d-cap-obj" style="max-width:820px"></div>
    <p class="figcap">Worst-case in-sample objective (mean \(\pm\) SD over 3 seeds): the value methods (IPW-O-W/IPW-O-X/IPW/DoublyRobust-X-X)
    report a worst-case value, the regret methods a worst-case regret \(\le 0\).</p>
    <div class="figwrap"><img onerror="this.style.display='none'" src="assets/exp_1d/realized_bar_cap.png" alt="Realised value at matched Gamma, capped" style="width:100%;max-width:900px"></div>
    <p class="figcap">Realised value at the matched Γ, capped — <strong>DoublyRobust-O-W on top</strong>; the box-only <strong>IPW-O-X dips below IPW</strong> (odds-box robustness alone hurts), and the Wasserstein methods recover it.</p>
    <div class="figwrap"><img onerror="this.style.display='none'" src="assets/exp_1d/treat_fraction_cap.png" alt="Treat fraction vs Gamma, capped" style="width:100%;max-width:900px"></div>
    <p class="figcap">Fraction treated vs Γ, capped — methods press against the 0.5 cap (red dashed); the difference is <em>which</em> half they treat.</p>
    </div>

    <div id="exp1d-uncap" class="inner-panel">
    <h3>Performance — uncapped (treat \(\le 100\%\))</h3>
    <p class="note">No capacity limit: every unit may be treated independently, so there is no scarce budget to allocate and
    nothing forces a ranking. The robust covariate-balance term does not bind, so robustness has no lever here. Curves are
    mean \(\pm\) SD over 3 seeds.</p>
    <h4 style="margin:18px 0 4px;font-size:1.04rem">Treatment policy \(\pi(\text{treat}\mid x)\)</h4>
    <div class="ichart" id="ichart-exp1d-uncap-policy" style="max-width:940px"></div>
    <p class="figcap"><strong>Interactive</strong> — uncapped \(\pi(\text{treat}\mid X)\) (seed / Average, Γ, method toggles
    as above). Without the cap IPW-O-W behaves \(\approx\) IPW — a per-\(X\) bang-bang with no pooling — because there is no
    scarce budget for the Wasserstein balance to reallocate.</p>
    <h4 style="margin:18px 0 4px;font-size:1.04rem">Realised outcome vs Γ — train (in-sample) vs test (deployed)</h4>
    <div class="ichart" id="ichart-exp1d-uncap-rz-train" style="max-width:880px"></div>
    <div class="ichart" id="ichart-exp1d-uncap-rz-test" style="max-width:880px"></div>
    <p class="figcap">mean \(\pm\) SD over 3 seeds. At the matched Γ: <strong>Hajek-O-W 0.698 &gt; IPW 0.686 ≈ DoublyRobust-X-X 0.685
    &gt; IPW-O-W 0.674 &gt; DoublyRobust-O-X 0.672 ≈ IPW-O-X 0.671 &gt; DoublyRobust-O-W 0.663 &gt; Kallus 0.618 &gt; Hajek-O-X 0.617</strong>.
    <strong>IPW-O-W (0.674) sits below IPW (0.686)</strong>, and DoublyRobust-O-W (0.663) below both — with no binding cap, robustness
    does not win (it can even cost a little). Best-means ceiling 0.726.</p>
    <h4 style="margin:18px 0 4px;font-size:1.04rem">Worst-case objective vs Γ</h4>
    <div class="ichart" id="ichart-exp1d-uncap-obj" style="max-width:820px"></div>
    <p class="figcap">Worst-case in-sample objective, uncapped (mean \(\pm\) SD over 3 seeds).</p>
    <div class="figwrap"><img onerror="this.style.display='none'" src="assets/exp_1d/realized_bar_uncap.png" alt="Realised value at matched Gamma, uncapped" style="width:100%;max-width:900px"></div>
    <p class="figcap">Realised value at the matched Γ, uncapped — the robust/DR methods do <em>not</em> lead; IPW, DoublyRobust-X-X and Hajek-O-W sit at the top.</p>
    <div class="figwrap"><img onerror="this.style.display='none'" src="assets/exp_1d/treat_fraction_uncap.png" alt="Treat fraction vs Gamma, uncapped" style="width:100%;max-width:900px"></div>
    <p class="figcap">Fraction treated vs Γ, uncapped — confounded methods drift toward treat-all.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="card" style="border-left:4px solid var(--c-row)">
    <strong>Finding.</strong> Read the capped regime as an <strong>ablation</strong> of two ingredients. Plain <strong>IPW</strong>
    (inverse-propensity only — no outcome model, no robustness) realises 0.670. The non-robust doubly-robust baseline
    <strong>DoublyRobust-X-X</strong> (\(=\) IPW \(+\) an estimated outcome model \(\hat\mu_k\), still Γ=1) jumps to <strong>0.715</strong>:
    the <em>outcome model alone</em> is the big gain. Adding <em>confounding robustness</em> on top, <strong>DoublyRobust-O-W</strong>
    reaches <strong>0.718</strong> — essentially the best-deployable ceiling (0.726). Among the IPW-only methods the two
    Wasserstein methods (IPW-O-W 0.705, Hajek-O-W 0.698) beat IPW, while box-only <strong>IPW-O-X dips below IPW</strong>
    (0.646 &lt; 0.670) — odds-box robustness <em>alone</em> hurts; the Wasserstein balance recovers it. <strong>Uncapped</strong>,
    the whole effect disappears: with no binding capacity there is no scarce budget to reallocate, so IPW-O-W (0.674) \(\approx\)
    falls just below IPW (0.686) and DoublyRobust-O-W (0.663) below both — the cap is exactly what activates the robust edge (consistent
    with the 2-D and Case-4 experiments). Code: <code>assets/exp_1d/{exp_1d_multiseed.py, exp_1d_multiseed_agg.py, perf_plots.py, build_policy_chart.py}</code>;
    raw numbers in <code>_multiseed/bern_seed*.json</code> + <code>exp_1d_results.json</code>.
    </div>

    <h3 style="margin-top:30px">Why these results happen — the mechanism</h3>
    <p class="muted" style="margin:0 0 10px">The diagnostic panels below are the original <strong>single-seed, \(n=800\)</strong>
    illustration of the causal mechanism (capped regime); the headline performance charts above are the <strong>3-seed,
    \(n=500\)</strong> multi-seed run, so the exact ablation deltas differ slightly but the qualitative story is identical.</p>
    <div class="card" style="border-left:4px solid var(--accent)">
    <strong>One causal chain.</strong> The unobserved \(S\) drives <em>both</em> the outcome (treatment success
    \(=\sigma(-1.2+4X+\mathbf{5S})\)) <em>and</em> the assignment (\(\operatorname{logit}P(T{=}1)=-X+\mathbf{5}(S-\tfrac12)\)).
    So high-\(S\) units — exactly the ones treatment helps most — are <strong>treated more</strong>, which makes the
    <em>observed</em> treated outcome look better than the true effect. A method that trusts the data (IPW) over-treats; one
    that models the outcome (DoublyRobust-X-X) or hedges the weights (IPW-O-W) escapes the trap. The four panels below show this directly in
    the \(n=800\) training sample — what the methods actually see.
    </div>

    <h4 style="margin:16px 0 4px;font-size:1.04rem">1 · What the methods see (the confounded training data)</h4>
    <div class="figwrap"><img onerror="this.style.display='none'" src="assets/exp_1d/data_diagnostics.png" alt="Training-data diagnostics: what the methods see" style="width:100%;max-width:1100px"></div>
    <p class="figcap"><strong>(A)</strong> who is treated &amp; data per cell; <strong>(B)</strong> the confounding — treated
    units are selected toward high \(S\) (gap \(E[S\,|\,T{=}1]-E[S\,|\,T{=}0]=\mathbf{+0.81}\), severe); <strong>(C)</strong> so
    the observed treated outcome (dots) is <em>inflated</em> above the true marginal \(\mu_1(X)\) (line), while the estimated
    \(\hat\mu_1\) (orange) tracks the truth; <strong>(D)</strong> the <strong>trap</strong> — the naive CATE says "treat" at
    low \(X\) where the true CATE says treatment <em>hurts</em> (7 of 21 cells). IPW falls in; \(\hat\mu\) and IPW-O-W do not.</p>

    <h4 style="margin:16px 0 4px;font-size:1.04rem">2 · How confounded is the typical unit? (why robustness pays here)</h4>
    <div class="figwrap"><img onerror="this.style.display='none'" src="assets/exp_1d/confounding_dist.png" alt="Per-unit confounding strength, Bernoulli vs uniform S" style="width:100%;max-width:1100px"></div>
    <p class="figcap">Per-unit confounding \(\Gamma_i=e^{\gamma|S-1/2|}\). With <strong>Bernoulli \(S\) every unit sits at the
    worst-case \(\Gamma=12.18\)</strong> (median = matched), so robustifying to the matched Γ is exactly calibrated —
    <strong>robustness pays</strong>. (Contrast uniform-\(S\), right panel: only 17% at the worst case, median \(\Gamma_i=4.5\)
    — there the same robustification over-hedges.)</p>

    <h4 style="margin:16px 0 4px;font-size:1.04rem">3 · What each ingredient adds (ablation)</h4>
    <div class="figwrap"><img onerror="this.style.display='none'" src="assets/exp_1d/ablation_bars.png" alt="Ablation: IPW to DoublyRobust-X-X to DoublyRobust-O-X to DoublyRobust-O-W" style="width:100%;max-width:1100px"></div>
    <p class="figcap">Decomposing the matched-Γ test outcome into ingredients. <strong>The outcome model is the big win</strong>
    (IPW→DoublyRobust-X-X \(+0.041\)). <strong>Box-O robustness over-hedges</strong> (DoublyRobust-X-X→DoublyRobust-O-X \(-0.014\)); the <strong>Wasserstein-W
    balance recovers and surpasses it</strong> (→DoublyRobust-O-W \(+0.026\)). Net robustness here: \(+0.012\) — it <em>pays</em>
    because the confounding is real. (The same decomposition flips sign in uniform-\(S\), where net robustness is \(-0.025\).)</p>

    <h4 style="margin:16px 0 4px;font-size:1.04rem">4 · The robust optimum tracks the <em>typical</em> confounding</h4>
    <div class="figwrap"><img onerror="this.style.display='none'" src="assets/exp_1d/robustness_vs_typical.png" alt="Realised vs Gamma with typical Gamma marked" style="width:100%;max-width:1100px"></div>
    <p class="figcap">Realised test outcome vs Γ, with the worst-case matched Γ (red) and the <em>typical</em> median
    \(\Gamma_i\) (blue) marked. In Bernoulli-\(S\) the two coincide (12.18), so DoublyRobust-O-W is robustified to exactly the right
    level and stays above the no-robustness DoublyRobust-X-X. The shaded "over-hedging zone" (Γ above the typical) is essentially empty
    here — which is precisely why robustness pays in this experiment.</p>

    <h3>IPW-weight uncertainty over the support — O vs OW set</h3>
    <p>How far can the unobserved confounder push the inverse-propensity weights in the worst case, and how much does
    the Wasserstein term rein that in? For each support point and outcome class we take the <em>robust lower-bound</em>
    adversary — the weights \(w^*\) that minimise the estimated value over the uncertainty set — and measure the
    distortion \(|\hat w - w^*|\) it can impose. This is the per-point weight uncertainty the robust method must hedge.</p>
    <div class="figwrap"><img onerror="this.style.display='none'" src="assets/exp_1d/weight_uncertainty.png" alt="IPW-weight uncertainty, O vs OW" style="width:100%;max-width:1040px"></div>
    <p class="figcap">\(y=|\hat w - w^*|\) at the matched Γ; green = good outcome (Y=1), grey = bad (Y=0), bubble size ∝ #units.
    <strong>O</strong> (left) = odds-box ∩ Hájek calibration; <strong>OW</strong> (right) = O ∩ Wasserstein. The effect is
    sharply <em>localised</em>: the <strong>control arm is unchanged</strong> (its outcome is flat, so there is no covariate
    imbalance to exploit), while for the <strong>treatment arm the high-X good-outcome distortion collapses</strong>
    (≈1.1 → ≈0.35) under OW — exactly where the unobserved \(S\) confounds the treated outcome \(\sigma(-1.2+4X+5S)\). The
    Wasserstein balance constraint <em>resists</em> the worst-case reweighting precisely where it would unbalance the
    covariates — the mechanism behind IPW-O-W's edge. (Note: a purely <em>static</em> per-weight range is uninformative here —
    box ∩ calibration already lets any single weight reach its box bound, so O and OW coincide; the difference appears only
    in the worst-case <em>adversarial</em> weights plotted above.)</p>
    <p class="muted">Raw data: <code>assets/exp_1d/exp_1d.csv</code> + <code>exp_1d_data.npz</code> + <code>weight_uncertainty.csv</code>.</p>
  </div>

  <div id="sub-exp_first" class="subpanel active">
    <div class="mhead"><span class="pill" style="background:#334155">First experiment</span><h2 style="font-size:1.22rem">Case 4 — discrete-X job training, non-monotone effect under hidden confounding</h2></div>
    <div class="accent-bar" style="background:#334155"></div>
    <p class="lead">A binary-treatment job-training DGP on a discrete employability score \(X\) with a hidden perseverance trait \(S\). Training <strong>helps</strong> high-\(X\) people and <strong>hurts</strong> low-\(X\) people, so the optimal rule is the threshold <strong>treat iff \(X\ge 0\)</strong> — while \(S\) both raises outcomes and drives who actually gets trained. We report both an <strong>uncapped</strong> and a <strong>capped (\(\le 50\%\) treated)</strong> deployment, over 5 seeds at \(n_{\text{train}}=500\).</p>

    <h3>1 · The data-generating process</h3>
    <div class="eqcard"><div class="eqwrap">\[
    \begin{aligned}
    X &\sim \mathrm{Unif}\{-1,-0.9,\dots,0.9,1\}\ (21\text{ pts}), & S &\sim \mathrm{Bernoulli}(\tfrac12)\ \textbf{unobserved},\\[4pt]
    P(Y^0{=}1\mid X,S) &= \min\{\sigma(X)+\tfrac{S}{2},\,1\}, &
    P(Y^1{=}1\mid X,S) &= \begin{cases}\min\{\sigma(X{+}5)+S,\,1\} & X\ge 0\\[2pt]\min\{\sigma(X{-}5)+S,\,1\} & X\lt 0\end{cases}\\[6pt]
    \pi^1(X,S) &= \sigma(X+\gamma S-2), & \gamma &= 2,\quad \Gamma_{\text{matched}}=e^{\gamma/2}=e\approx 2.72,\\[4pt]
    \mu_k(X) &= \tfrac12 P(Y^k{=}1\mid X,0)+\tfrac12 P(Y^k{=}1\mid X,1), & \text{optimal}&:\ \text{treat iff } X\ge 0.
    \end{aligned}
    \]</div></div>
    <p class="note">\(\gamma=2\) was selected by a screen so that (i) IPW-O-W beats IPW in both regimes and (ii) \(X\) plays a real role in assignment (the \(\pi^1\) curves slope, range \(\approx0.46\) across \(X\) at \(S{=}1\)); \(\gamma\ge 2.5\) makes the hidden \(S\) dominate and IPW-O-W collapses to treat-all. Treatment never observes \(S\); each method <em>estimates</em> \(\hat P(T\mid X)\) from data.</p>

    <div class="figwrap"><img onerror="this.style.display='none'" src="assets/exp_first/potential.png" alt="Potential outcomes per arm, S=0 vs S=1" style="width:100%;max-width:1080px"></div>
    <p class="figcap">Potential outcomes \(P(Y^k{=}1\mid X,S)\): training (arm 1) jumps from \(\approx0\) for \(X\lt0\) to \(\approx1\) for \(X\ge0\) (the \(\sigma(X\pm5)\) shift); the hidden \(S\) boosts both arms (\(+\tfrac{S}{2}\) on control, \(+S\) on treatment).</p>
    <div class="figwrap"><img onerror="this.style.display='none'" src="assets/exp_first/average_potential.png" alt="Average potential outcomes mu_k(X)" style="width:100%;max-width:900px"></div>
    <p class="figcap">Average potential outcomes \(\mu_k(X)\) — control \(\mu_0\) rises smoothly; treatment \(\mu_1\approx1\) for \(X\ge0\), \(\approx0.5\) for \(X\lt0\). The strip marks the optimal arm: <strong>treat iff \(X\ge0\)</strong> (\(E[Y]\): never\(=0.719\), treat-all\(=0.762\), optimal\(=0.817\)).</p>
    <div class="figwrap"><img onerror="this.style.display='none'" src="assets/exp_first/assignment_rule.png" alt="Treatment assignment rule pi(X,S)" style="width:100%;max-width:900px"></div>
    <p class="figcap">Treatment-assignment rule \(\pi^1(X,S)\) — \(S{=}1\) units are treated far more than \(S{=}0\), and \(X\) still tilts the curves (so \(X\) is informative, not swamped by \(S\)).</p>
    <div class="figwrap"><img onerror="this.style.display='none'" src="assets/exp_first/inverse_propensity.png" alt="Inverse propensity scores" style="width:100%;max-width:1080px"></div>
    <p class="figcap">Inverse-propensity weights — the rare \(S{=}0\) treated units (and \(S{=}1\) control units) carry large weights; this thin overlap is what the robust box must hedge.</p>
    <div class="figwrap"><img onerror="this.style.display='none'" src="assets/exp_first/whats_seen.png" alt="Observed per-arm outcomes vs truth" style="width:100%;max-width:1080px"></div>
    <p class="figcap">What the methods see — observed treated outcomes are <strong>inflated</strong> above \(\mu_1\) (especially at \(X\lt0\), where the treated are a high-\(S\) elite that does well anyway). A naive method reads this as "training helps everywhere" and over-treats; IPW-O-W must discount it.</p>

    <div class="innertabs">
      <span class="innertabs-label">2 · Performance:</span>
      <button class="inner-btn active" onclick="showInner('first-uncap',this)">Uncapped&nbsp; (treat ≤ 100%)</button>
      <button class="inner-btn" onclick="showInner('first-cap',this)">Capped&nbsp; (treat ≤ 50%)</button>
    </div>

    <div id="first-uncap" class="inner-panel active">
    <h3>Performance — uncapped (treat \(\le 100\%\))</h3>
    <p class="note">Deployed policy \(\pi(\text{treat}\mid X)\), realised \(E[Y]\) on a fresh test set, mean \(\pm\) SD over 5 seeds. Methods: IPW-O-W / IPW-O-X / their DR variants / IPW / DoublyRobust-X-X / Regret / Kallus. Ceilings: Full-info (oracle on \(Y^{\text{pot}}\)) and Best-means (oracle on \(\mu\)).</p>
    <div class="ichart" id="ichart-first-uncap-policy" style="max-width:940px"></div>
    <p class="figcap"><strong>Interactive</strong> — deployed \(\pi(\text{treat}\mid X)\), <strong>mean over the 5 seeds</strong> (so a value strictly between 0 and 1 is the <em>fraction of seeds</em> that treat at that \(X\), i.e. policy instability). Use <strong>Show methods ▾</strong> and the <strong>Γ ▾</strong> selector. The optimal policy steps up at \(X{=}0\). Note IPW-O-W / DoublyRobust-O-W sit cleanly at \(1\) for \(X\ge0\), whereas IPW / DoublyRobust-X-X dip below \(1\) at scattered \(X\ge0\) — the per-\(X\) finite-sample flips (no pooling across \(X\)); they also over-treat \(X\lt0\).</p>
    <div class="figwrap"><img onerror="this.style.display='none'" src="assets/exp_first/realized_vs_gamma_uncap.png" alt="Realized value vs Gamma, uncapped" style="width:100%;max-width:900px"></div>
    <p class="figcap">Realised test \(E[Y]\) vs \(\Gamma\), uncapped.</p>
    <div class="figwrap"><img onerror="this.style.display='none'" src="assets/exp_first/realized_bar_uncap.png" alt="Realized value bar at matched Gamma, uncapped" style="width:100%;max-width:900px"></div>
    <p class="figcap">Realised value at the matched \(\Gamma\), uncapped — IPW-O-W edges out IPW; the DR value methods are strongest.</p>
    <div class="figwrap"><img onerror="this.style.display='none'" src="assets/exp_first/treat_fraction_uncap.png" alt="Treat fraction vs Gamma, uncapped" style="width:100%;max-width:900px"></div>
    <p class="figcap">Fraction treated vs \(\Gamma\), uncapped — the optimal fraction is \(\approx0.52\) (\(X\ge0\)); confounded methods drift toward treat-all.</p>
    <div class="figwrap"><img onerror="this.style.display='none'" src="assets/exp_first/objective_vs_gamma_uncap.png" alt="Worst-case objective vs Gamma, uncapped" style="width:100%;max-width:900px"></div>
    <p class="figcap">Worst-case in-sample objective vs \(\Gamma\), uncapped.</p>
    </div>

    <div id="first-cap" class="inner-panel">
    <h3>Performance — capped (treat \(\le 50\%\))</h3>
    <p class="note">The same DGP under a binding capacity: at most 50% of units may be trained, so each method must <strong>rank</strong> who benefits most. This is where the robust covariate-balance term pays off — IPW-O-W spends its budget on the genuinely-helped \(X\ge0\) units instead of the confounded \(X\lt0\) ones.</p>
    <div class="ichart" id="ichart-first-cap-policy" style="max-width:940px"></div>
    <p class="figcap"><strong>Interactive</strong> — deployed \(\pi(\text{treat}\mid X)\) under the \(\le 50\%\) cap, <strong>mean over the 5 seeds</strong> (a value in \((0,1)\) is the fraction of seeds that treat at that \(X\)). IPW-O-W tilts its capped budget toward the genuinely-helped \(X\ge0\) region; the confounded \(X\lt0\) edge — where treated outcomes look saturated-good — draws the non-robust DR / DoublyRobust-X-X variants into over-spending there. The aggregate <em>value</em> ranking (IPW-O-W best) is the robust signal.</p>
    <div class="figwrap"><img onerror="this.style.display='none'" src="assets/exp_first/realized_vs_gamma_cap.png" alt="Realized value vs Gamma, capped" style="width:100%;max-width:900px"></div>
    <p class="figcap">Realised test \(E[Y]\) vs \(\Gamma\), capped — <strong>IPW-O-W is the best method</strong>, clearly above IPW and DoublyRobust-X-X.</p>
    <div class="figwrap"><img onerror="this.style.display='none'" src="assets/exp_first/realized_bar_cap.png" alt="Realized value bar at matched Gamma, capped" style="width:100%;max-width:900px"></div>
    <p class="figcap">Realised value at the matched \(\Gamma\), capped — <strong>IPW-O-W is the top method (0.776)</strong>; Hajek-O-W (0.766) and the DR variants (0.75–0.76) follow; IPW (0.738) and DoublyRobust-X-X (0.737) trail at the bottom. Best-means ceiling 0.813.</p>
    <div class="figwrap"><img onerror="this.style.display='none'" src="assets/exp_first/treat_fraction_cap.png" alt="Treat fraction vs Gamma, capped" style="width:100%;max-width:900px"></div>
    <p class="figcap">Fraction treated vs \(\Gamma\), capped — every method saturates the 50% cap; the difference is <em>which</em> 50% they treat.</p>
    <div class="figwrap"><img onerror="this.style.display='none'" src="assets/exp_first/objective_vs_gamma_cap.png" alt="Worst-case objective vs Gamma, capped" style="width:100%;max-width:900px"></div>
    <p class="figcap">Worst-case in-sample objective vs \(\Gamma\), capped.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="card" style="border-left:4px solid #334155">
    <strong>Finding.</strong> A non-monotone training effect (helps \(X\ge0\), hurts \(X\lt0\)) with positive hidden confounding (the perseverance trait \(S\) raises outcomes <em>and</em> selects who is trained). <strong>Uncapped</strong>, IPW-O-W (0.760) beats IPW (0.753) only by a small margin and the DR value methods are strongest (\(\approx0.775\)): at \(X\lt0\) every observed treated unit is a high-\(S\) elite with \(Y\!\approx\!1\), so the worst-case box has no low outcomes to up-weight and robustness cannot correct the edge at the matched \(\Gamma\) (it only flips as \(\Gamma\) grows — hence IPW-O-W's value <em>rises</em> with \(\Gamma\)). <strong>Under the \(\le 50\%\) capacity cap, IPW-O-W is the best method</strong> (realised \(0.776\) vs IPW \(0.738\), DoublyRobust-X-X \(0.737\); best-means ceiling \(0.813\)): the budget forces a ranking, and IPW-O-W's worst-case hedging keeps that ranking better calibrated, tilting the scarce budget toward the genuinely-helped \(X\ge0\) units. Code: <code>assets/exp_first/{dgp.py, ground_truth_plots.py, run_multiseed.py, perf_plots.py}</code>; raw numbers in <code>_multiseed/seed*.json</code> + <code>exp_first_results.json</code>.
    </div>

    <h3 style="margin-top:26px">Large-sample policies at \(N_{\text{train}}=3000\) (uncapped) — IPW-X-X vs Direct-X-X vs IPW-O-X vs IPW-O-W</h3>
    <div class="card" style="border-left:4px solid var(--accent)">
    <strong>Four value methods at large \(N\) (3 seeds).</strong> Same Case-4 DGP, uncapped. <strong>IPW-X-X</strong>
    (Hájek inverse-propensity, Γ-free), <strong>Direct-X-X</strong> (Direct-X-X — unit weights \(\hat w\equiv1\), no
    propensity correction, no robustness, Γ-free), <strong>IPW-O-X</strong> (worst-case IPW value over the odds box) and
    <strong>IPW-O-W</strong> (the box <em>intersected with</em> the Wasserstein covariate-balance ball) — both robust
    methods swept over the full \(\Gamma\) grid. The true optimum is <strong>treat iff \(X\ge0\)</strong> (52% of units) —
    none recovers it, and each fails in a different, instructive direction. <em>IPW-O-W is run at \(n=600\)</em> because its
    Wasserstein transport LP is \(O(n^2)\) and runs out of memory at \(n=3000\).</div>
    <div class="ichart" id="ichart-first-bigN" style="max-width:940px"></div>
    <p class="figcap"><strong>Interactive</strong> — deployed \(\pi(\text{treat}\mid X)\) over the 21-\(X\) grid at
    \(N_{\text{train}}=3000\) (IPW-O-W at \(n=600\)); use the <strong>seed</strong> dropdown (Seed 0–2 / Average), the
    <strong>Γ ▾</strong> dropdown and toggle methods. <em>IPW-X-X and Direct-X-X are flat across Γ</em> (no sensitivity
    model); <em>IPW-O-X saturates</em> — it equals IPW-X-X at Γ=1 then jumps to treat-all by Γ≈1.5 and stays pinned there;
    <em>IPW-O-W is the one that keeps moving</em> — it rises toward treat-all near the matched Γ, then its Wasserstein
    balance term binds at large Γ and pulls it back down. Data:
    <code>assets/exp_first/policy_first_bigN_n3000.json</code>.</p>
    <table style="margin-top:8px">
    <tr><th>method</th><th>treat-frac (overall)</th><th>X &lt; 0 (treatment hurts)</th><th>X ≥ 0 (should treat)</th><th>behaviour</th></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Direct-X-X</strong> (unit weights)</td><td>0.22</td><td>0.00</td><td>0.42</td><td><strong>under</strong>-treats</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>IPW-X-X</strong> (Hájek IPW)</td><td>0.92</td><td>0.97</td><td>0.88</td><td><strong>over</strong>-treats</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>IPW-O-X</strong> (robust box, \(\Gamma=e\))</td><td>1.00</td><td>1.00</td><td>1.00</td><td>treats <strong>all</strong>, saturates</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>IPW-O-W</strong> (box∩Wasserstein, \(\Gamma=e\), \(n{=}600\))</td><td>1.00</td><td>1.00</td><td>1.00</td><td>treats all @\(\Gamma{=}e\); <strong>pulls back</strong> at large Γ (0.76 @ Γ=7.1)</td></tr>
    <tr><td>optimal (\(\mu_1\!>\!\mu_0\))</td><td>0.52</td><td>0.00</td><td>1.00</td><td>treat iff \(X\ge0\)</td></tr>
    </table>
    <p class="muted" style="margin:8px 0 0"><strong>Direct-X-X</strong> uses unit weights and compares raw outcome
    <em>sums</em> per cell: the treated are a minority (the propensity treats few), so even though each treated unit looks
    good, the larger control group's sum usually wins → it treats almost no one (0 for \(X\lt0\)). <strong>IPW-X-X</strong>
    up-weights the rare treated by \(1/\hat e\), flipping to heavy over-treatment (including the harmful \(X\lt0\)).
    <strong>IPW-O-X</strong> maximises the worst-case value over the odds box. At Γ=1 it <em>equals</em> IPW-X-X; but for
    any Γ&gt;1 it goes to <strong>treat-all</strong> (by Γ≈1.5) — because at \(X\lt0\) every observed treated unit is an
    \(S{=}1\) elite with \(Y\approx1\), so the box has <em>no low treated outcomes</em> to up-weight against, and the
    worst-case value of treating only <em>rises</em> with Γ. So here robustness makes it <em>more</em> aggressive, not less.
    <strong>IPW-O-W</strong> adds the Wasserstein covariate-balance ball on top of the box, and it is the only method whose
    Γ dropdown keeps moving: averaged over seeds its treat-fraction runs <em>0.79 → 0.91 → 0.94 → 1.00 (Γ=e) → 1.00 →
    0.91 → 0.76</em> across Γ = 1, 1.5, 2, 2.72, 3, 4.35, 7.07. It rises to treat-all near the matched Γ like IPW-O-X, but
    at <em>large</em> Γ the Wasserstein term binds (it penalises transporting mass onto the confounded \(X\lt0\) treated
    cells) and pulls the policy <em>back</em> from treat-all — the one robustness mechanism here that resists the
    over-treatment, though still without recovering treat-iff-\(X\ge0\) absent a capacity ranking. Net:
    <em>without a binding capacity to force a ranking, none of the four recovers treat-iff-\(X\ge0\)</em> — the case for
    the capacity-constrained robust methods (see the Capped tab).</p>

    <h3 style="margin-top:30px">All four methods at the <em>same</em> \(N_{\text{train}}=2000\) (uncapped, seed 3)</h3>
    <div class="card" style="border-left:4px solid var(--accent)">
    <strong>IPW-O-W at the same \(n\) as the rest.</strong> In the panel above IPW-O-W had to run at \(n=600\) (its
    Wasserstein transport LP is \(O(n^2)\)). Here all four methods are solved on the <em>same</em> \(n=2000\) sample
    (one seed; each IPW-O-W solve is ~27 min). The \(\Gamma\) dropdown carries <strong>three</strong> points,
    \(\Gamma\in\{1,\,e\approx2.72,\,8\}\). At \(\Gamma=1\) the odds box pins \(W=\hat w\), so the Wasserstein ball cannot
    move it either — <strong>IPW-X-X, IPW-O-X and IPW-O-W all coincide</strong> (we copy that curve rather than re-solve).
    This is the cleanest view of the finding: as \(\Gamma\) grows IPW-O-X saturates at treat-all, while <strong>IPW-O-W's
    Wasserstein term pulls it back — and pulls it back in the <em>right place</em></strong>.</div>
    <div class="ichart" id="ichart-first-n2000" style="max-width:940px"></div>
    <p class="figcap"><strong>Interactive</strong> — deployed \(\pi(\text{treat}\mid X)\) over the 21-\(X\) grid,
    \(N_{\text{train}}=2000\), seed 3; use the <strong>Γ ▾</strong> dropdown (\(1,\,e,\,8\)) and toggle methods.
    At \(\Gamma=1\) the three non-Direct-X-X curves overlap exactly. Data:
    <code>assets/exp_first/policy_first_n2000_seed3.json</code>.</p>
    <table style="margin-top:8px">
    <tr><th>method</th><th>Γ=1</th><th>Γ=e (2.72)</th><th>Γ=8</th><th>behaviour</th></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Direct-X-X</strong> (unit weights)</td><td>0.19</td><td>0.19</td><td>0.19</td><td>Γ-free, <strong>under</strong>-treats</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>IPW-X-X</strong> (Hájek IPW)</td><td>0.95</td><td>0.95</td><td>0.95</td><td>Γ-free, <strong>over</strong>-treats</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>IPW-O-X</strong> (box)</td><td>0.95</td><td>1.00</td><td>1.00</td><td>saturates at <strong>treat-all</strong></td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>IPW-O-W</strong> (box∩Wasserstein)</td><td>0.95</td><td>1.00</td><td><strong>0.57</strong></td><td><strong>pulls back</strong> at large Γ</td></tr>
    <tr><td>optimal (\(\mu_1\!>\!\mu_0\))</td><td colspan="3" style="text-align:center">0.52</td><td>treat iff \(X\ge0\)</td></tr>
    </table>
    <p class="muted" style="margin:8px 0 0">The \(\Gamma=8\) column is the punch-line. IPW-O-X treats everyone
    (\(X\lt0\): 1.00, \(X\ge0\): 1.00). <strong>IPW-O-W instead collapses to \(X\lt0\): 0.12, \(X\ge0\): 0.98</strong> —
    it stops treating the harmful, confounded \(X\lt0\) region while keeping the genuinely-helped \(X\ge0\) units, which
    is exactly the optimal shape (treat iff \(X\ge0\): \(0.00 / 1.00\)). So at large \(\Gamma\) the Wasserstein
    covariate-balance term does not just make IPW-O-W <em>less</em> aggressive than IPW-O-X — it makes it less aggressive
    <em>where it should be</em>, recovering the right policy even <strong>uncapped</strong>. (The pullback is sharper than
    the \(n=600\) panel — 0.57 at Γ=8 vs 0.76 at Γ=7.07 — because the balance constraint is estimated more tightly at
    larger \(n\).)</p>
  </div>


  <!-- ============================== Second experiment · 2-D (IPW-O-W wins) ============================== -->
  <div id="sub-exp_second" class="subpanel">
    <div class="mhead"><span class="pill" style="background:#be185d">Second experiment</span><h2 style="font-size:1.22rem">2-D discrete-X, two treatments — where the Wasserstein term wins</h2></div>
    <div class="accent-bar" style="background:#be185d"></div>
    <p class="lead">The same unobserved-\(S\) setting as the first experiment, but with <strong>2-D covariates</strong> \(X=(X_1,X_2)\). The control arm is flat and clean; the treatment helps along the direction \(\text{proj}=X_1+0.6X_2\) and is inflated by the hidden trait \(S\), while assignment is <em>mis-targeted</em> (it treats where proj is low). Now the Wasserstein covariate-balance term has 2-D imbalance to correct, so <strong>IPW-O-W beats the box-only IPW-O-X</strong>. Reported over 5 seeds at \(n_{\text{train}}=500\), in both an <strong>uncapped</strong> and a <strong>capped (\(\le 50\%\))</strong> regime.</p>

    <h3>1 · The data-generating process</h3>
    <div class="eqcard"><div class="eqwrap">\[
    \begin{aligned}
    X=(X_1,X_2) &\sim \mathrm{Unif}\ \text{on the }11\times11\text{ grid in }[-1,1]^2, & S &\sim \mathrm{Bernoulli}(\tfrac12)\ \textbf{unobserved},\\[3pt]
    \text{proj}(X) &= X_1 + 0.6\,X_2, &&\\[3pt]
    P(Y^0{=}1\mid X,S) &= \sigma(0.5)\ \ (\text{flat, clean}), &
    P(Y^1{=}1\mid X,S) &= \sigma(-1.2 + 4\,\text{proj} + 5\,S),\\[4pt]
    \operatorname{logit} P(T{=}1\mid X,S) &= -\,\text{proj} + \gamma\,(S-\tfrac12), & \gamma&=5,\quad \Gamma_{\text{matched}}=e^{\gamma/2}=e^{2.5}\approx 12.18,\\[4pt]
    \mu_k(X) &= \tfrac12 P(Y^k{=}1\mid X,0)+\tfrac12 P(Y^k{=}1\mid X,1), & \text{optimal}&:\ \text{treat where }\mu_1>\mu_0\ (\text{proj}\gtrsim 0.04,\ \approx47\%).
    \end{aligned}
    \]</div></div>
    <p class="note">The hidden \(S\) inflates BOTH the treated outcome (\(+5S\)) and the propensity (\(+\gamma(S-\tfrac12)\)); the assignment is mis-targeted (treats where proj is low). \(E[Y]\): never-treat\(=0.622\), treat-all\(=0.615\), optimal\(=0.732\). Treatment never observes \(S\); each method <em>estimates</em> \(\hat P(T\mid X)\). The Wasserstein ground cost is z-scored (2-D).</p>

    <div class="figwrap"><img onerror="this.style.display='none'" src="assets/exp_second/potential.png" alt="Potential outcome surfaces" style="width:100%;max-width:1180px"></div>
    <p class="figcap">Potential outcomes — control is a flat \(\sigma(0.5)\); the treatment success probability rises along \(\text{proj}=X_1+0.6X_2\), and the hidden \(S\) lifts it further (\(+5S\)).</p>
    <div class="figwrap"><img onerror="this.style.display='none'" src="assets/exp_second/average_potential.png" alt="Average potential outcomes + optimal region" style="width:100%;max-width:1280px"></div>
    <p class="figcap">Average potential outcomes — \(\mu_0\) flat, \(\mu_1\) increasing in proj; CATE flips sign across the proj diagonal, so the optimal rule is a clean contiguous region: <strong>treat where proj\(\gtrsim0.04\)</strong> (\(\approx47\%\) of cells).</p>
    <div class="figwrap"><img onerror="this.style.display='none'" src="assets/exp_second/assignment_rule.png" alt="Treatment assignment propensity heatmaps" style="width:100%;max-width:980px"></div>
    <p class="figcap">Treatment-assignment rule \(P(T{=}1\mid X,S)\) — <strong>mis-targeted</strong>: it treats more where proj is <em>low</em> (the opposite of where treatment helps), and \(S{=}1\) shifts everyone toward treatment — the hidden confounding.</p>
    <div class="figwrap"><img onerror="this.style.display='none'" src="assets/exp_second/inverse_propensity.png" alt="Inverse propensity heatmaps" style="width:100%;max-width:980px"></div>
    <p class="figcap">Inverse-propensity scores — because assignment is mis-targeted, treated units are rare at high-proj (where treatment helps), so those cells carry large IPW weights.</p>
    <div class="figwrap"><img onerror="this.style.display='none'" src="assets/exp_second/whats_seen.png" alt="Observed treated vs truth heatmaps" style="width:100%;max-width:1180px"></div>
    <p class="figcap">What the methods see — observed treated outcomes are <strong>inflated</strong> above \(\mu_1\) (the treated are an \(S{=}1\)-selected elite). IPW-O-W must discount this; the Wasserstein term flags the imbalanced treated covariate distribution.</p>

    <div class="innertabs">
      <span class="innertabs-label">2 · Performance:</span>
      <button class="inner-btn active" onclick="showInner('second-uncap',this)">Uncapped&nbsp; (treat ≤ 100%)</button>
      <button class="inner-btn" onclick="showInner('second-cap',this)">Capped&nbsp; (treat ≤ 50%)</button>
    </div>

    <div id="second-uncap" class="inner-panel active">
    <h3>Performance — uncapped (treat \(\le 100\%\))</h3>
    <p class="note">Realised \(E[Y]\) on a fresh test set, mean \(\pm\) SD over 5 seeds. Methods: IPW-O-W / IPW-O-X / DR variants / IPW / DoublyRobust-X-X / Regret / Kallus. Ceilings: Full-info and Best-means.</p>
    <div class="ichart" id="ichart-second-uncap-hm" style="max-width:620px"></div>
    <p class="figcap"><strong>Interactive 2-D heatmap</strong> — \(\pi(\text{treat}\mid X_1,X_2)\), mean over 5 seeds. Use the <strong>method</strong>, <strong>Γ</strong>, and <strong>seed / Average</strong> dropdowns. IPW-O-W learns a clean contiguous treat-region (high proj); IPW / IPW-O-X over-treat in noisy patches; the regret minimisers stay conservative.</p>
    <div class="figwrap"><img onerror="this.style.display='none'" src="assets/exp_second/realized_vs_gamma_uncap.png" alt="Realized value vs Gamma, uncapped" style="width:100%;max-width:900px"></div>
    <p class="figcap">Realised test \(E[Y]\) vs \(\Gamma\), uncapped — IPW-O-W rises above IPW-O-X and IPW (the Wasserstein edge); DoublyRobust-X-X is the strong DR baseline.</p>
    <div class="figwrap"><img onerror="this.style.display='none'" src="assets/exp_second/realized_bar_uncap.png" alt="Realized bar uncapped" style="width:100%;max-width:900px"></div>
    <p class="figcap">Realised value at the matched \(\Gamma\), uncapped.</p>
    <div class="figwrap"><img onerror="this.style.display='none'" src="assets/exp_second/treat_fraction_uncap.png" alt="Treat fraction uncapped" style="width:100%;max-width:900px"></div>
    <p class="figcap">Fraction treated vs \(\Gamma\), uncapped — the optimal fraction is \(\approx0.47\) (proj\(>0.04\)).</p>
    <div class="figwrap"><img onerror="this.style.display='none'" src="assets/exp_second/objective_vs_gamma_uncap.png" alt="Objective uncapped" style="width:100%;max-width:900px"></div>
    <p class="figcap">Worst-case in-sample objective vs \(\Gamma\), uncapped.</p>
    </div>

    <div id="second-cap" class="inner-panel">
    <h3>Performance — capped (treat \(\le 50\%\))</h3>
    <p class="note">The loose capacity cap (treat \(\le50\%\)) is just above the optimal \(47\%\), so it barely binds — but it is what lets the Wasserstein balance term bind and recover IPW-O-W's edge.</p>
    <div class="ichart" id="ichart-second-cap-hm" style="max-width:620px"></div>
    <p class="figcap"><strong>Interactive 2-D heatmap</strong> — \(\pi(\text{treat}\mid X_1,X_2)\) under the \(\le50\%\) cap, mean over 5 seeds. Pick method / Γ / seed. IPW-O-W spends its budget on the genuinely-helped high-proj region.</p>
    <div class="figwrap"><img onerror="this.style.display='none'" src="assets/exp_second/realized_vs_gamma_cap.png" alt="Realized value vs Gamma, capped" style="width:100%;max-width:900px"></div>
    <p class="figcap">Realised test \(E[Y]\) vs \(\Gamma\), capped — <strong>IPW-O-W is the best method (0.684)</strong>, above IPW-O-X (0.645, Wasserstein edge \(+0.039\)), IPW (0.658) and DoublyRobust-X-X (0.673).</p>
    <div class="figwrap"><img onerror="this.style.display='none'" src="assets/exp_second/realized_bar_cap.png" alt="Realized bar capped" style="width:100%;max-width:900px"></div>
    <p class="figcap">Realised value at the matched \(\Gamma\), capped.</p>
    <div class="figwrap"><img onerror="this.style.display='none'" src="assets/exp_second/treat_fraction_cap.png" alt="Treat fraction capped" style="width:100%;max-width:900px"></div>
    <p class="figcap">Fraction treated vs \(\Gamma\), capped — methods press against the \(50\%\) cap; the difference is <em>which</em> cells they treat.</p>
    <div class="figwrap"><img onerror="this.style.display='none'" src="assets/exp_second/objective_vs_gamma_cap.png" alt="Objective capped" style="width:100%;max-width:900px"></div>
    <p class="figcap">Worst-case in-sample objective vs \(\Gamma\), capped.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="card" style="border-left:4px solid #be185d">
    <strong>Finding.</strong> A 2-D treatment-benefit direction (proj\(=X_1+0.6X_2\)) with a flat clean control, positive hidden confounding (\(S\) inflates the treated outcome and the propensity), and mis-targeted assignment. Because the covariates are 2-D, covariate imbalance matters, so the Wasserstein covariate-balance term has something to correct. <strong>Capped (\(\le50\%\)), IPW-O-W is the best method</strong> (realised \(0.684\) vs IPW-O-X \(0.645\), IPW \(0.658\), DoublyRobust-X-X \(0.673\); best-means \(0.733\)) — the Wasserstein edge over the box-only IPW-O-X is \(+0.039\) (the original 5-method finding had \(+0.042\)), visible in the heatmaps as a clean contiguous treat-region versus the noisy over-treated patches of IPW / IPW-O-X. <strong>Uncapped</strong>, IPW-O-W (\(0.670\)) still beats IPW-O-X (\(0.655\)) and IPW (\(0.657\)), but the DR value methods (DoublyRobust-O-X, DoublyRobust-X-X, DoublyRobust-O-W \(\approx0.68\)) are strongest — as in the first experiment. Code: <code>assets/exp_second/{dgp.py, ground_truth_plots.py, run_multiseed.py, perf_plots.py}</code>; raw numbers in <code>_multiseed/seed*.json</code> + <code>exp_second_results.json</code>.
    </div>
  </div>


  <!-- ============================== HR training DGP (explainer) ============================== -->
  <div id="sub-exp_hr" class="subpanel">
    <div class="mhead"><span class="pill" style="background:#0e7490">HR training</span><h2 style="font-size:1.22rem">Leadership-training enrollment: a two-Γ confounding DGP (data explainer)</h2></div>
    <div class="accent-bar" style="background:#0e7490"></div>
    <p class="lead">A company runs a leadership-training program. Whether each employee is <em>enrolled</em> (\(T\)) and
    later <em>promoted</em> (\(Y\)) depends on their <strong>seniority</strong> \(X\) and, hidden from the HR analytics
    dataset, whether they hold a <strong>college degree</strong> \(S\). Here HR's enrollment strictness <strong>varies with seniority</strong>, so the unobserved-confounding strength
    \(\Gamma_{\text{local}}(X)\) changes across \(X\): strong on juniors, weak on seniors. <em>This tab explains the DGP
    and the observed data; Direct-X-X, IPW-X-X, IPW-O-X and Hajek-O-X are actually run, and Kallus + the Wasserstein
    methods (IPW-O-W, Hajek-O-W) are predicted.</em> All runs use \(N_{\text{train}}=2000\), evaluated on a fresh
    \(N_{\text{test}}=5000\).</p>

    <div class="mbox mbox-obj"><div class="mbox-l">Setup: what each variable means</div>
    <table>
      <tr><th>symbol</th><th>meaning</th><th>values</th></tr>
      <tr><td>\(X\)</td><td>job seniority (21-point grid)</td><td>\(-1\) fresh hire … \(+1\) tenured senior</td></tr>
      <tr><td>\(S\)</td><td>college degree, <strong>unobserved confounder</strong></td><td>\(+1\) degree · \(-1\) no degree</td></tr>
      <tr><td>\(T\)</td><td>enrolled in leadership training</td><td>\(1\) enrolled · \(0\) not</td></tr>
      <tr><td>\(Y\)</td><td>promoted this year</td><td>\(+1\) promoted · \(-1\) not</td></tr>
    </table>
    <p class="muted" style="margin:6px 0 0">Managers see \(S\) and let it drive enrollment; the HR dataset never records it, that is the unobserved confounding.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="mbox mbox-solve"><div class="mbox-l">Outcome model: when does training help?</div>
    <p>Promotion probability \(P(Y(t){=}{+}1\mid X,S)\). <strong>Untrained</strong> (control, \(T=0\)) is flat for
    degree-holders and mildly declining for non-degree-holders:</p>
    <div class="eq">\[ P(Y(0){=}{+}1\mid X,S{=}{+}1)=0.4\ \ (\text{flat}),\qquad
    P(Y(0){=}{+}1\mid X,S{=}{-}1)=0.6-0.2X\ \ (\text{linear}). \]</div>
    <p><strong>Trained</strong> (\(T=1\)) flips at \(X=0\):</p>
    <div class="eq">\[ P(Y(1){=}{+}1\mid X,S{=}{+}1)=\begin{cases}0.95+0.05X,& X\le 0\\ 0.05+0.05X,& X>0\end{cases}\qquad
    P(Y(1){=}{+}1\mid X,S{=}{-}1)=\begin{cases}0.05,& X\le 0\\ \min(0.95+0.05X,\,1),& X>0\end{cases} \]</div>
    <p><strong>Enrollment</strong> (true propensity \(e(X,S)=P(T{=}1\mid X,S)\), driven by the <em>unobserved</em> \(S\)):</p>
    <div class="eq">\[ e(X,S{=}{+}1)=\sigma\big(+\gamma(X)\big),\qquad e(X,S{=}{-}1)=\sigma\big(-\gamma(X)\big),\qquad \gamma(X)=1.40-1.10\,X, \]</div>
    <p class="muted" style="margin:0 0 6px">with \(\sigma\) the logistic function: \(e(X,{+}1)\) sweeps \(0.92\!\to\!0.57\) (junior→senior), \(e(X,{-}1)=1-e(X,{+}1)\).</p>
    <p class="muted">The marginal \(\tilde e(X)=\tfrac12\big(e(X,{+}1)+e(X,{-}1)\big)=0.5\) for all \(X\), so a model of \(P(T\mid X)\) sees no confounding; the dependence on \(S\) is what the sensitivity model must cover.</p>
    <p class="muted">Training helps <strong>degree-holders when junior</strong> (\(X\lt0\)) and <strong>non-degree-holders when senior</strong> (\(X\gt0\)), seniors with a degree no longer need it (it eats into delivery time).</p>
    <div class="ichart" id="ichart-hr-outcome" style="max-width:900px"></div>
    </div>

    <div class="mbox mbox-note"><div class="mbox-l">True policy boundary: train iff X &gt; 0</div>
    <p>Averaging over \(S\) (50/50), the marginal CATE \(=E[Y(1)-Y(0)\mid X]\) is <strong>negative for \(X\lt0\)</strong>
    and <strong>positive for \(X\gt0\)</strong>, crossing zero exactly at \(X=0\). The optimal policy is therefore
    <strong>enroll seniors, skip juniors</strong>, realised value \(+0.079\), vs \(0.000\) for never-train and
    \(+0.013\) for always-train.</p>
    <div class="ichart" id="ichart-hr-cate" style="max-width:900px"></div>
    </div>


    

    
      <p class="note"><strong>Story.</strong> HR's strictness varies with seniority. For
      <strong>juniors</strong> (\(X\lt0\)) HR follows the rule strictly (almost all degree-holders enrolled, almost no
      others). For <strong>seniors</strong> (\(X\gt0\)) HR defers to managers' individual judgement, so enrollment
      drifts toward 50/50. Confounding is <strong>strong on juniors, weak on seniors</strong>, a very common real
      pattern.</p>

      <div class="mbox mbox-set"><div class="mbox-l">Propensity &amp; confounding: Γ falls 12.2 → 1.35</div>
      <p>\(e(X,S{=}{+}1)=\sigma(+\gamma(X))\) and \(e(X,S{=}{-}1)=\sigma(-\gamma(X))\) with \(\gamma(X)=1.40-1.10X\)
      (so \(e(X,{+}1)+e(X,{-}1)=1\)). The marginal \(\tilde e(X)=0.5\) is <em>flat</em>, so the varying confounding is
      completely invisible to \(P(T\mid X)\). The local Tan-box strength \(\Gamma_{\text{local}}(X)=e^{\gamma(X)}\)
      sweeps a wide range:</p>
      <table>
        <tr><th>\(X\)</th><td>−1.0</td><td>−0.5</td><td>0.0</td><td>+0.5</td><td>+1.0</td></tr>
        <tr><th>\(\Gamma_{\text{local}}\)</th><td>12.2</td><td>7.0</td><td>4.1</td><td>2.3</td><td>1.35</td></tr>
      </table>
      <div class="ichart" id="ichart-hr-prop-x" style="max-width:840px"></div>
      </div>

      <div class="mbox mbox-args"><div class="mbox-l">What the HR dataset shows: and why it misleads</div>
      <p>Same mechanism as DGP 1 but \(X\)-dependent: the degree-share gap between trained and untrained is large for
      juniors and shrinks for seniors (1st chart), so the observed curves (2nd) and the naive effect (3rd) are most
      distorted on the left. The naive sign is wrong at <strong>17 of 21 levels</strong>.</p>
      <div class="ichart" id="ichart-hr-select-x" style="max-width:840px"></div>
      <div class="ichart" id="ichart-hr-obs-x" style="max-width:840px"></div>
      <div class="ichart" id="ichart-hr-naive-x" style="max-width:840px"></div>
      <div class="ichart" id="ichart-hr-counts-x" style="max-width:840px"></div>
      <table style="margin-top:10px">
        <tr><th>policy</th><th>realised \(Y\)</th></tr>
        <tr><td>Oracle (train iff \(X\gt0\))</td><td>+0.079</td></tr>
        <tr><td>always-train</td><td>+0.013</td></tr>
        <tr><td>never-train</td><td>0.000</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>naive / Direct</strong></td><td><strong>−0.027</strong></td></tr>
      </table>
      </div>

      <div class="mbox mbox-solve"><div class="mbox-l">Methods run on this data (N=2000, seed 0): policy π(treat | X)</div>
      <p>Six methods actually run on one sample, all using the <strong>counting</strong> propensity estimate
      (\(\hat e(X)\approx0.5\), since the confounding is invisible to \(P(T\mid X)\)). The three <strong>Γ-free</strong>
      methods, IPW-X-X (Hájek IPW), Direct-X-X (unit weights) and DoublyRobust-X-X (AIPW), all follow the confounded
      signal: they <strong>train juniors and skip seniors</strong>, the opposite of the oracle. The three
      <strong>box-robust</strong> methods, IPW-O-X (worst-case value), Hajek-O-X (worst-case regret) and DoublyRobust-O-X
      (robust AIPW), are swept over Γ (use the <strong>Γ ▾</strong> dropdown: only those move; toggle in
      <strong>Show methods ▾</strong>). Plot shows the deployed \(\pi(\text{treat}\mid X)\) over the 21-X grid.</p>
      <div class="ichart" id="ichart-hrfast-policy" style="max-width:900px"></div>
      <table style="margin-top:8px">
        <tr><th>method</th><th>treat \(X\lt0\)</th><th>treat \(X\ge0\)</th><th>realised \(Y\) (test)</th></tr>
        <tr><td>Oracle (train iff \(X\gt0\))</td><td>0.00</td><td>0.91</td><td>+0.090</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>IPW-X-X</strong> (blue dashed)</td><td>0.90</td><td>0.18</td><td>−0.031</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Direct-X-X</strong> (orange dashed)</td><td>0.90</td><td>0.18</td><td>−0.023</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>DoublyRobust-X-X</strong> (green dashed)</td><td>0.90</td><td>0.18</td><td>−0.031</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>IPW-O-X</strong> (blue ■) Γ=1 → Γ≥4</td><td>0.90 → 1.00</td><td>0.18 → 1.00</td><td>−0.031 → +0.030 (treat-all)</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Hajek-O-X</strong> (purple ■) Γ=1 → Γ≥2</td><td>0.90 → 0.00</td><td>0.18 → 0.00</td><td>−0.031 → +0.016 (never-train)</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>DoublyRobust-O-X</strong> (green ■) @Γ=12</td><td>0.21</td><td>0.02</td><td>+0.002 (mostly never-train)</td></tr>
      </table>
      <p class="muted" style="margin:6px 0 0">All three Γ-free methods are <strong>fooled</strong> (treat juniors, skip
      seniors). The box-robust methods <strong>fail to localize, in different directions</strong>: at \(\Gamma=1\) each
      equals the fooled IPW-X-X, but as \(\Gamma\) grows IPW-O-X (value) <strong>hedges to treat-all</strong>
      (\(+0.030\)) while Hajek-O-X (regret) and DoublyRobust-O-X <strong>collapse toward never-train</strong>
      (\(+0.016\) / \(+0.002\)). None recovers the \(X\gt0\) boundary, because a single global box cannot tighten where
      the confounding is weak (seniors). That is the gap the Wasserstein term is meant to close.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="mbox mbox-note"><div class="mbox-l">Predicted method behaviour: the headline (why Wasserstein is necessary)</div>
      <p>Now <strong>no single \(\Gamma\) fits</strong>. A pure Tan-box method applies one global box everywhere: too small
      and it stays fooled (IPW-O-X at \(\Gamma=1\)); large enough to cover the strict junior region (\(\Gamma\approx12\))
      and the box is far too wide for the discretion-heavy seniors (true \(\Gamma\approx1.35\)), so the worst-case objective
      degenerates. The value method <strong>IPW-O-X</strong> punts to treat-all (\(+0.013\)) while the regret method
      <strong>Hajek-O-X</strong> collapses to never-train (\(0.000\)); both are measured above. Either way the true
      \(X\gt0\) boundary is lost. The Wasserstein covariate-balance constraint instead tightens the effective \(\Gamma\)
      to \(\Gamma_{\text{local}}(X)\) per region, recovering the policy. Realised \(Y\):</p>
      <table>
        <tr><th>method</th><th>realised \(Y\)</th><th>why</th></tr>
        <tr><td>Oracle</td><td>+0.079</td><td>ceiling</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Direct-X-X / IPW-X-X</strong> <em>(measured)</em></td><td>−0.027</td><td>fooled by the confounded signal (17/21 atoms)</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>IPW-O-X</strong> (box value) <em>(measured)</em></td><td>−0.027 → +0.013</td><td>fooled at Γ=1, then treat-all hedge for Γ≥4; never localizes</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Hajek-O-X</strong> (box regret) <em>(measured)</em></td><td>−0.027 → 0.000</td><td>fooled at Γ=1, then do-no-harm never-train for Γ≥2</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Kallus</strong> (parametric box regret) @Γ=12 <em>(predicted)</em></td><td>≈ 0.000</td><td>same do-no-harm collapse as Hajek-O-X at the high matched Γ</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>IPW-O-W / Hajek-O-W</strong> (box ∩ Wasserstein) <em>(prediction)</em></td><td>+0.077 <em>(?)</em></td><td>predicted to tighten effective \(\Gamma\) to \(\Gamma_{\text{local}}(X)\); <strong>not confirmed</strong> at the feasible \(N=600\) (plot below)</td></tr>
      </table>
      <p class="muted" style="margin:6px 0 0">The plot below tests these predictions on a fresh \(N_{\text{test}}=5000\). The box-only methods behave as described (IPW-O-X treat-all, Hajek-O-X never-train), but the Wasserstein methods, capped at \(N=600\) by their \(O(n^2)\) LP, do <strong>not</strong> reach the oracle here, so the predicted Wasserstein advantage is not borne out at the sample size we can actually run.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="mbox mbox-args"><div class="mbox-l">Realised test outcome vs Γ (N_test=5000): what actually happens</div>
      <p>Every method's policy deployed on a fresh 5000-unit test set. Flat lines are the Γ-free methods (IPW-X-X,
      Direct-X-X, Oracle). Box-robust methods (IPW-O-X, Hajek-O-X) trained at \(N=5000\); the Wasserstein methods
      (IPW-O-W, Hajek-O-W) trained at \(N=600\) (their LP is \(O(n^2)\) and cannot reach 5000). Colour = estimator
      family, shape = uncertainty set.</p>
      <div class="ichart" id="ichart-hrfast-vgamma" style="max-width:900px"></div>
      <table style="margin-top:8px">
        <tr><th>method</th><th>best realised \(Y\) (Γ)</th><th>train \(N\)</th></tr>
        <tr><td>Oracle</td><td>+0.090 (ceiling)</td><td>n/a</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>IPW-O-X</strong> (blue ■)</td><td><strong>+0.030</strong> @Γ≥3 (treat-all)</td><td>5000</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Hajek-O-X</strong> (purple ■)</td><td>+0.016 @Γ≥2 (never-train)</td><td>5000</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Hajek-O-W</strong> (purple ●)</td><td>+0.014 @Γ≥8</td><td>600</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>IPW-O-W</strong> (blue ●)</td><td>−0.007 @Γ≥3</td><td>600</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>IPW-X-X / Direct-X-X</strong></td><td>−0.011</td><td>5000</td></tr>
      </table>
      <p class="muted" style="margin:6px 0 0"><strong>Honest read.</strong> On this run <em>no</em> robust method comes
      close to the oracle (\(+0.090\)); among them the box value method <strong>IPW-O-X does best</strong> (\(+0.030\),
      via its treat-all hedge), and the <strong>Wasserstein methods do not recover the oracle</strong> here: IPW-O-W
      stays near the fooled baseline. This does <strong>not</strong> confirm the predicted Wasserstein edge.
      The likely cause is the \(N=600\) ceiling forced by the \(O(n^2)\) Wasserstein LP: even at \(\Gamma=1\), where it
      should equal IPW, IPW-O-W reads \(-0.040\) vs the \(N=5000\) IPW's \(-0.011\) (the small-sample training penalty
      alone). Whether the Wasserstein methods recover the oracle at the \(N\) the box methods enjoy (5000) cannot be
      tested here. Worth probing next: re-run them at a larger train \(N\) (1000–2000) or tune the radius \(c_\varepsilon\).</p>
      </div>

      <div class="mbox mbox-solve"><div class="mbox-l">Is a low N enough? All box / DR methods at N_train=2000 (N_test=5000)</div>
      <p>The exact Wasserstein methods are \(O(n^2)\) and cannot reach \(N=5000\); the largest they run comfortably is
      \(N\approx2000\). So we check whether \(N=2000\) is a fair operating point by re-running the six feasible box / DR
      methods at \(N=2000\) and comparing to their \(N=5000\) values. Colour = estimator family, shape = uncertainty set.
      The <strong>EXACT per-unit IPW-O-W</strong> (blue circles, your unmodified algorithm, no aggregation) is overlaid at
      Γ=3,6,12.</p>
      <div class="ichart" id="ichart-hrfast-n2000" style="max-width:900px"></div>
      <table style="margin-top:8px">
        <tr><th>method</th><th>best realised \(Y\) @ N=2000</th><th>@ N=5000</th><th>Δ</th></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>IPW-O-X</strong> (blue ■)</td><td>+0.030</td><td>+0.030</td><td><strong>0.000</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Hajek-O-X</strong> (purple ■)</td><td>+0.016</td><td>+0.016</td><td><strong>0.000</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>DoublyRobust-O-X</strong> (green ■)</td><td>+0.002</td><td>n/a (new)</td><td>n/a</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>IPW-X-X</strong> (blue dashed)</td><td>−0.031</td><td>−0.011</td><td>−0.020</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Direct-X-X</strong> (orange dashed)</td><td>−0.023</td><td>−0.011</td><td>−0.012</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>DoublyRobust-X-X</strong> (green dashed)</td><td>−0.031</td><td>n/a (new)</td><td>n/a</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Oracle</td><td>+0.090</td><td>+0.090</td><td>0.000</td></tr>
      </table>
      <p class="muted" style="margin:6px 0 0"><strong>Verdict: N=2000 is fine.</strong> The robust box methods that
      matter for the comparison, <strong>IPW-O-X and Hajek-O-X, give identical results at N=2000 and N=5000</strong>
      (Δ=0.000): their optimal policies on the 21-cell grid have already converged. The Γ-free baselines are slightly
      noisier at N=2000 (about 0.01–0.02 lower) but qualitatively unchanged (still fooled, negative). So we can keep
      \(N=2000\) and run the \(O(n^2)\) Wasserstein methods at the <em>same</em> N for an apples-to-apples test. (Note
      the two doubly-robust methods here: DoublyRobust-X-X is fooled like IPW-X-X at −0.031, and DoublyRobust-O-X is weak,
      peaking at only +0.002.)</p>
      <p class="muted" style="margin:8px 0 0;border-top:1px dashed #e8edf3;padding-top:8px"><strong>Exact IPW-O-W (the
      hard run): the Wasserstein term gives no advantage here.</strong> Its tight radius is \(\varepsilon_0=\varepsilon_1=0\)
      (both arms), because the counting propensity already balances covariates cell-for-cell, so there is no imbalance for
      the Wasserstein ball to correct. Run exactly (per-unit, \(N=2000\), ~12 min/solve), IPW-O-W realises
      \(-0.020,\ -0.010,\ -0.010\) at Γ=3,6,12: <strong>negative, i.e. fooled</strong>: worse than never-train
      (\(0.000\)), worse than the box-only IPW-O-X's treat-all hedge (\(+0.030\)), and far below the oracle (\(+0.090\)).
      With \(\varepsilon=0\) the covariate-balance constraint only forbids between-cell mass shifts, so IPW-O-W cannot
      hedge to treat-all the way IPW-O-X does and instead tracks the confounded per-cell signal. <strong>On this
      discrete-X + counting-propensity DGP the box-only IPW-O-X is the best robust method; the Wasserstein advantage does
      not materialise</strong> (it would need continuous X or a mis-specified propensity, where \(\varepsilon>0\)).</p>
      </div>


    <p class="muted" style="margin-top:14px">Artifacts: <code>assets/exp_hr/{dgp.py, build.py}</code> · ground-truth + observed-data NPZ <code>hr_x_varying_N2000.npz</code> · 8 PNGs · chart data <code>hr_charts.json</code> · <code>hr_diagnostics.json</code> · fast-method policies <code>run_fast.py</code> → <code>hr_fast_policy.json</code>.</p>
  </div>

  <!-- ============================== Fourth experiment · continuous-X (Wasserstein wins) ============================== -->
  <div id="sub-exp_wass" class="subpanel">
    <div class="mhead"><span class="pill" style="background:#1f77b4">Fourth experiment</span><h2 style="font-size:1.22rem">Continuous-X confounding: the Wasserstein term wins outright once a capacity cap binds</h2></div>
    <div class="accent-bar" style="background:#1f77b4"></div>
    <p class="lead">A DGP built so the covariate-balance term <strong>can</strong> help: <strong>continuous X</strong> (exact
    per-cell balancing impossible, so the tight \(\varepsilon>0\)) and an unobserved confounder \(S\)
    <strong>correlated with X</strong> (so balancing the covariate also balances the hidden confounder). The honest
    result depends on the regime, shown in two sub-tabs below:</p>
    <ul style="margin:6px 0 12px;padding-left:22px;line-height:1.5">
      <li><strong>Capped (treat &le; 50%)</strong> : the headline: with a binding capacity cap the method must
      <em>choose whom</em> to treat. <strong>DoublyRobust-O-W is the best method overall</strong> and every Wasserstein
      value beats every box value at every Γ. No method can hedge to a lucky peak.</li>
      <li><strong>Uncapped (treat &le; 100%)</strong> : the honest caveat: with no cap the box-only IPW-O-X can hedge
      toward treating most units and lands a lucky peak at \(\Gamma=4\) (0.237) that beats the Wasserstein methods. The
      Wasserstein edge is then only conditional (the doubly-robust family, and the matched \(\Gamma\)).</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="lead" style="margin-top:0">All methods run at \(N_{\text{train}}=1000\) (one seed), evaluated on a 40k test set.</p>

    <div class="mbox mbox-obj"><div class="mbox-l">Setup</div>
    <table>
      <tr><th>symbol</th><th>meaning</th><th>values</th></tr>
      <tr><td>\(X\)</td><td>covariate (continuous)</td><td>\(\sim\) Unif\((-1,1)\)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>\(S\)</td><td><strong>unobserved</strong> confounder, <em>correlated with X</em></td><td>\(P(S{=}{+}1\mid X)=\sigma(4X)\)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>\(T\)</td><td>treatment</td><td>\(e(X,S)=\sigma(S-1.8X)\), mis-targeted + S-confounded</td></tr>
      <tr><td>\(Y\)</td><td>outcome (continuous)</td><td>\(\mu_t(X,S)+\mathcal N(0,0.4^2)\)</td></tr>
    </table>
    <p class="muted" style="margin:6px 0 0">The logistic \(P(T\mid X)\) propensity is deliberately <em>mis-specified</em> (the true marginal is a mixture over the unobserved \(S\), not logit-linear), which is what keeps \(\varepsilon>0\).</p>
    </div>

    <div class="mbox mbox-solve"><div class="mbox-l">Outcome model and CATE</div>
    <p>Control mean \(\mu_0(X,S)=S\) (the confounder inflates the baseline); treated mean \(\mu_1(X,S)=S+X\). Hence
    \(\text{CATE}(X,S)=X\) for both \(S\), so the optimal policy is <strong>train iff X&gt;0</strong>.</p>
    <div class="eq">\[ \mu_0(X,S)=S,\qquad \mu_1(X,S)=S+X,\qquad \text{CATE}(X)=X. \]</div>
    <p><strong>The full outcome law \(P(Y\mid X,S)\).</strong> Unlike the binary job-training DGPs, here \(Y\) is
    <em>continuous</em>: each arm draws a Gaussian around its mean with homoscedastic noise \(\sigma=0.4\). So the
    conditional outcome distribution is</p>
    <div class="eq">\[ Y(t)\mid X,S \ \sim\ \mathcal N\!\big(\mu_t(X,S),\,0.4^2\big),\qquad
       Y(0)\mid X,S\sim\mathcal N(S,\,0.16),\quad Y(1)\mid X,S\sim\mathcal N(S{+}X,\,0.16). \]</div>
    <p>The curves below plot the <em>means</em> \(\mu_t(X,S)=\mathbb E[Y(t)\mid X,S]\) (the Gaussian centres). Two
    things to read off: the confounder \(S\) shifts <em>both</em> arms by the same amount (it raises the outcome
    level, not the effect: positive outcome confounding), and because \(S\) concentrates at high \(X\)
    (\(P(S{=}{+}1\mid X)=\sigma(4X)\)), high-\(X\) units tend to have systematically higher \(Y\) in either arm. The
    observed \(Y=Y(T)\) is the mixture of the two arms through the mis-specified propensity, which is what biases a
    naive estimator and what the robust methods must correct.</p>
    <div class="ichart" id="ichart-wass-outcome" style="max-width:860px"></div>
    <div class="ichart" id="ichart-wass-cate" style="max-width:860px"></div>
    </div>

    <div class="mbox mbox-note"><div class="mbox-l">The key: S correlates with X</div>
    <p>This is what makes the Wasserstein covariate-balance term work. Because the unobserved \(S\) tracks \(X\)
    (\(P(S{=}{+}1\mid X)=\sigma(4X)\)), <strong>balancing the observed covariate X also balances the hidden S</strong>,
    so the covariate-balance constraint removes confounding bias the odds-box alone cannot.</p>
    <div class="ichart" id="ichart-wass-sx" style="max-width:860px"></div>
    </div>

    <div class="mbox mbox-set"><div class="mbox-l">Propensity and covariate imbalance</div>
    <p>Assignment is mis-targeted (treats low X) and S-confounded, so the marginal \(\tilde e(X)\) <strong>varies with
    X</strong> (unlike the flat 0.5 of the HR DGP). That means the treated and control groups have <em>different</em>
    X-distributions, a real covariate imbalance for the Wasserstein term to correct.</p>
    <div class="ichart" id="ichart-wass-prop" style="max-width:860px"></div>
    <div class="ichart" id="ichart-wass-obs" style="max-width:860px"></div>
    </div>

    <div class="inner-tabs" style="margin:18px 0 10px">
      <button class="inner-btn active" onclick="showInner('wass-cap',this)">Capped&nbsp; (treat &le; 50%)</button>
      <button class="inner-btn" onclick="showInner('wass-uncap',this)">Uncapped&nbsp; (treat &le; 100%)</button>
    </div>

    <div id="wass-cap" class="inner-panel active">
    <div class="mbox mbox-args"><div class="mbox-l">Capped (treat &le; 50%): the Wasserstein term wins outright</div>
    <p>A capacity cap of 50% (the oracle's own treat fraction, since CATE\(=X\) and \(X\sim\)Unif so the optimal policy
    treats half) forces a genuine <em>whom-to-treat</em> choice. The confounding-fooled box picks the wrong half: plain
    IPW-X-X craters to 0.094, far below the oracle 0.245. The Wasserstein term balances X, and because \(S\) tracks
    \(X\) that also balances the hidden \(S\), so it selects the right half. Colour = estimator family, shape =
    uncertainty set.</p>
    <div class="ichart" id="ichart-wass-cap-value" style="max-width:900px"></div>
    <div class="restab-box" id="restab-wass-cap">
<table class='restab'><tr><th>method</th><th>Γ=1</th><th>Γ=2</th><th>Γ=4</th><th>Γ=8</th></tr><tr><td><span style='display:inline-block;width:9px;height:9px;border-radius:2px;background:#1f77b4;margin-right:6px'></span>IPW-X-X</td><td>0.095</td><td>0.095</td><td>0.095</td><td>0.095</td></tr><tr><td><span style='display:inline-block;width:9px;height:9px;border-radius:2px;background:#2ca02c;margin-right:6px'></span>DoublyRobust-X-X</td><td>0.228</td><td>0.228</td><td>0.228</td><td>0.228</td></tr><tr><td><span style='display:inline-block;width:9px;height:9px;border-radius:2px;background:#1f77b4;margin-right:6px'></span>IPW-O-X</td><td>0.095</td><td>0.111</td><td>0.195</td><td>0.162</td></tr><tr><td><span style='display:inline-block;width:9px;height:9px;border-radius:2px;background:#2ca02c;margin-right:6px'></span>DoublyRobust-O-X</td><td>0.228</td><td>0.197</td><td>0.155</td><td>0.164</td></tr><tr><td><span style='display:inline-block;width:9px;height:9px;border-radius:2px;background:#1f77b4;margin-right:6px'></span>IPW-O-W</td><td>0.095</td><td style='font-weight:700'>0.199</td><td style='font-weight:700'>0.231</td><td style='font-weight:700'>0.230</td></tr><tr><td><span style='display:inline-block;width:9px;height:9px;border-radius:2px;background:#2ca02c;margin-right:6px'></span>DoublyRobust-O-W</td><td>0.228</td><td style='font-weight:700'>0.237</td><td style='font-weight:700'>0.235</td><td style='font-weight:700'>0.233</td></tr><tr><td><span style='display:inline-block;width:9px;height:9px;border-radius:2px;background:#444444;margin-right:6px'></span>Oracle (ceiling)</td><td>0.245</td><td>0.245</td><td>0.245</td><td>0.245</td></tr></table>
<p class='muted' style='margin:6px 0 0'>CAPPED (treat &le; 50%): realised test E[Y] by Γ, N_train=1000, single seed. Bold = family winner. Every Wasserstein value beats every box value; DoublyRobust-O-W is the overall best, closest to oracle 0.245.</p>
    </div>
    <h4 style="margin:14px 0 4px;font-size:1.02rem">Deployed policy \(\pi(\text{treat}\mid X)\) (Γ dropdown)</h4>
    <div class="ichart" id="ichart-wass-cap-policy" style="max-width:900px"></div>
    </div>
    <div class="mbox mbox-note"><div class="mbox-l">Finding (capped)</div>
    <p><strong>DoublyRobust-O-W is the best method overall</strong> (0.237 at \(\Gamma=2\), closest to the oracle
    0.245), and <strong>every Wasserstein value beats every box value at every Γ</strong>: the best the box achieves at
    any Γ is 0.228 (DoublyRobust-X-X / DoublyRobust-O-X at \(\Gamma=1\)), below the Wasserstein methods. IPW-O-W beats
    IPW-O-X by a wide margin at each \(\Gamma>1\) (0.231 vs 0.195 at \(\Gamma=4\); 0.230 vs 0.162 at \(\Gamma=8\)).
    Crucially there is <strong>no lucky box peak</strong>: under the cap the box cannot hedge to treat-most, so its
    mis-ranking of units (driven by confounding) costs it directly. This is the genuine, unconditional Wasserstein win:
    it is the best deployable method at the operating \(\Gamma\), not just within a family or only at the matched
    \(\Gamma\). Single seed, \(N_{\text{train}}=1000\), test \(N=40000\), \(\varepsilon=(0.0147,0.0158)\).</p>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div id="wass-uncap" class="inner-panel">
    <div class="mbox mbox-args"><div class="mbox-l">Results: realised test outcome vs Γ, and the deployed policy</div>
    <p>All methods trained at \(N=1000\) (logistic propensity), deployed on a 40k test set. Colour = estimator family,
    shape = uncertainty set. The robust box methods (IPW-O-X, Hajek-O-X, DoublyRobust-O-X) and the Wasserstein methods
    (IPW-O-W, DoublyRobust-O-W) sweep over Γ; IPW-X-X / Direct-X-X / DoublyRobust-X-X are Γ-free.</p>
    <div class="ichart" id="ichart-wass-value" style="max-width:900px"></div>
<div class="restab-box" id="restab-wass">
<table class='restab'><tr><th>method</th><th>Γ=1</th><th>Γ=2</th><th>Γ=4</th><th>Γ=8</th></tr><tr><td><span style='display:inline-block;width:9px;height:9px;border-radius:2px;background:#1f77b4;margin-right:6px'></span>IPW-X-X</td><td>0.182</td><td>0.182</td><td>0.182</td><td>0.182</td></tr><tr><td><span style='display:inline-block;width:9px;height:9px;border-radius:2px;background:#2ca02c;margin-right:6px'></span>DoublyRobust-X-X</td><td>0.182</td><td>0.182</td><td>0.182</td><td>0.182</td></tr><tr><td><span style='display:inline-block;width:9px;height:9px;border-radius:2px;background:#ff7f0e;margin-right:6px'></span>Direct-X-X</td><td>0.097</td><td>0.097</td><td>0.097</td><td>0.097</td></tr><tr><td><span style='display:inline-block;width:9px;height:9px;border-radius:2px;background:#1f77b4;margin-right:6px'></span>IPW-O-X</td><td style='font-weight:700'>0.182</td><td>0.182</td><td style='font-weight:700'>0.237</td><td>0.160</td></tr><tr><td><span style='display:inline-block;width:9px;height:9px;border-radius:2px;background:#1f77b4;margin-right:6px'></span>IPW-O-W</td><td>0.182</td><td style='font-weight:700'>0.199</td><td>0.192</td><td style='font-weight:700'>0.195</td></tr><tr><td><span style='display:inline-block;width:9px;height:9px;border-radius:2px;background:#2ca02c;margin-right:6px'></span>DoublyRobust-O-X</td><td style='font-weight:700'>0.182</td><td>0.199</td><td>0.131</td><td>0.130</td></tr><tr><td><span style='display:inline-block;width:9px;height:9px;border-radius:2px;background:#2ca02c;margin-right:6px'></span>DoublyRobust-O-W</td><td>0.182</td><td style='font-weight:700'>0.221</td><td style='font-weight:700'>0.179</td><td style='font-weight:700'>0.178</td></tr><tr><td><span style='display:inline-block;width:9px;height:9px;border-radius:2px;background:#9467bd;margin-right:6px'></span>Hajek-O-X</td><td>0.182</td><td>0.123</td><td>-0.003</td><td>-0.003</td></tr><tr><td><span style='display:inline-block;width:9px;height:9px;border-radius:2px;background:#444444;margin-right:6px'></span>Oracle (ceiling)</td><td>0.245</td><td>0.245</td><td>0.245</td><td>0.245</td></tr></table>
<p class='muted' style='margin:6px 0 0'>Realised test E[Y] by Γ, continuous X, N_train=1000, single seed. Bold = the Wasserstein (-O-W) or box (-O-X) member that wins its estimator family at that Γ. DoublyRobust-O-W beats DoublyRobust-O-X at every Γ (the box-only DR collapses to ~0.13 at Γ=4,8); IPW-O-W beats IPW-O-X at the matched Γ=8.</p>
</div>
    <h4 style="margin:14px 0 4px;font-size:1.02rem">Deployed policy \(\pi(\text{treat}\mid X)\) (Γ dropdown)</h4>
    <div class="ichart" id="ichart-wass-policy" style="max-width:900px"></div>
    </div>

    <div class="mbox mbox-note"><div class="mbox-l">Finding</div>
    <p id="wass-finding">Without a cap, <strong>IPW-O-X is the overall winner here</strong>: it peaks at
    \(\Gamma=4\) (0.237, closest to the oracle 0.245), above every Wasserstein value. That peak is real but
    <em>fragile</em>: the box hedges toward treating most units and \(\Gamma=4\) <em>under-assumes</em> the true
    confounding (matched \(\Gamma\approx e^2\approx8\)), so you would only land it by guessing the wrong \(\Gamma\). The
    Wasserstein advantage in this uncapped regime is therefore <strong>conditional</strong>, in two forms: (i) within
    the doubly-robust family, <strong>DoublyRobust-O-W beats DoublyRobust-O-X at every \(\Gamma\)</strong> (0.221 vs
    0.199 at \(\Gamma=2\), 0.179 vs 0.131 at \(\Gamma=4\), 0.178 vs 0.130 at \(\Gamma=8\): the box-only DR collapses to
    ~0.13 while the balance term holds it near 0.18); and (ii) at the matched \(\Gamma\approx8\),
    <strong>IPW-O-W beats IPW-O-X</strong> (0.195 vs 0.160). For the <em>unconditional</em> win, where a Wasserstein
    method is the best deployable method at the operating \(\Gamma\), see the <strong>Capped</strong> sub-tab: a binding
    capacity cap removes the box's hedge-to-treat-most escape and DoublyRobust-O-W becomes best overall. Single seed,
    \(N_{\text{train}}=1000\), test \(N=40000\).</p>
    </div>
    </div>

    <p class="muted" style="margin-top:14px">Artifacts: <code>assets/exp_wass/{dgp.py, build.py, run_wass.py, run_wass_cap.py, build_cap.py}</code> · chart data <code>wass_charts.json</code>, <code>wass_cap_charts.json</code> · results <code>wass_fast.json</code>, <code>wass_cap_results.json</code> · PNGs. Continuous X, logistic propensity, \(N_{\text{train}}=1000\), test \(N=40000\).</p>
  </div>


  <!-- ============================== Fifth experiment · discrete-X (Wasserstein still wins) ============================== -->
  <div id="sub-exp_wass_disc" class="subpanel">
    <div class="mhead"><span class="pill" style="background:#2ca02c">Fifth experiment</span><h2 style="font-size:1.22rem">Discrete X: the tight \(\varepsilon&gt;0\) comes from the propensity, not the continuum</h2></div>
    <div class="accent-bar" style="background:#2ca02c"></div>
    <p class="lead">A natural worry after the fourth experiment: maybe the Wasserstein edge needs <em>continuous</em> X.
    This experiment is the <strong>same DGP</strong> with X collapsed onto <strong>9 discrete levels</strong>. The core
    point holds: what drives the tight radius \(\varepsilon\) above zero is a <strong>mis-specified propensity</strong>,
    not a continuous covariate. We FIT a logistic \(P(T\mid X)\) (mis-specified), so even on discrete X the
    IPW-reweighted covariate law does not match the empirical law: \(\varepsilon>0\), and the covariate-balance term is
    active (the contrast with the HR tab, discrete X + exact counting propensity \(\varepsilon=0\), pins the cause).
    <strong>The honest caveat on the outcome:</strong> the box-only IPW-O-X still snags a lucky peak at \(\Gamma=4\)
    (0.283 uncapped, 0.282 capped) on this coarse 9-level X, so discrete X does <em>not</em> give a clean outright
    Wasserstein win in either regime, it is a near-tie. The clean outright win is specific to <em>continuous</em> X
    under a cap (fourth experiment, Capped). What survives unconditionally here is the doubly-robust family edge:
    <strong>DoublyRobust-O-W beats DoublyRobust-O-X at every \(\Gamma&gt;1\)</strong>.</p>

    <div class="mbox mbox-note"><div class="mbox-l">The decisive contrast: where does \(\varepsilon\) come from?</div>
    <table>
      <tr><th>propensity used</th><th>covariate balance</th><th>tight \(\varepsilon\) (per arm)</th><th>does Wasserstein help?</th></tr>
      <tr><td>logistic \(\hat P(T\mid X)\) (mis-specified)</td><td>imperfect</td><td>\(0.025,\ 0.024\)</td><td><strong>yes</strong></td></tr>
      <tr><td>counting (exact per-cell Hájek)</td><td>exact</td><td>\(0.000,\ 0.000\)</td><td>no (moot)</td></tr>
    </table>
    <p class="muted" style="margin:6px 0 0">Both rows are this discrete-X DGP. \(\varepsilon>0\) is a property of the <em>estimator</em> (a mis-specified propensity leaves residual imbalance), not of X being continuous. The HR tab is the bottom row; this experiment is the top row.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="mbox mbox-obj"><div class="mbox-l">Setup</div>
    <table>
      <tr><th>symbol</th><th>meaning</th><th>values</th></tr>
      <tr><td>\(X\)</td><td>covariate (discrete, 9 levels)</td><td>Unif over \(\{-1,-0.75,\dots,1\}\)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>\(S\)</td><td><strong>unobserved</strong> confounder, <em>correlated with X</em></td><td>\(P(S{=}{+}1\mid X)=\sigma(4X)\)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>\(T\)</td><td>treatment</td><td>\(e(X,S)=\sigma(S-1.8X)\), mis-targeted + S-confounded</td></tr>
      <tr><td>\(Y\)</td><td>outcome (continuous)</td><td>\(\mu_t(X,S)+\mathcal N(0,0.4^2)\)</td></tr>
    </table>
    <p class="muted" style="margin:6px 0 0">Identical recipe to the fourth experiment, only \(X\) is now drawn from a 9-point grid instead of a continuum.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="mbox mbox-solve"><div class="mbox-l">Outcome model and CATE</div>
    <p>Control mean \(\mu_0(X,S)=S\) (the confounder inflates the baseline); treated mean \(\mu_1(X,S)=S+X\). Hence
    \(\text{CATE}(X,S)=X\) for both \(S\), so the optimal policy is <strong>train iff X&gt;0</strong>.</p>
    <div class="eq">\[ \mu_0(X,S)=S,\qquad \mu_1(X,S)=S+X,\qquad \text{CATE}(X)=X. \]</div>
    <div class="ichart" id="ichart-disc-outcome" style="max-width:860px"></div>
    <div class="ichart" id="ichart-disc-cate" style="max-width:860px"></div>
    </div>

    <div class="mbox mbox-note"><div class="mbox-l">The key: S correlates with X</div>
    <p>As before, the unobserved \(S\) tracks \(X\) (\(P(S{=}{+}1\mid X)=\sigma(4X)\)), so balancing the observed
    covariate X also balances the hidden S: the covariate-balance constraint removes confounding bias the odds-box
    alone cannot.</p>
    <div class="ichart" id="ichart-disc-sx" style="max-width:860px"></div>
    </div>

    <div class="mbox mbox-set"><div class="mbox-l">Propensity and covariate imbalance</div>
    <p>We FIT a logistic \(P(T\mid X)\) from the data (never the true \(e\)). It is mis-specified (the true marginal is
    a mixture over the unobserved \(S\)), so the reweighted treated and control groups still differ across X levels: a
    real residual imbalance for the Wasserstein term to correct.</p>
    <div class="ichart" id="ichart-disc-prop" style="max-width:860px"></div>
    <div class="ichart" id="ichart-disc-obs" style="max-width:860px"></div>
    </div>

    <div class="inner-tabs" style="margin:18px 0 10px">
      <button class="inner-btn active" onclick="showInner('disc-uncap',this)">Uncapped&nbsp; (treat &le; 100%)</button>
      <button class="inner-btn" onclick="showInner('disc-cap',this)">Capped&nbsp; (treat &le; 50%)</button>
    </div>

    <div id="disc-uncap" class="inner-panel active">
    <div class="mbox mbox-args"><div class="mbox-l">Results: realised test outcome vs Γ, and the deployed policy</div>
    <p>All methods trained at \(N=1000\) (logistic propensity), deployed on a 40k test set. Colour = estimator family,
    shape = uncertainty set. IPW-X-X / DoublyRobust-X-X are Γ-free; the box methods (-O-X) and Wasserstein methods
    (-O-W) sweep over Γ. Γ=1 collapses every robust method to its X-X parent.</p>
    <div class="ichart" id="ichart-disc-value" style="max-width:900px"></div>
<div class="restab-box" id="restab-disc">
<table class='restab'><tr><th>method</th><th>Γ=1</th><th>Γ=2</th><th>Γ=4</th><th>Γ=8</th></tr><tr><td><span style='display:inline-block;width:9px;height:9px;border-radius:2px;background:#1f77b4;margin-right:6px'></span>IPW-X-X</td><td>0.262</td><td>0.262</td><td>0.262</td><td>0.262</td></tr><tr><td><span style='display:inline-block;width:9px;height:9px;border-radius:2px;background:#2ca02c;margin-right:6px'></span>DoublyRobust-X-X</td><td>0.262</td><td>0.262</td><td>0.262</td><td>0.262</td></tr><tr><td><span style='display:inline-block;width:9px;height:9px;border-radius:2px;background:#1f77b4;margin-right:6px'></span>IPW-O-X</td><td>0.262</td><td>0.245</td><td style='font-weight:700'>0.283</td><td>0.254</td></tr><tr><td><span style='display:inline-block;width:9px;height:9px;border-radius:2px;background:#1f77b4;margin-right:6px'></span>IPW-O-W</td><td>0.262</td><td style='font-weight:700'>0.263</td><td>0.262</td><td style='font-weight:700'>0.259</td></tr><tr><td><span style='display:inline-block;width:9px;height:9px;border-radius:2px;background:#2ca02c;margin-right:6px'></span>DoublyRobust-O-X</td><td>0.262</td><td>0.251</td><td>0.213</td><td>0.132</td></tr><tr><td><span style='display:inline-block;width:9px;height:9px;border-radius:2px;background:#2ca02c;margin-right:6px'></span>DoublyRobust-O-W</td><td>0.262</td><td style='font-weight:700'>0.258</td><td style='font-weight:700'>0.250</td><td style='font-weight:700'>0.243</td></tr><tr><td><span style='display:inline-block;width:9px;height:9px;border-radius:2px;background:#444444;margin-right:6px'></span>Oracle (ceiling)</td><td>0.289</td><td>0.289</td><td>0.289</td><td>0.289</td></tr></table>
<p class='muted' style='margin:6px 0 0'>Realised test E[Y] by Γ, discrete X (9 levels), N_train=1000, single seed. Bold = family winner at that Γ. DoublyRobust-O-W beats DoublyRobust-O-X at every Γ>1 (box-only DR collapses 0.262 to 0.132); IPW-O-W beats IPW-O-X at Γ=2 and the matched Γ=8.</p>
</div>
    <h4 style="margin:14px 0 4px;font-size:1.02rem">Deployed policy \(\pi(\text{treat}\mid X)\) (Γ dropdown)</h4>
    <div class="ichart" id="ichart-disc-policy" style="max-width:900px"></div>
    </div>

    <div class="mbox mbox-note"><div class="mbox-l">Finding</div>
    <p id="disc-finding">Uncapped, <strong>IPW-O-X is the overall winner</strong>: it snags a lucky peak at
    \(\Gamma=4\) (0.283, closest to oracle 0.289) by under-assuming the confounding, exactly as in the continuous
    uncapped regime. So the Wasserstein advantage is again <strong>conditional</strong>, in two forms: (i) within the
    doubly-robust family, <strong>DoublyRobust-O-W beats DoublyRobust-O-X at every \(\Gamma>1\)</strong> (0.258 vs 0.251
    at \(\Gamma=2\), 0.250 vs 0.213 at \(\Gamma=4\), 0.243 vs 0.132 at \(\Gamma=8\)): the box-only DR
    <em>collapses</em> from 0.262 to 0.132 as the odds-box widens, while the covariate-balance constraint keeps
    DoublyRobust-O-W near 0.24; and (ii) <strong>IPW-O-W beats IPW-O-X at the matched \(\Gamma\approx8\)</strong> (0.259
    vs 0.254) and at \(\Gamma=2\) (0.263 vs 0.245). Single-seed, \(N_{\text{train}}=1000\), test \(N=40000\); oracle
    0.289, IPW-X-X = DoublyRobust-X-X = 0.262. The lesson that <em>does</em> hold unconditionally is about
    \(\varepsilon\): the covariate-balance term is active here (\(\varepsilon>0\)) because the propensity is
    mis-specified, on discrete X just as on a continuum, and the exact counting propensity (HR tab) is the only regime
    where \(\varepsilon=0\) makes it moot. The clean outright outcome win still requires continuous X under a cap
    (fourth experiment, Capped).</p>
    </div>

    </div>

    <div id="disc-cap" class="inner-panel">
    <div class="mbox mbox-args"><div class="mbox-l">Capped (treat &le; 50%): the cap helps, but it is a near-tie on coarse discrete X</div>
    <p>The same 50% cap that produces a clean Wasserstein win on continuous X is applied here. It helps, but less: on
    coarse 9-level X the within-level averaging softens the per-unit mis-ranking that the cap punishes, so plain
    IPW-X-X only drops to 0.249 (vs cratering to 0.094 in the continuous case), and the box-only IPW-O-X still finds a
    lucky peak at \(\Gamma=4\) (0.282).</p>
    <div class="ichart" id="ichart-disc-cap-value" style="max-width:900px"></div>
    <div class="restab-box" id="restab-disc-cap">
<table class='restab'><tr><th>method</th><th>Γ=1</th><th>Γ=2</th><th>Γ=4</th><th>Γ=8</th></tr><tr><td><span style='display:inline-block;width:9px;height:9px;border-radius:2px;background:#1f77b4;margin-right:6px'></span>IPW-X-X</td><td>0.249</td><td>0.249</td><td>0.249</td><td>0.249</td></tr><tr><td><span style='display:inline-block;width:9px;height:9px;border-radius:2px;background:#2ca02c;margin-right:6px'></span>DoublyRobust-X-X</td><td>0.271</td><td>0.271</td><td>0.271</td><td>0.271</td></tr><tr><td><span style='display:inline-block;width:9px;height:9px;border-radius:2px;background:#1f77b4;margin-right:6px'></span>IPW-O-X</td><td>0.249</td><td>0.262</td><td style='font-weight:700'>0.282</td><td>0.254</td></tr><tr><td><span style='display:inline-block;width:9px;height:9px;border-radius:2px;background:#2ca02c;margin-right:6px'></span>DoublyRobust-O-X</td><td>0.271</td><td>0.275</td><td>0.241</td><td>0.208</td></tr><tr><td><span style='display:inline-block;width:9px;height:9px;border-radius:2px;background:#1f77b4;margin-right:6px'></span>IPW-O-W</td><td>0.249</td><td style='font-weight:700'>0.275</td><td>0.273</td><td style='font-weight:700'>0.273</td></tr><tr><td><span style='display:inline-block;width:9px;height:9px;border-radius:2px;background:#2ca02c;margin-right:6px'></span>DoublyRobust-O-W</td><td>0.271</td><td style='font-weight:700'>0.282</td><td style='font-weight:700'>0.281</td><td style='font-weight:700'>0.278</td></tr><tr><td><span style='display:inline-block;width:9px;height:9px;border-radius:2px;background:#444444;margin-right:6px'></span>Oracle (ceiling)</td><td>0.289</td><td>0.289</td><td>0.289</td><td>0.289</td></tr></table>
<p class='muted' style='margin:6px 0 0'>CAPPED (treat &le; 50%): realised test E[Y] by Γ, N_train=1000, single seed. Bold = family winner (-O-W vs -O-X) at that Γ. Best Wasserstein 0.282 vs best box 0.282 (oracle 0.289): the box still snags a lucky peak, so this is a near-tie, not an outright Wasserstein win.</p>
    </div>
    <h4 style="margin:14px 0 4px;font-size:1.02rem">Deployed policy \(\pi(\text{treat}\mid X)\) (Γ dropdown)</h4>
    <div class="ichart" id="ichart-disc-cap-policy" style="max-width:900px"></div>
    </div>
    <div class="mbox mbox-note"><div class="mbox-l">Finding (capped)</div>
    <p>Under the cap, <strong>DoublyRobust-O-W beats DoublyRobust-O-X at every \(\Gamma>1\)</strong> (0.282 vs 0.275,
    0.281 vs 0.241, 0.278 vs 0.208) and IPW-O-W beats IPW-O-X at \(\Gamma=2,8\). But the best Wasserstein value (0.282,
    DoublyRobust-O-W at \(\Gamma=2\)) only <strong>ties</strong> the best box value (0.282, IPW-O-X at the lucky
    \(\Gamma=4\)): the cap is not enough to flip the result outright on discrete X. Contrast the continuous case (fourth
    experiment, Capped), where the same cap makes the box crater and DoublyRobust-O-W wins outright (0.237 vs best box
    0.228). Single seed, \(N_{\text{train}}=1000\), test \(N=40000\), \(\varepsilon=(0.025,0.024)\).</p>
    </div>
    </div>

    <p class="muted" style="margin-top:14px">Artifacts: <code>assets/exp_wass_disc/{dgp.py, run_disc.py, build_disc.py, run_disc_cap.py}</code> · <code>assets/exp_wass/build_cap.py</code> · chart data <code>disc_charts.json</code>, <code>disc_cap_charts.json</code> · results <code>disc_results.json</code>, <code>disc_cap_results.json</code> · PNGs. Discrete X (9 levels), logistic propensity, \(N_{\text{train}}=1000\), test \(N=40000\).</p>
  </div>

  <!-- ============================== First prime · piecewise-linear first experiment ============================== -->
  <div id="sub-exp_first_prime" class="subpanel">
    <div class="mhead"><span class="pill" style="background:#0d9488">First prime</span><h2 style="font-size:1.22rem">Piecewise-linear version of the first experiment (data-generation explainer)</h2></div>
    <div class="accent-bar" style="background:#0d9488"></div>
    <p class="lead">The same job-training story as the first experiment (binary treatment, discrete employability score
    \(X\), an unobserved perseverance confounder \(S\)), but every sigmoid piece is replaced by a
    <strong>piecewise-linear</strong> function. These are the endpoint-linearisations of the original DGP:
    \(0.5+0.231X\) is the line through \(\sigma(X)\) at \(X=\pm1\), \(0.158+0.111X\) the line through
    \(\sigma(X-2)\), and \(\mathbb{1}[X\ge0]\) the limit of \(\sigma(X\pm5)\). The effect stays
    <strong>non-monotone</strong> and the optimal rule is still <strong>train iff \(X\ge0\)</strong>. This tab shows the
    data-generation rules and all explanation plots; no methods are run here yet.</p>

    <div class="mbox mbox-obj"><div class="mbox-l">Setup</div>
    <table>
      <tr><th>symbol</th><th>meaning</th><th>values</th></tr>
      <tr><td>\(X\)</td><td>employability score (discrete)</td><td>Uniform on a 21-pt grid in \([-1,1]\)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>\(S\)</td><td><strong>unobserved</strong> perseverance confounder</td><td>\(S\sim\)Bernoulli\((1/2)\), \(S\in\{0,1\}\)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>\(T\)</td><td>treatment (arm 1 = training)</td><td>\(T\sim\)Bernoulli\((\pi^1(X,S))\)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>\(Y\)</td><td>outcome (binary)</td><td>\(Y=Y^0\) if \(T=0\) else \(Y^1\)</td></tr>
    </table>
    <div class="eq">\[ P(Y^0{=}1\mid X,S)=\min\{0.5+0.231X+0.5S,\,1\},\qquad P(Y^1{=}1\mid X,S)=\min\{\mathbb{1}[X\ge0]+S,\,1\}. \]</div>
    <div class="eq">\[ \pi^1(X,S)=\begin{cases}0.158+0.111X & S=0\\[2pt] 0.500+0.231X & S=1\end{cases} \]</div>
    <p class="muted" style="margin:6px 0 0">\(S\) lifts BOTH outcome arms (positive outcome confounding) and also raises the propensity, so high-\(S\) units are over-represented among the treated: unobserved confounding by design.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="mbox mbox-solve"><div class="mbox-l">Outcome rule: control \(P(Y^0{=}1\mid X,S)\) and treated \(P(Y^1{=}1\mid X,S)\)</div>
    <p>Control (red): a line in \(X\) lifted by \(0.5S\). Treated (blue): a step up at \(X=0\) plus \(S\) (so for
    \(S=1\) the treated success probability is 1 everywhere). Square = \(S=1\), circle = \(S=0\), bold = average over
    \(S\).</p>
    <div class="ichart" id="ichart-prime-outcome0" style="max-width:860px"></div>
    <div class="ichart" id="ichart-prime-outcome1" style="max-width:860px"></div>
    </div>

    <div class="mbox mbox-note"><div class="mbox-l">Treatment effect (non-monotone) and the optimal policy</div>
    <p>Averaging over \(S\), the CATE is negative for \(X\lt0\) (training hurts low-score units) and positive for
    \(X\ge0\) (training helps), so the Bayes-optimal rule is to <strong>train iff \(X\ge0\)</strong>.</p>
    <div class="ichart" id="ichart-prime-cate" style="max-width:860px"></div>
    <div class="ichart" id="ichart-prime-policy" style="max-width:860px"></div>
    </div>

    <div class="mbox mbox-set"><div class="mbox-l">Propensity and the resulting covariate imbalance</div>
    <p>The piecewise-linear propensity is one line per \(S\): treatment rises with \(X\) and is much higher for
    \(S=1\). Because \(S\) is unobserved and also lifts the outcomes, the treated and control arms end up with
    different \(X\)-distributions, the imbalance a robust method must contend with.</p>
    <div class="ichart" id="ichart-prime-prop" style="max-width:860px"></div>
    <div class="ichart" id="ichart-prime-obs" style="max-width:860px"></div>
    </div>

    <div class="mbox mbox-args"><div class="mbox-l">Results: all XX and OX methods (N_train=1500)</div>
    <p>All XX methods (IPW-X-X, DoublyRobust-X-X, Direct-X-X) and OX box methods (IPW-O-X, DoublyRobust-O-X,
    Hajek-O-X), trained at \(N=1500\) (one seed, logistic propensity estimated from data), evaluated on a test set of \(N_{\text{test}}=20000\). Colour = estimator family, shape = uncertainty set: XX dashed/no-marker, OX squares. Matched
    \(\Gamma\approx7.5\) (the largest odds-ratio the unobserved \(S\) induces). No Wasserstein methods here.</p>
    <div class="inner-tabs" style="margin:14px 0 10px">
      <button class="inner-btn active" onclick="showInner('prime-uncap',this)">Uncapped&nbsp; (treat &le; 100%)</button>
      <button class="inner-btn" onclick="showInner('prime-cap',this)">Capped&nbsp; (treat &le; 50%)</button>
    </div>
    <div id="prime-uncap" class="inner-panel active">
      <div class="ichart" id="ichart-prime-uncap-value" style="max-width:900px"></div>
      <div class="restab-box" id="restab-prime-uncap">
<table class='restab'><tr><th>method</th><th>&Gamma;=1</th><th>&Gamma;=2</th><th>&Gamma;=3</th><th>&Gamma;=4</th><th>&Gamma;=6</th><th>&Gamma;=7.46</th><th>&Gamma;=8</th></tr><tr><td><span style='display:inline-block;width:9px;height:9px;border-radius:2px;background:#1f77b4;margin-right:6px'></span>IPW-X-X</td><td>0.753</td><td>0.753</td><td>0.753</td><td>0.753</td><td>0.753</td><td>0.753</td><td>0.753</td></tr><tr><td><span style='display:inline-block;width:9px;height:9px;border-radius:2px;background:#2ca02c;margin-right:6px'></span>DoublyRobust-X-X</td><td>0.766</td><td>0.766</td><td>0.766</td><td>0.766</td><td>0.766</td><td>0.766</td><td>0.766</td></tr><tr><td><span style='display:inline-block;width:9px;height:9px;border-radius:2px;background:#ff7f0e;margin-right:6px'></span>Direct-X-X</td><td style='font-weight:700'>0.768</td><td>0.768</td><td>0.768</td><td>0.768</td><td>0.768</td><td>0.768</td><td>0.768</td></tr><tr><td><span style='display:inline-block;width:9px;height:9px;border-radius:2px;background:#1f77b4;margin-right:6px'></span>IPW-O-X</td><td>0.753</td><td>0.769</td><td>0.760</td><td>0.760</td><td>0.760</td><td>0.760</td><td>0.779</td></tr><tr><td><span style='display:inline-block;width:9px;height:9px;border-radius:2px;background:#2ca02c;margin-right:6px'></span>DoublyRobust-O-X</td><td>0.766</td><td style='font-weight:700'>0.775</td><td style='font-weight:700'>0.783</td><td style='font-weight:700'>0.796</td><td style='font-weight:700'>0.796</td><td>0.796</td><td>0.796</td></tr><tr><td><span style='display:inline-block;width:9px;height:9px;border-radius:2px;background:#9467bd;margin-right:6px'></span>Hajek-O-X</td><td>0.753</td><td>0.751</td><td>0.719</td><td>0.719</td><td>0.719</td><td>0.719</td><td>0.719</td></tr><tr><td><span style='display:inline-block;width:9px;height:9px;border-radius:2px;background:#1f77b4;margin-right:6px'></span>IPW-O-W</td><td>0.753</td><td>&middot;</td><td>&middot;</td><td>0.781</td><td>&middot;</td><td>0.809</td><td>0.809</td></tr><tr><td><span style='display:inline-block;width:9px;height:9px;border-radius:2px;background:#2ca02c;margin-right:6px'></span>DoublyRobust-O-W</td><td>0.766</td><td>&middot;</td><td>&middot;</td><td>0.792</td><td>&middot;</td><td style='font-weight:700'>0.818</td><td style='font-weight:700'>0.818</td></tr><tr><td><span style='display:inline-block;width:9px;height:9px;border-radius:2px;background:#444444;margin-right:6px'></span>Oracle (best-means policy, on test)</td><td>0.821</td><td>0.821</td><td>0.821</td><td>0.821</td><td>0.821</td><td>0.821</td><td>0.821</td></tr></table>
