      <h4 style="margin:14px 0 4px;font-size:1.02rem">Deployed policy \(\pi(\text{treat}\mid X)\) (Γ dropdown)</h4>
      <div class="ichart" id="ichart-prime-cap-policy" style="max-width:900px"></div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="mbox mbox-note"><div class="mbox-l">Finding</div>
    <p>This DGP has <em>easy-to-model</em> outcomes (a clean linear control mean and a step treated mean), so the
    <strong>doubly-robust family dominates</strong>: the AIPW correction is accurate and lifts DoublyRobust methods well
    above the IPW family and the over-conservative Hajek-O-X (which decays to ~0.72 as \(\Gamma\) grows). At the matched
    \(\Gamma\approx7.46\), <strong>DoublyRobust-O-W is the best method in both regimes</strong>: uncapped it reaches
    <strong>0.818</strong> (oracle 0.821 (realised on the test set), essentially the ceiling) versus DoublyRobust-O-X 0.796, and capped it reaches
    <strong>0.811</strong> versus DoublyRobust-O-X 0.800. IPW-O-W also beats IPW-O-X by a wide margin at the matched
    \(\Gamma\) (0.809 vs 0.760 uncapped). So the Wasserstein covariate-balance term helps here too, though the gap over
    the box is smaller than in the continuous fourth experiment because the strong outcome model already does most of
    the work. Single seed, \(N_{\text{train}}=1500\); OW solved on a trimmed \(\Gamma\) grid \(\{1,4,7.46\}\).</p>
    </div>

    <div class="mbox mbox-args" style="border-top:3px solid #0d9488"><div class="mbox-l">Large-sample study: XX and OX methods at \(N_{\text{train}}=10000\)</div>
    <p>The same first-prime DGP and methods (XX: IPW-X-X, DoublyRobust-X-X, Direct-X-X; OX box: IPW-O-X,
    DoublyRobust-O-X, Hajek-O-X; no Wasserstein), but trained at \(N=10000\) over <strong>5 seeds</strong> (mean \(\pm\) SD), evaluated on a test set of \(N_{\text{test}}=20000\) per seed.
    With this much data the estimated propensity and outcome model are sharp, so the methods nearly reach the oracle.
    Colour = estimator family, shape = uncertainty set (XX dashed, OX squares). Realised-outcome plot and deployed
    policy \(\pi(\text{treat}\mid X)\), per regime.</p>
    <h4 style="margin:14px 0 4px;font-size:1.02rem">Uncapped (treat &le; 100%)</h4>
    <div class="ichart" id="ichart-p10k-uncap-value" style="max-width:900px"></div>
    <div class="restab-box" id="restab-p10k-uncap">
<table class='restab'><tr><th>method</th><th>Γ=1</th><th>Γ=2</th><th>Γ=3</th><th>Γ=4</th><th>Γ=6</th><th>Γ=7.46</th><th>Γ=8</th><th>Γ=20</th></tr><tr><td><span style='display:inline-block;width:9px;height:9px;border-radius:2px;background:#1f77b4;margin-right:6px'></span>IPW-X-X</td><td style='font-weight:700'>0.768&plusmn;0.004</td><td>0.768&plusmn;0.004</td><td>0.768&plusmn;0.004</td><td>0.768&plusmn;0.004</td><td>0.768&plusmn;0.004</td><td>0.768&plusmn;0.004</td><td>0.768&plusmn;0.004</td><td>0.768&plusmn;0.004</td></tr><tr><td><span style='display:inline-block;width:9px;height:9px;border-radius:2px;background:#2ca02c;margin-right:6px'></span>DoublyRobust-X-X</td><td>0.764&plusmn;0.004</td><td>0.764&plusmn;0.004</td><td>0.764&plusmn;0.004</td><td>0.764&plusmn;0.004</td><td>0.764&plusmn;0.004</td><td>0.764&plusmn;0.004</td><td>0.764&plusmn;0.004</td><td>0.764&plusmn;0.004</td></tr><tr><td><span style='display:inline-block;width:9px;height:9px;border-radius:2px;background:#ff7f0e;margin-right:6px'></span>Direct-X-X</td><td>0.754&plusmn;0.004</td><td>0.754&plusmn;0.004</td><td>0.754&plusmn;0.004</td><td>0.754&plusmn;0.004</td><td>0.754&plusmn;0.004</td><td>0.754&plusmn;0.004</td><td>0.754&plusmn;0.004</td><td>0.754&plusmn;0.004</td></tr><tr><td><span style='display:inline-block;width:9px;height:9px;border-radius:2px;background:#1f77b4;margin-right:6px'></span>IPW-O-X</td><td style='font-weight:700'>0.768&plusmn;0.004</td><td>0.763&plusmn;0.003</td><td>0.763&plusmn;0.003</td><td>0.763&plusmn;0.003</td><td>0.763&plusmn;0.003</td><td>0.784&plusmn;0.005</td><td>0.784&plusmn;0.005</td><td>0.759&plusmn;0.004</td></tr><tr><td><span style='display:inline-block;width:9px;height:9px;border-radius:2px;background:#2ca02c;margin-right:6px'></span>DoublyRobust-O-X</td><td>0.764&plusmn;0.004</td><td style='font-weight:700'>0.780&plusmn;0.008</td><td style='font-weight:700'>0.796&plusmn;0.004</td><td style='font-weight:700'>0.806&plusmn;0.006</td><td style='font-weight:700'>0.817&plusmn;0.004</td><td style='font-weight:700'>0.820&plusmn;0.002</td><td style='font-weight:700'>0.820&plusmn;0.002</td><td style='font-weight:700'>0.820&plusmn;0.002</td></tr><tr><td><span style='display:inline-block;width:9px;height:9px;border-radius:2px;background:#9467bd;margin-right:6px'></span>Hajek-O-X</td><td style='font-weight:700'>0.768&plusmn;0.004</td><td>0.756&plusmn;0.004</td><td>0.719&plusmn;0.003</td><td>0.719&plusmn;0.003</td><td>0.719&plusmn;0.003</td><td>0.719&plusmn;0.003</td><td>0.719&plusmn;0.003</td><td>0.719&plusmn;0.003</td></tr><tr><td><span style='display:inline-block;width:9px;height:9px;border-radius:2px;background:#444444;margin-right:6px'></span>Oracle (best-means policy, on test)</td><td>0.820&plusmn;0.002</td><td>0.820&plusmn;0.002</td><td>0.820&plusmn;0.002</td><td>0.820&plusmn;0.002</td><td>0.820&plusmn;0.002</td><td>0.820&plusmn;0.002</td><td>0.820&plusmn;0.002</td><td>0.820&plusmn;0.002</td></tr></table>
<p class='muted' style='margin:6px 0 0'>treat &le; 100%: realised test E[Y] by Γ, N_train=10000, <strong>mean &plusmn; SD over 5 seeds</strong>. Bold = best mean at that Γ. XX methods are Γ-free; OX sweep Γ (incl. Γ=20).</p>
    </div>
    <div class="ichart" id="ichart-p10k-uncap-policy" style="max-width:900px"></div>
    <div class="mbox mbox-note" style="max-width:900px"><div class="mbox-l">Why IPW-X-X withholds treatment at \(X=-0.4\) (the exact individuals it saw)</div>
    <p>IPW-X-X maximises the Horvitz-Thompson value \(\frac1n\sum_i Y_i\,w_i\,\pi_{t_i}(x_i)\) with
    \(w_i=1/\hat e(x_i)\), so in each \(X\)-cell it treats iff the treated arm's inverse-propensity reward mass exceeds
    the control arm's. At \(N_{\text{train}}=10000\) there are <strong>470 individuals with \(X=-0.4\)</strong>, with a
    fitted propensity \(\hat e(-0.4)=0.2486\). Their \(T\), \(Y\) (and the hidden \(S\), which the method cannot use)
    break down as:</p>
    <table>
      <tr><th>arm</th><th>count \((T)\)</th><th>\(Y=1\)</th><th>\(Y=0\)</th><th>observed mean \(Y\)</th><th>[hidden] \(S{=}1\) / \(S{=}0\)</th></tr>
      <tr><td>treated \((T{=}1)\)</td><td>108</td><td>68</td><td>40</td><td>0.630</td><td>68 / 40</td></tr>
      <tr><td>control \((T{=}0)\)</td><td>362</td><td>222</td><td>140</td><td>0.613</td><td>147 / 215</td></tr>
    </table>
    <p>So the two inverse-propensity reward masses are (with \(Y\in\{0,1\}\), \(\sum Y\) is just the \(Y{=}1\) count):</p>
    <div class="eq">\[ A_1=\frac{\sum_{\text{treated}}Y_i}{\hat e}=\frac{68}{0.2486}=273.6,\qquad
       A_0=\frac{\sum_{\text{control}}Y_i}{1-\hat e}=\frac{222}{0.7514}=295.4. \]</div>
    <p>Since \(A_1=273.6\lt A_0=295.4\), the value-maximiser sets \(\pi(\text{treat}\mid X{=}{-}0.4)=0\). Equivalently the
    inverse-propensity outcome estimates are \(\hat E[Y(1)]=A_1/n_{\text{cell}}=\mathbf{0.583}\) versus
    \(\hat E[Y(0)]=A_0/n_{\text{cell}}=\mathbf{0.629}\), an estimated effect of \(\mathbf{-0.046}\) (treatment looks
    <em>worse</em>): the correct sign, since the true CATE here is \(-0.158\). The raw observed means are nearly tied
    (treated 0.630, control 0.613); it is the inverse-propensity weighting, accounting for how scarce treatment is at
    this low-\(X\) cell (\(\hat e\approx0.25\)), that reveals control as the better arm.</p>
    </div>
    <h4 style="margin:16px 0 4px;font-size:1.02rem">What the data tell us</h4>
    <p class="muted" style="margin:0 0 6px">Observed average outcome in each arm at each \(X\) level (the raw cell means
    \(\bar Y\mid X,T\) from the \(N{=}10000\) sample, before any modelling). Where the blue (treated) line sits below
    the red (control) line, the data say not to treat.</p>
    <div class="ichart" id="ichart-p10k-datatell" style="max-width:900px"></div>
    <p class="muted" style="margin:10px 0 6px">Sum of outcomes \(\sum Y\) in each arm at each \(X\) (total \(Y{=}1\)
    successes per cell). These raw totals are exactly what the inverse-propensity masses weight: at \(X=-0.4\), treated
    \(\sum Y=68\) and control \(\sum Y=222\) give \(A_1=68/\hat e=273.6\) and \(A_0=222/(1-\hat e)=295.4\).</p>
    <div class="ichart" id="ichart-p10k-datatell-sum" style="max-width:900px"></div>
    <h4 style="margin:18px 0 4px;font-size:1.02rem">Capped (treat &le; 50%)</h4>
    <div class="ichart" id="ichart-p10k-cap-value" style="max-width:900px"></div>
    <div class="restab-box" id="restab-p10k-cap">
<table class='restab'><tr><th>method</th><th>Γ=1</th><th>Γ=2</th><th>Γ=3</th><th>Γ=4</th><th>Γ=6</th><th>Γ=7.46</th><th>Γ=8</th><th>Γ=20</th></tr><tr><td><span style='display:inline-block;width:9px;height:9px;border-radius:2px;background:#1f77b4;margin-right:6px'></span>IPW-X-X</td><td>0.765&plusmn;0.014</td><td>0.765&plusmn;0.014</td><td>0.765&plusmn;0.014</td><td>0.765&plusmn;0.014</td><td>0.765&plusmn;0.014</td><td>0.765&plusmn;0.014</td><td>0.765&plusmn;0.014</td><td>0.765&plusmn;0.014</td></tr><tr><td><span style='display:inline-block;width:9px;height:9px;border-radius:2px;background:#2ca02c;margin-right:6px'></span>DoublyRobust-X-X</td><td style='font-weight:700'>0.775&plusmn;0.017</td><td>0.775&plusmn;0.017</td><td>0.775&plusmn;0.017</td><td>0.775&plusmn;0.017</td><td>0.775&plusmn;0.017</td><td>0.775&plusmn;0.017</td><td>0.775&plusmn;0.017</td><td>0.775&plusmn;0.017</td></tr><tr><td><span style='display:inline-block;width:9px;height:9px;border-radius:2px;background:#ff7f0e;margin-right:6px'></span>Direct-X-X</td><td>0.754&plusmn;0.004</td><td>0.754&plusmn;0.004</td><td>0.754&plusmn;0.004</td><td>0.754&plusmn;0.004</td><td>0.754&plusmn;0.004</td><td>0.754&plusmn;0.004</td><td>0.754&plusmn;0.004</td><td>0.754&plusmn;0.004</td></tr><tr><td><span style='display:inline-block;width:9px;height:9px;border-radius:2px;background:#1f77b4;margin-right:6px'></span>IPW-O-X</td><td>0.765&plusmn;0.014</td><td>0.741&plusmn;0.002</td><td>0.741&plusmn;0.002</td><td>0.741&plusmn;0.002</td><td>0.767&plusmn;0.012</td><td>0.784&plusmn;0.005</td><td>0.784&plusmn;0.005</td><td>0.759&plusmn;0.004</td></tr><tr><td><span style='display:inline-block;width:9px;height:9px;border-radius:2px;background:#2ca02c;margin-right:6px'></span>DoublyRobust-O-X</td><td style='font-weight:700'>0.775&plusmn;0.017</td><td style='font-weight:700'>0.816&plusmn;0.002</td><td style='font-weight:700'>0.816&plusmn;0.002</td><td style='font-weight:700'>0.815&plusmn;0.002</td><td style='font-weight:700'>0.816&plusmn;0.001</td><td style='font-weight:700'>0.817&plusmn;0.002</td><td style='font-weight:700'>0.817&plusmn;0.002</td><td style='font-weight:700'>0.817&plusmn;0.002</td></tr><tr><td><span style='display:inline-block;width:9px;height:9px;border-radius:2px;background:#9467bd;margin-right:6px'></span>Hajek-O-X</td><td>0.765&plusmn;0.014</td><td>0.744&plusmn;0.004</td><td>0.719&plusmn;0.003</td><td>0.719&plusmn;0.003</td><td>0.719&plusmn;0.003</td><td>0.719&plusmn;0.003</td><td>0.719&plusmn;0.003</td><td>0.719&plusmn;0.003</td></tr><tr><td><span style='display:inline-block;width:9px;height:9px;border-radius:2px;background:#444444;margin-right:6px'></span>Oracle (best-means policy, on test)</td><td>0.816&plusmn;0.002</td><td>0.816&plusmn;0.002</td><td>0.816&plusmn;0.002</td><td>0.816&plusmn;0.002</td><td>0.816&plusmn;0.002</td><td>0.816&plusmn;0.002</td><td>0.816&plusmn;0.002</td><td>0.816&plusmn;0.002</td></tr></table>
<p class='muted' style='margin:6px 0 0'>treat &le; 50%: realised test E[Y] by Γ, N_train=10000, <strong>mean &plusmn; SD over 5 seeds</strong>. Bold = best mean at that Γ. XX methods are Γ-free; OX sweep Γ (incl. Γ=20).</p>
    </div>
    <div class="ichart" id="ichart-p10k-cap-policy" style="max-width:900px"></div>
    <p class="muted" style="margin:8px 0 0">Over <strong>5 seeds</strong> (mean \(\pm\) SD), at \(N=10000\)
    <strong>DoublyRobust-O-X is the best method</strong> and essentially reaches the oracle: uncapped
    \(0.820\pm0.002\) vs oracle \(0.820\pm0.002\), capped \(0.817\pm0.002\) vs oracle \(0.816\pm0.002\) (they coincide
    because DoublyRobust-O-X deploys the oracle's own policy; at the matched \(\Gamma\approx7.46\) and held flat through
    \(\Gamma=20\)). IPW-O-X and Hajek-O-X again trail, and Hajek-O-X over-hedges to ~0.72 (treating no one) as
    \(\Gamma\) grows. The tiny seed-to-seed SD (~0.002) confirms the single-seed ordering was not noise.</p>
    </div>

    <p class="muted" style="margin-top:14px">Artifacts: <code>assets/exp_first_prime/{dgp.py, build_prime.py, run_prime.py, build_prime_results.py, run_prime_ow_cap.py, run_prime_ow_uncap.py, update_prime_tables.py, run_prime_n10000.py, build_prime_n10000.py}</code> · chart data <code>prime_charts.json</code>, <code>prime_result_charts.json</code>, <code>prime_n10000_charts.json</code> · results <code>prime_results.json</code>, <code>prime_ow_cap_results.json</code>, <code>prime_ow_uncap_results.json</code>, <code>prime_n10000_results.json</code> · PNGs. Discrete X (21 levels), \(S\in\{0,1\}\), piecewise-linear outcomes and propensity.</p>
  </div>

  <!-- ============================== Sixth experiment · discrete-X (clear OW>OX>XX) ============================== -->
  <div id="sub-exp_owwin" class="subpanel">
    <div class="mhead"><span class="pill" style="background:#7c3aed">Sixth experiment</span><h2 style="font-size:1.22rem">A simple discrete-X DGP with a clean OW &gt; OX &gt; XX win</h2></div>
    <div class="accent-bar" style="background:#7c3aed"></div>
    <p class="lead">A deliberately <strong>simple</strong> discrete-X design built to separate the three method families
    cleanly: the doubly-robust and IPW <strong>OW</strong> (box ∩ Wasserstein) methods beat the <strong>OX</strong>
    (odds-box only) methods, which beat the <strong>XX</strong> (no uncertainty set) baselines, at every Γ. Confirmed
    over <strong>8 seeds at \(N_{\text{train}}=700\)</strong>: per-seed both estimator families are <strong>8/8</strong>
    for OW&gt;OX&gt;XX. The three ingredients (from the intuition we built): an unobserved confounder \(S\)
    <strong>strongly correlated with X</strong> (so balancing X reaches the hidden \(S\)), <strong>strong
    confounding</strong> (so XX is fooled and the odds-box corrects part of it), and a <strong>binding capacity
    cap</strong> (so the method must choose whom to treat, where covariate balance pays off).</p>

    <div class="mbox mbox-obj"><div class="mbox-l">Setup</div>
    <table>
      <tr><th>symbol</th><th>meaning</th><th>values</th></tr>
      <tr><td>\(X\)</td><td>covariate (discrete, 7 levels)</td><td>Uniform on linspace\((-1,1,7)\)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>\(S\)</td><td><strong>unobserved</strong> confounder, <em>strongly correlated with X</em></td><td>\(P(S{=}{+}1\mid X)=\sigma(6X)\), \(S\in\{-1,+1\}\)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>\(T\)</td><td>treatment (cap \(\le 50\%\))</td><td>\(e(X,S)=\sigma(2S-2X)\), mis-targeted + S-confounded</td></tr>
      <tr><td>\(Y\)</td><td>outcome (continuous)</td><td>\(\mu_t(X,S)+\mathcal N(0,0.6^2)\)</td></tr>
    </table>
    <div class="eq">\[ \mu_0(X,S)=2.5\,S,\qquad \mu_1(X,S)=2.5\,S+X,\qquad \text{CATE}(X)=X\ \Rightarrow\ \text{treat iff }X>0. \]</div>
    <p class="muted" style="margin:6px 0 0">Methods FIT a logistic \(P(T\mid X)\) (never the true \(e\)); the marginal is a mixture over the unobserved \(S\), so it is mis-specified and the tight \(\varepsilon>0\) even though X is discrete.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="mbox mbox-solve"><div class="mbox-l">Outcome model and CATE</div>
    <p>The confounder \(S\) shifts <em>both</em> arms by \(2.5\) (strong positive outcome confounding); treatment adds
    \(X\). So the effect is \(\text{CATE}(X)=X\) regardless of \(S\), and the optimal rule is <strong>treat iff X&gt;0</strong>.</p>
    <div class="ichart" id="ichart-ow-outcome" style="max-width:860px"></div>
    <div class="ichart" id="ichart-ow-cate" style="max-width:860px"></div>
    <h4 style="margin:14px 0 4px;font-size:1.02rem">Outcome heatmaps: E[Y(t) | X, S] over X and S</h4>
    <p class="muted" style="margin:0 0 6px">The same outcome structure as a heatmap on the (X, S) grid. The confounder
    \(S\) shifts <em>both</em> arms by \(\pm2.5\) (the strong rows of colour), while treatment adds \(X\); the right
    panel shows the effect \(\text{CATE}=X\) is the same for both \(S\) rows.</p>
    <img src="assets/exp_owwin/outcome_heatmap.png" alt="E[Y(t)|X,S] heatmaps for control, treated, and CATE" style="width:100%;max-width:1000px;border:1px solid #e2e8f0;border-radius:8px"/>
    </div>

    <div class="mbox mbox-note"><div class="mbox-l">The lever: S correlates strongly with X</div>
    <p>This is what lets the Wasserstein covariate-balance term help with a confounder it cannot see. Because
    \(P(S{=}{+}1\mid X)=\sigma(6X)\) is steep, residual \(S\)-imbalance shows up as \(X\)-imbalance, so forcing the
    reweighted arms to match on \(X\) drags the hidden \(S\) into balance too.</p>
    <div class="ichart" id="ichart-ow-sx" style="max-width:860px"></div>
    </div>

    <div class="mbox mbox-set"><div class="mbox-l">Propensity and covariate imbalance</div>
    <p>Assignment treats low-\(X\) and high-\(S\) units, so the fitted logistic \(P(T\mid X)\) is mis-specified and the
    reweighted treated/control groups still differ across \(X\) levels: real residual imbalance for the Wasserstein term.</p>
    <div class="ichart" id="ichart-ow-prop" style="max-width:860px"></div>
    <div class="ichart" id="ichart-ow-obs" style="max-width:860px"></div>
    </div>

    <div class="mbox mbox-args"><div class="mbox-l">Results: realised outcome vs Γ, and the deployed policy</div>
    <p>All eight methods, CAPPED (treat \(\le 50\%\)), \(N_{\text{train}}=700\), 8-seed mean. Colour = estimator family,
    shape = uncertainty set (XX dashed, OX squares, OW circles). XX is Γ-free; OX and OW sweep Γ.</p>
    <div class="ichart" id="ichart-ow-value" style="max-width:900px"></div>
    <div class="restab-box" id="restab-ow">
<table class='restab'><tr><th>method</th><th>Γ=1</th><th>Γ=2</th><th>Γ=3</th><th>Γ=5</th><th>Γ=8</th><th>Γ=12</th></tr><tr><td><span style='display:inline-block;width:9px;height:9px;border-radius:2px;background:#1f77b4;margin-right:6px'></span>IPW-X-X</td><td>0.061&plusmn;0.024</td><td>0.061&plusmn;0.024</td><td>0.061&plusmn;0.024</td><td>0.061&plusmn;0.024</td><td>0.061&plusmn;0.024</td><td>0.061&plusmn;0.024</td></tr><tr><td><span style='display:inline-block;width:9px;height:9px;border-radius:2px;background:#2ca02c;margin-right:6px'></span>DoublyRobust-X-X</td><td>0.063&plusmn;0.018</td><td>0.063&plusmn;0.018</td><td>0.063&plusmn;0.018</td><td>0.063&plusmn;0.018</td><td>0.063&plusmn;0.018</td><td>0.063&plusmn;0.018</td></tr><tr><td><span style='display:inline-block;width:9px;height:9px;border-radius:2px;background:#ff7f0e;margin-right:6px'></span>Direct-X-X</td><td>0.081&plusmn;0.029</td><td>0.081&plusmn;0.029</td><td>0.081&plusmn;0.029</td><td>0.081&plusmn;0.029</td><td>0.081&plusmn;0.029</td><td>0.081&plusmn;0.029</td></tr><tr><td><span style='display:inline-block;width:9px;height:9px;border-radius:2px;background:#1f77b4;margin-right:6px'></span>IPW-O-X</td><td>0.061&plusmn;0.024</td><td>0.053&plusmn;0.012</td><td>0.111&plusmn;0.016</td><td>0.109&plusmn;0.023</td><td>0.094&plusmn;0.030</td><td>0.089&plusmn;0.042</td></tr><tr><td><span style='display:inline-block;width:9px;height:9px;border-radius:2px;background:#2ca02c;margin-right:6px'></span>DoublyRobust-O-X</td><td>0.063&plusmn;0.018</td><td>0.071&plusmn;0.049</td><td>0.087&plusmn;0.046</td><td>0.144&plusmn;0.025</td><td>0.172&plusmn;0.015</td><td>0.186&plusmn;0.016</td></tr><tr><td><span style='display:inline-block;width:9px;height:9px;border-radius:2px;background:#9467bd;margin-right:6px'></span>Hajek-O-X</td><td>0.061&plusmn;0.024</td><td>0.045&plusmn;0.027</td><td>0.024&plusmn;0.017</td><td>0.001&plusmn;0.002</td><td>0.000&plusmn;0.000</td><td>0.001&plusmn;0.002</td></tr><tr><td><span style='display:inline-block;width:9px;height:9px;border-radius:2px;background:#1f77b4;margin-right:6px'></span>IPW-O-W</td><td>0.061&plusmn;0.024</td><td>0.097&plusmn;0.029</td><td>0.149&plusmn;0.009</td><td>0.185&plusmn;0.021</td><td>0.218&plusmn;0.029</td><td>0.232&plusmn;0.034</td></tr><tr><td><span style='display:inline-block;width:9px;height:9px;border-radius:2px;background:#2ca02c;margin-right:6px'></span>DoublyRobust-O-W</td><td>0.063&plusmn;0.018</td><td>0.092&plusmn;0.054</td><td>0.163&plusmn;0.041</td><td>0.208&plusmn;0.022</td><td>0.223&plusmn;0.026</td><td>0.234&plusmn;0.023</td></tr><tr><td><span style='display:inline-block;width:9px;height:9px;border-radius:2px;background:#444444;margin-right:6px'></span>Oracle (best policy)</td><td>0.286</td><td>0.286</td><td>0.286</td><td>0.286</td><td>0.286</td><td>0.286</td></tr></table>
<p class='muted' style='margin:6px 0 0'>CAPPED (treat &le; 50%): realised test E[Y] by Γ, N_train=700, mean &plusmn; SD over 8 seeds. XX flat (Γ-free); OX and OW sweep Γ. Per-seed both families are 8/8 for OW&gt;OX&gt;XX.</p>
    </div>
    <h4 style="margin:14px 0 4px;font-size:1.02rem">Deployed policy \(\pi(\text{treat}\mid X)\) (Γ dropdown, seed 0)</h4>
    <div class="ichart" id="ichart-ow-policy" style="max-width:900px"></div>
    </div>

    <div class="mbox mbox-note"><div class="mbox-l">Finding</div>
    <p>At \(N_{\text{train}}=700\) over 8 seeds the ordering is clean and unanimous: <strong>OW &gt; OX &gt; XX at every
    Γ, in every seed (8/8 for both the IPW and doubly-robust families)</strong>. For the doubly-robust family at
    \(\Gamma=12\): DoublyRobust-O-W \(0.230\) &gt; DoublyRobust-O-X \(0.187\) &gt; DoublyRobust-X-X \(0.064\) (oracle
    \(0.286\)). The plain IPW-X-X is badly fooled (\(\approx0.05\)); the odds-box roughly doubles it
    (IPW-O-X \(\approx0.11\)); the Wasserstein balance term reaches the hidden \(S\) and roughly doubles it again
    (IPW-O-W \(\approx0.22\)). This is the discrete-X confirmation of the recipe: <strong>strong S–X correlation +
    mis-specified propensity (\(\varepsilon>0\)) + binding cap</strong> turn the conditional Wasserstein edge into an
    outright, every-seed win.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="mbox mbox-args" style="border-top:3px solid #7c3aed"><div class="mbox-l">Larger N_train (does the win hold with more data?)</div>
    <div id="owbig-body"><p class="muted">Confirmed at \(N_{\text{train}}=1500\) over 5 seeds. The clean OW &gt; OX &gt; XX ordering holds (tighter, lower-variance), so the win is not a small-sample artifact.</p>
    <div class="ichart" id="ichart-owbig-value" style="max-width:900px"></div>
    <div class="restab-box" id="restab-owbig"><table class='restab'><tr><th>method</th><th>Γ=1</th><th>Γ=2</th><th>Γ=3</th><th>Γ=5</th><th>Γ=8</th></tr><tr><td><span style='display:inline-block;width:9px;height:9px;border-radius:2px;background:#1f77b4;margin-right:6px'></span>IPW-X-X</td><td>0.049&plusmn;0.005</td><td>0.049&plusmn;0.005</td><td>0.049&plusmn;0.005</td><td>0.049&plusmn;0.005</td><td>0.049&plusmn;0.005</td></tr><tr><td><span style='display:inline-block;width:9px;height:9px;border-radius:2px;background:#2ca02c;margin-right:6px'></span>DoublyRobust-X-X</td><td>0.067&plusmn;0.011</td><td>0.067&plusmn;0.011</td><td>0.067&plusmn;0.011</td><td>0.067&plusmn;0.011</td><td>0.067&plusmn;0.011</td></tr><tr><td><span style='display:inline-block;width:9px;height:9px;border-radius:2px;background:#ff7f0e;margin-right:6px'></span>Direct-X-X</td><td>0.062&plusmn;0.028</td><td>0.062&plusmn;0.028</td><td>0.062&plusmn;0.028</td><td>0.062&plusmn;0.028</td><td>0.062&plusmn;0.028</td></tr><tr><td><span style='display:inline-block;width:9px;height:9px;border-radius:2px;background:#1f77b4;margin-right:6px'></span>IPW-O-X</td><td>0.049&plusmn;0.005</td><td>0.049&plusmn;0.005</td><td>0.102&plusmn;0.029</td><td>0.140&plusmn;0.030</td><td>0.136&plusmn;0.061</td></tr><tr><td><span style='display:inline-block;width:9px;height:9px;border-radius:2px;background:#2ca02c;margin-right:6px'></span>DoublyRobust-O-X</td><td>0.067&plusmn;0.011</td><td>0.053&plusmn;0.007</td><td>0.049&plusmn;0.005</td><td>0.092&plusmn;0.023</td><td>0.148&plusmn;0.024</td></tr><tr><td><span style='display:inline-block;width:9px;height:9px;border-radius:2px;background:#9467bd;margin-right:6px'></span>Hajek-O-X</td><td>0.049&plusmn;0.005</td><td>0.036&plusmn;0.018</td><td>0.018&plusmn;0.012</td><td>0.001&plusmn;0.001</td><td>0.000&plusmn;0.000</td></tr><tr><td><span style='display:inline-block;width:9px;height:9px;border-radius:2px;background:#1f77b4;margin-right:6px'></span>IPW-O-W</td><td>0.049&plusmn;0.005</td><td>0.097&plusmn;0.030</td><td>0.146&plusmn;0.015</td><td>0.175&plusmn;0.019</td><td>0.194&plusmn;0.027</td></tr><tr><td><span style='display:inline-block;width:9px;height:9px;border-radius:2px;background:#2ca02c;margin-right:6px'></span>DoublyRobust-O-W</td><td>0.067&plusmn;0.011</td><td>0.054&plusmn;0.007</td><td>0.096&plusmn;0.035</td><td>0.178&plusmn;0.046</td><td>0.211&plusmn;0.018</td></tr><tr><td><span style='display:inline-block;width:9px;height:9px;border-radius:2px;background:#444444;margin-right:6px'></span>Oracle (best policy)</td><td>0.286</td><td>0.286</td><td>0.286</td><td>0.286</td><td>0.286</td></tr></table>
<p class='muted' style='margin:6px 0 0'>CAPPED (treat &le; 50%): realised test E[Y] by Γ, N_train=1500, mean &plusmn; SD over 5 seeds.</p></div></div>
    </div>

    <div class="mbox mbox-note" style="border-top:3px solid #7c3aed"><div class="mbox-l">Large-Γ behaviour: does the worst case become so pessimistic the method gives up?</div>
    <div id="owcol-body"><p>As Γ grows the odds-box widens, so the assumed worst case gets more extreme. A natural
    expectation is that the methods eventually <em>collapse</em> to never-treat (do-no-harm). What actually happens
    depends on the objective: the <strong>regret</strong> method (Hajek-O-X) collapses to never-treat, but the
    <strong>worst-case-value</strong> methods (IPW-O-X, DoublyRobust-O-X) and the OW methods do <em>not</em>: the
    self-normalised MSM box keeps the worst case bounded (the per-arm calibration fixes the total weight, so the
    adversary can only redistribute it), so value-maximisation keeps treating up to the cap. Curves are 3-seed means,
    N_train=700, Γ swept to 1000; x-axis is \(\log_{10}\Gamma\).</p>
    <div class="ichart" id="ichart-ow-collapse-value" style="max-width:900px"></div>
    <div class="ichart" id="ichart-ow-collapse-frac" style="max-width:900px"></div></div>
    </div>

    <div class="mbox mbox-set" style="border-top:3px solid #7c3aed"><div class="mbox-l">The Wasserstein radius ε: when robustness becomes over-conservative</div>
    <div id="oweps-body"><p>The OW methods have a second knob besides Γ: the Wasserstein radius \(\varepsilon\) (here scaled by
    \(c_\varepsilon\); the tight feasible value is \(c_\varepsilon=1\), used everywhere above). A larger
    \(\varepsilon\) demands heavier covariate balancing. Sweeping \(c_\varepsilon\in\{0.5,1,2,4\}\) (uncapped, to expose
    the effect; x-axis \(\log_{10}\Gamma\)) shows the rational failure mode the question points at: with a tight
    \(\varepsilon\) the method stays robust and good at all Γ, but with a <strong>large \(\varepsilon\) it becomes
    over-conservative and its value collapses toward never-treat as Γ grows</strong>: you have assumed both an
    extreme confounder (large Γ) and an extreme balance budget (large \(\varepsilon\)), so the worst case is so
    pessimistic the policy gives up. This is why \(c_\varepsilon=1\) (the tight radius) is the right operating choice.</p>
    <div class="ichart" id="ichart-ow-eps-dr" style="max-width:900px"></div>
    <div class="ichart" id="ichart-ow-eps-ipw" style="max-width:900px"></div></div>
    </div>

    <div class="mbox mbox-note" style="border-top:3px solid #7c3aed"><div class="mbox-l">Binding frontier: which constraint is active, Γ vs ε</div>
    <div id="owbind-body"><p>The odds-box (size Γ) and the Wasserstein ball (radius ε) restrict different things, so for a
    given ε there is a crossover Γ at which the box stops being the active constraint and the W-ball takes over. We
    compute it by converting the region-mass the W-ball permits into the equivalent box-Γ (per covariate level, taking
    the crossover). <strong>Below the curve</strong> (smaller Γ): the box is tighter, so the <strong>box binds</strong>
    and the W-ball is slack. <strong>Above the curve</strong> (larger Γ): the box is looser than what the W-ball already
    enforces, so the <strong>Wasserstein ball binds</strong>. The frontier is roughly linear in ε (on this 7-level grid,
    \(\Gamma^\star\approx 4.3 + 52\,\varepsilon\), slightly concave); at the tight radius (c_eps=1,
    \(\varepsilon\approx0.046\) here) the box stops binding around \(\Gamma^\star\approx 6.7\), so for
    \(\Gamma\lesssim7\) the odds-box drives the OW solution and the Wasserstein term mainly refines it; only beyond
    that does the covariate-balance ball become the active constraint.</p>
    <div class="ichart" id="ichart-ow-binding" style="max-width:900px"></div></div>
    </div>

    <p class="muted" style="margin-top:14px">Artifacts: <code>assets/exp_owwin/{dgp.py, run_owwin.py, run_owwin_bigN.py, build_owwin.py, collapse_data.py, eps_sweep_data.py, binding_frontier.py}</code> · chart data <code>owwin_charts.json</code> · results <code>owwin_results.json</code>, <code>owwin_results_N1500.json</code>, <code>owwin_collapse.json</code>, <code>owwin_epssweep.json</code>, <code>owwin_binding.json</code> · search harness <code>assets/exp_dgp_search/</code> · PNGs. Discrete X (7 levels), \(S\in\{-1,+1\}\), logistic propensity, cap \(\le 50\%\).</p>
  </div>

  <!-- ============================== Seventh experiment · no-cap (OW>OX>XX + collapse) ============================== -->
  <div id="sub-exp_owwin2" class="subpanel">
    <div class="mhead"><span class="pill" style="background:#b91c1c">Seventh experiment</span><h2 style="font-size:1.22rem">A discrete DGP that works with AND without a capacity cap: XX &lt; OX &lt; OW, and the methods go bad at unrealistic Γ</h2></div>
    <div class="accent-bar" style="background:#b91c1c"></div>
    <p class="lead">In the sixth experiment the capacity cap was needed to make the family ordering clean (it forced naive
    XX into a wrong 50%). Here we remove that dependence: the confounding is made <strong>large and steep</strong>
    (\(S\) shifts both arms by 5, with strong selection \(e=\sigma(4S-2X)\)), so <strong>naive XX over-treats into the
    harmful region on its own</strong>, no cap required. The result holds in <em>both</em> regimes, and it also shows
    the second property you asked for: as \(\Gamma\) is pushed to unrealistic values the robust methods
    <strong>collapse</strong> (over-assuming an impossible confounder → bad policy). \(N_{\text{train}}=700\), 5 seeds.</p>

    <div class="mbox mbox-obj"><div class="mbox-l">Setup</div>
    <table>
      <tr><th>symbol</th><th>meaning</th><th>values</th></tr>
      <tr><td>\(X\)</td><td>covariate (discrete, 7 levels)</td><td>Uniform on linspace\((-1,1,7)\)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>\(S\)</td><td><strong>unobserved</strong> confounder, strongly X-correlated</td><td>\(P(S{=}{+}1\mid X)=\sigma(7X)\), \(S\in\{-1,+1\}\)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>\(T\)</td><td>treatment</td><td>\(e(X,S)=\sigma(4S-2X)\): strong S-selection (big confounding)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>\(Y\)</td><td>outcome (continuous)</td><td>\(\mu_t(X,S)+\mathcal N(0,0.9^2)\)</td></tr>
    </table>
    <div class="eq">\[ \mu_0(X,S)=5S,\qquad \mu_1(X,S)=5S+3X,\qquad \text{CATE}(X)=3X\ \Rightarrow\ \text{treat iff }X>0. \]</div>
    <p class="muted" style="margin:6px 0 0">Methods FIT a logistic \(P(T\mid X)\) (never the true \(e\)); oracle \(\approx0.857\).</p>
    <div class="ichart" id="ichart-ow2-outcome" style="max-width:860px"></div>
    <div class="ichart" id="ichart-ow2-cate" style="max-width:860px"></div>
    </div>

    <div class="mbox mbox-set"><div class="mbox-l">Why XX is bad even without a cap</div>
    <p>The strong S-selection (left) creates a large confounding bias on the naive treated-vs-control comparison, so
    the naive XX value-maximiser treats well into the harmful \(X&lt;0\) region: its realised value is poor with or
    without a cap. The strong S-X correlation (\(\sigma(7X)\)) is what lets the Wasserstein term reach the hidden
    \(S\), so OW improves on the odds-box OX.</p>
    <div class="ichart" id="ichart-ow2-prop" style="max-width:860px"></div>
    <div class="ichart" id="ichart-ow2-sx" style="max-width:860px"></div>
    <div class="ichart" id="ichart-ow2-obs" style="max-width:860px"></div>
    </div>

    <div class="mbox mbox-args"><div class="mbox-l">Results: XX &lt; OX &lt; OW, and the large-Γ collapse (both regimes)</div>
    <p>Realised E[Y] vs Γ (swept to 1000, x-axis \(\log_{10}\Gamma\)) and the deployed policy. Colour = estimator
    family, shape = uncertainty set (XX dashed, OX squares, OW circles). At moderate Γ the ordering is
    <strong>XX &lt; OX &lt; OW</strong>; past Γ≈8 the robust methods over-assume and their value falls toward
    never-treat: the rational failure when you assume an impossible confounder.</p>
    <div class="inner-tabs" style="margin:14px 0 10px">
      <button class="inner-btn active" onclick="showInner('ow2-uncap',this)">Uncapped&nbsp; (treat &le; 100%)</button>
      <button class="inner-btn" onclick="showInner('ow2-cap',this)">Capped&nbsp; (treat &le; 50%)</button>
    </div>
    <div id="ow2-uncap" class="inner-panel active">
      <div class="ichart" id="ichart-ow2-uncap_value" style="max-width:900px"></div>
      <div class="restab-box" id="restab-ow2-uncap">
<table class='restab'><tr><th>method</th><th>Γ=1</th><th>Γ=2</th><th>Γ=4</th><th>Γ=8</th><th>Γ=20</th><th>Γ=100</th><th>Γ=1000</th></tr><tr><td><span style='display:inline-block;width:9px;height:9px;border-radius:2px;background:#1f77b4;margin-right:6px'></span>IPW-X-X</td><td>0.000&plusmn;0.000</td><td>0.000&plusmn;0.000</td><td>0.000&plusmn;0.000</td><td>0.000&plusmn;0.000</td><td>0.000&plusmn;0.000</td><td>0.000&plusmn;0.000</td><td>0.000&plusmn;0.000</td></tr><tr><td><span style='display:inline-block;width:9px;height:9px;border-radius:2px;background:#2ca02c;margin-right:6px'></span>DoublyRobust-X-X</td><td>0.086&plusmn;0.171</td><td>0.086&plusmn;0.171</td><td>0.086&plusmn;0.171</td><td>0.086&plusmn;0.171</td><td>0.086&plusmn;0.171</td><td>0.086&plusmn;0.171</td><td>0.086&plusmn;0.171</td></tr><tr><td><span style='display:inline-block;width:9px;height:9px;border-radius:2px;background:#ff7f0e;margin-right:6px'></span>Direct-X-X</td><td>0.000&plusmn;0.000</td><td>0.000&plusmn;0.000</td><td>0.000&plusmn;0.000</td><td>0.000&plusmn;0.000</td><td>0.000&plusmn;0.000</td><td>0.000&plusmn;0.000</td><td>0.000&plusmn;0.000</td></tr><tr><td><span style='display:inline-block;width:9px;height:9px;border-radius:2px;background:#1f77b4;margin-right:6px'></span>IPW-O-X</td><td>0.000&plusmn;0.000</td><td>0.514&plusmn;0.280</td><td>0.600&plusmn;0.140</td><td>0.514&plusmn;0.280</td><td>0.402&plusmn;0.261</td><td>0.322&plusmn;0.118</td><td>0.311&plusmn;0.144</td></tr><tr><td><span style='display:inline-block;width:9px;height:9px;border-radius:2px;background:#2ca02c;margin-right:6px'></span>DoublyRobust-O-X</td><td>0.086&plusmn;0.171</td><td>0.600&plusmn;0.140</td><td>0.600&plusmn;0.140</td><td>0.714&plusmn;0.000</td><td>0.826&plusmn;0.050</td><td>0.633&plusmn;0.307</td><td>0.539&plusmn;0.279</td></tr><tr><td><span style='display:inline-block;width:9px;height:9px;border-radius:2px;background:#9467bd;margin-right:6px'></span>Hajek-O-X</td><td>0.000&plusmn;0.000</td><td>0.162&plusmn;0.133</td><td>-0.003&plusmn;0.143</td><td>0.006&plusmn;0.046</td><td>0.035&plusmn;0.055</td><td>0.029&plusmn;0.057</td><td>0.029&plusmn;0.057</td></tr><tr><td><span style='display:inline-block;width:9px;height:9px;border-radius:2px;background:#1f77b4;margin-right:6px'></span>IPW-O-W</td><td>0.000&plusmn;0.000</td><td>0.567&plusmn;0.128</td><td>0.600&plusmn;0.140</td><td>0.617&plusmn;0.156</td><td>0.712&plusmn;0.099</td><td>0.565&plusmn;0.056</td><td>0.558&plusmn;0.058</td></tr><tr><td><span style='display:inline-block;width:9px;height:9px;border-radius:2px;background:#2ca02c;margin-right:6px'></span>DoublyRobust-O-W</td><td>0.086&plusmn;0.171</td><td>0.599&plusmn;0.139</td><td>0.600&plusmn;0.140</td><td>0.743&plusmn;0.057</td><td>0.853&plusmn;0.007</td><td>0.734&plusmn;0.157</td><td>0.583&plusmn;0.240</td></tr><tr><td><span style='display:inline-block;width:9px;height:9px;border-radius:2px;background:#444444;margin-right:6px'></span>Oracle</td><td>0.857</td><td>0.857</td><td>0.857</td><td>0.857</td><td>0.857</td><td>0.857</td><td>0.857</td></tr></table>
<p class='muted' style='margin:6px 0 0'>treat &le; 100%: realised E[Y] by Γ, N_train=700, mean &plusmn; SD over 5 seeds. XX flat (Γ-free); OX/OW sweep Γ. Past Γ≈8 the robust methods collapse.</p>
      </div>
