      </div>
      <h4 style="margin:14px 0 4px;font-size:1.02rem">Deployed policy \(\pi(\text{treat}\mid X)\) (Γ dropdown, seed 0)</h4>
      <div class="ichart" id="ichart-ow2-cap_policy" style="max-width:900px"></div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="mbox mbox-note"><div class="mbox-l">Finding</div>
    <p>This DGP delivers both requested properties <strong>without relying on the cap</strong>. Uncapped (5 seeds):
    XX≈0.29 &lt; OX≈0.70 &lt; OW≈0.77 at the operating Γ; capped: XX≈0.20 &lt; OX≈0.66 &lt; OW≈0.66. In both regimes,
    pushing Γ to unrealistic values makes the robust methods <strong>collapse</strong> (e.g. uncapped DoublyRobust-O-X
    falls from ≈0.70 at Γ=8 to ≈0.21 at Γ=1000; IPW-O-X to ≈0.13) because the odds-box, with no cap to pin the
    treat-fraction, lets the worst case drive the policy toward never-treat. The Wasserstein methods (OW) are the most
    robust to this but eventually decline too. So: <strong>OW &gt; OX &gt; XX at the sensible Γ, and all robust methods
    correctly degrade once Γ is unrealistic</strong>, with or without a capacity constraint.</p>
    </div>

    <p class="muted" style="margin-top:14px">Artifacts: <code>assets/exp_owwin2/{dgp.py, run_owwin2.py, build_owwin2.py}</code> · chart data <code>owwin2_charts.json</code> · results <code>owwin2_results.json</code> · search/verify <code>assets/exp_dgp_search/search_uncap*.py, verify_E3.py</code> · PNGs. Discrete X (7 levels), \(S\in\{-1,+1\}\), logistic propensity, \(N_{\text{train}}=700\); both regimes.</p>
  </div>

  <!-- ============================== Eighth experiment · OW beats every method at small Γ ============================== -->
  <div id="sub-exp_owwin3" class="subpanel">
    <div class="mhead"><span class="pill" style="background:#0891b2">Eighth experiment</span><h2 style="font-size:1.22rem">Both Wasserstein methods beat every other method, at a small Γ, with and without a cap</h2></div>
    <div class="accent-bar" style="background:#0891b2"></div>
    <p class="lead">The strongest separation: here <strong>both IPW-O-W and DoublyRobust-O-W beat every other method</strong>
    (including plain DoublyRobust-X-X, DoublyRobust-O-X, Direct-X-X, IPW-O-X, IPW-X-X and Hajek-O-X), and they do it at
    a <strong>small \(\Gamma\) (≈1.5-2)</strong>, in <em>both</em> the capped and uncapped regimes. The design trick:
    the unobserved \(S\) boosts <strong>both</strong> arms strongly (so the outcome model is badly biased by selection
    on \(S\)), but with only a <strong>small treatment-by-\(S\) synergy</strong> (so the true effect is subtle and even
    plain doubly-robust is beatable). The strong \(S\)-\(X\) correlation lets the Wasserstein covariate-balance recover
    the right policy. \(N_{\text{train}}=700\), 5 seeds. Tight \(\varepsilon\) (the search found larger
    \(\varepsilon\) hurts here).</p>

    <div class="mbox mbox-obj"><div class="mbox-l">Setup</div>
    <table>
      <tr><th>symbol</th><th>meaning</th><th>values</th></tr>
      <tr><td>\(X\)</td><td>covariate (discrete, 7 levels)</td><td>Uniform on linspace\((-1,1,7)\)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>\(S\)</td><td><strong>unobserved</strong> confounder, strongly X-correlated</td><td>\(P(S{=}{+}1\mid X)=\sigma(10X)\), \(S\in\{-1,+1\}\)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>\(T\)</td><td>treatment</td><td>\(e(X,S)=\sigma(0.8S-2X)\): moderate S-selection (matched \(\Gamma\approx5\), small)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>\(Y\)</td><td>outcome (continuous)</td><td>\(\mu_t(X,S)+\mathcal N(0,0.6^2)\)</td></tr>
    </table>
    <div class="eq">\[ \mu_0(X,S)=5S,\qquad \mu_1(X,S)=6S+X,\qquad \text{CATE}(X,S)=S+X,\ \ \mathbb E[\text{CATE}\mid X]=(2\sigma(10X){-}1)+X. \]</div>
    <p class="muted" style="margin:6px 0 0">\(S\) shifts both arms by \(\approx\pm5\) (it dominates outcomes and badly biases the fitted outcome model), but helps the treated only slightly more (\(6\) vs \(5\)); the optimal rule is \(\approx\)treat iff \(X>0\) (oracle \(\approx0.70\)).</p>
    <div class="ichart" id="ichart-ow3-outcome" style="max-width:860px"></div>
    <div class="ichart" id="ichart-ow3-cate" style="max-width:860px"></div>
    </div>

    <div class="mbox mbox-set"><div class="mbox-l">Why the outcome-model methods lose and OW wins</div>
    <p>Because \(S\) (which tracks \(X\)) dominates the outcome and the treated are selected on \(S\), the fitted
    outcome model and the naive comparison are badly biased, so DoublyRobust-X-X / DoublyRobust-O-X / Direct over-rate
    treatment. The Wasserstein methods balance the \(X\)-distribution across arms, which (because \(S\) tracks \(X\))
    also balances the hidden \(S\), recovering the subtle true effect.</p>
    <div class="ichart" id="ichart-ow3-prop" style="max-width:860px"></div>
    <div class="ichart" id="ichart-ow3-sx" style="max-width:860px"></div>
    <div class="ichart" id="ichart-ow3-obs" style="max-width:860px"></div>
    </div>

    <div class="mbox mbox-args"><div class="mbox-l">Results: OW beats every method at small Γ (both regimes)</div>
    <p>Realised E[Y] vs Γ and the deployed policy. Colour = estimator family, shape = uncertainty set (XX dashed, OX
    squares, OW circles). Both <strong>IPW-O-W and DoublyRobust-O-W (the two circle curves) sit above all others</strong>
    around \(\Gamma\approx1.5\text{-}2\). Policy charts have Γ and seed dropdowns (default = 5-seed average, matching
    the value chart).</p>
    <div class="inner-tabs" style="margin:14px 0 10px">
      <button class="inner-btn active" onclick="showInner('ow3-uncap',this)">Uncapped&nbsp; (treat &le; 100%)</button>
      <button class="inner-btn" onclick="showInner('ow3-cap',this)">Capped&nbsp; (treat &le; 50%)</button>
    </div>
    <div id="ow3-uncap" class="inner-panel active">
      <div class="ichart" id="ichart-ow3-uncap_value" style="max-width:900px"></div>
      <div class="restab-box" id="restab-ow3-uncap">
